# CLAUDE.md — Superchat

## 人机协作约定

- 面向用户的总结默认使用“人话”：先用一句话说结论，再用具体例子或
  类比解释，最后说清已经证实什么、尚未证实什么、下一步是什么。说明应让
  初中生也能理解；必须使用术语时，先解释意思，再给出术语名称。
- 能从代码和测试查清的事情，不用“可疑”“可能”“似乎”等词代替调查。
  明确区分代码事实、基于事实的推论和仍待验证的假设。
- 不把“写了计划”“新增了文件”或“测试通过”自动说成产品目标已经实现。
  用户总结要说明实际行为发生了什么，以及证据覆盖到哪里。
- 技术记录可以保留函数名、事件名、哈希和失败输出；给用户的结论先翻译成
  普通语言，再附上必要的技术证据。

## 测试集所有权

- M3 回归测试集（`scripts/m3-regression.el`、`.scratch/m3-verification/regression-mutants/`、
  `.scratch/m3-verification/REGRESSION-SET.md`）只归用户指定的 leader agent 修改。
  其它 agent 和会话，包括其它 Claude 会话、正在修复测试所发现缺陷的实现者，
  都只能运行和阅读，禁止修改。
- 认为某个测试写错了，或者修复后需要去掉"已知失败"标记时，带着证据报告给
  leader，由 leader 决定并修改。
# AGENTS.md — Superchat

Superchat is an Emacs-native chat and Agent Runtime, packaged as one Emacs Lisp package over `llm.el`. This file is a navigation guide, not a second specification.

## Start here

- Read [CONTEXT.md](CONTEXT.md) and relevant [accepted ADRs](docs/adr/) before changing domain behavior; follow [domain navigation](docs/agents/domain.md).
- Use [docs/architecture.md](docs/architecture.md) for the current implementation, [docs/roadmap.md](docs/roadmap.md) for the only remaining plan, and [docs/README.md](docs/README.md) to place documentation. Accepted direction is not proof of implementation.
- The [composition contract](docs/design/plugin-architecture.md) and [request lifecycle contract](docs/protocols/request-lifecycle.md) describe current interfaces. `.wayfinder/` and the old fixed-pipeline specification are historical, not work queues.
- Work locally in the existing worktree. Preserve unrelated edits. Use jj when `.jj/` exists; do not rename modules merely to satisfy a file count.

## Locate the work

- UI and producers: `superchat.el`, `superchat-dispatcher.el`, `superchat-inline.el`, `superchat-rewrite.el`; the full ownership map is in [architecture](docs/architecture.md#composition-and-ownership).
- Execution: `superchat-runtime.el`; model adapter: `superchat-llm.el`; tool execution pipeline: `superchat-tool-execution.el`; permission levels: `superchat-sandbox.el`; approval: `superchat-approval.el`; selection: `superchat-select-tools.el`.
- Composition: `superchat-plugin.el`, `superchat-service.el`, `superchat-event.el`, `superchat-invariant.el`, `superchat-sink.el`. `superchat-pipeline.el` also contains the product driver; it is not a product-free registry file.
- Persistence/read models: `superchat-tape.el`, `superchat-db.el`, `superchat-projection.el`, `superchat-tape-view.el`. Keep DB storage, Tape facts and derived views distinct; recorded Run outcomes are consumed under the [request lifecycle contract](docs/protocols/request-lifecycle.md).
- Find public commands with `rg -n ';;;###autoload' --glob '*.el'`; inspect Service and Event declarations in source rather than copying an inventory.

## Preserve these contracts

- Runtime alone accepts, completes and cancels Runs. Plugins use the driver Service and the exact accepted Sink; see [request lifecycle](docs/protocols/request-lifecycle.md). Only Org Babel and Magit use `submit-blocking`.
- Every model-invoked tool crosses the one execution pipeline (`superchat-tool-execute`). The Session's permission level decides, the approval seam asks, a `tools/pre-execute` listener can only tighten. Missing trusted wrappers fail closed; a delegated child inherits its parent's sandbox mode and never asks. See [tool admission](docs/adr/0005-fail-closed-ordinary-llm-tool-admission.md) and [refusal delivery](docs/adr/0006-authority-refusal-consumer-delivery.md).
- Preset teardown calls Runtime retirement before plugin disposal. An unresolved external Effect may leave a terminal `audit-incomplete` Run; a genuine late fact cannot restart it. See [composition lifetime](docs/design/plugin-architecture.md#preset-composition-and-lifetime).
- Scope is registration visibility, not Session identity or buffer ownership. Only the Session EditorWorkspace closes its exact owned buffers under the [close protocol](docs/protocols/session-editor-workspace-close.md); Runtime borrows them.
- Tape is append-only. Anchor, View and Projection change interpretation or selection, not past Entries; a representation must retain its source relationship. See [Tape vocabulary](CONTEXT.md#tape-and-context) and the [Context View contract](docs/design/context-continuity-contract-v0.md).

## Develop and verify

- Follow [development verification](docs/agents/development.md): focused behavior, canonical smoke, Kernel check, package load and byte-compilation as appropriate. The deleted test suite is not a requirement to recreate. Report untested boundaries.
- **Do not edit the M3 regression set.** `scripts/m3-regression.el`, `.scratch/m3-verification/regression-mutants/` and `.scratch/m3-verification/REGRESSION-SET.md` are owned by the leader agent the user designated; no other agent or session may modify them, even when fixing a defect a test found. Run the set, and report a test you believe is wrong, or a known-failure marker your fix should flip, to the leader with evidence.
- Keep `lexical-binding: t`; use `superchat--name` for internal helpers and `superchat-name` for public functions; match surrounding code. Optional dependencies and package metadata rules are in the development guide.
- When editing inline widget.el controls, read the `emacs-widget-controls` skill. Plan review/progress belong in their side window, not inline in chat; see [UI conventions](docs/agents/development.md#ui-and-editor-conventions).
- Product `skills/` contains in-Emacs prompt text for `>skill`, not coding-agent skills. Use the [Skills guide](docs/SKILLS_QUICKSTART.md) for the actual discovery layout and format limitations.
- Write local issues under `.scratch/<feature>/` using the [issue tracker conventions](docs/agents/issue-tracker.md) and [triage labels](docs/agents/triage-labels.md). No separate publication, evidence-ledger or plan/capture ceremony is required.
- Keep README.md and README_cn.md aligned when changing user-facing guidance. Documentation changes should distinguish current behavior, accepted decisions, proposals and history; they do not authorize production changes.
# Superchat Agent Runtime

Superchat coordinates durable, tool-using work inside Emacs.  This language
keeps execution identity separate from work purpose, permissions, and
organization.

## Language

**Actor**:
An identity to which Runtime actions and events are attributed.  Users,
workflow steps, and agent Runs can all be Actors; not every Actor is an
Agent.
_Avoid_: Agent, when the participant may be non-agent

**Capability**:
The purpose and default behavior for a kind of work, selected independently
from Topology and the Session's Permission level.  An agent Preset fixes its Run
Capability; a Request may omit it or repeat it, but cannot reinterpret it.
_Avoid_: Agent, mode

**Topology**:
The organization of execution participants, such as single, subagent, team, or
workflow.  Topology does not define their Capability.
_Avoid_: Team Agent

**Permission level**:
A Session's standing answer to what a tool call may do: a sandbox mode
(`read-only`, `edit-buffers`, `danger-full-access`; `workspace-write` is the
default's former name and still accepted) plus an approval policy (`ask`,
`never`).  A level decides how the tools that exist may execute, never which
tools exist.  Built-in tools edit ordinary buffers freely except under
`read-only`, and never Superchat's own buffers; full-Emacs-control and
third-party tools are asked about, refused or free by level.  A delegated child inherits its parent's
mode with `never`.  It replaced Authority (effect policies) in M6; what was left of that module,
the tool execution pipeline, is `superchat-tool-execution.el` (named
`superchat-authority.el` until 2026-09-20).
_Avoid_: Authority, authority policy, attendance

**Session**:
A durable logical context spanning multiple Turns and Runs.  The chat View
shows one Session at a time, either beside the file being edited (editor mode)
or in the Session's own tab (agent mode); both modes show the same Session.
Entry points invoked from an ordinary buffer — inline ask, inline act, region
rewrite, annotation — join the main conversation of the Workspace that buffer
belongs to; from a buffer that belongs to none they join the temporary
conversation.
_Avoid_: Buffer, Run, project session

**Session Buffer Set**:
The live set of Emacs buffers one Session owns.  Ownership comes from exact
markers on the buffer, never from its name, tab, project or directory.  Where a
buffer is displayed (the Session's tab, or a side window of another tab) is
not ownership.  It is distinct from the durable Artifact Board; Runtime
borrows a buffer from it and never closes one.
_Avoid_: Session EditorWorkspace and EditorWorkspace (the name code identifiers
and older documents still carry), Session buffer, HostLease

**Owned Editor Buffer**:
A buffer created and marked by one Session Buffer Set.  Only the owning
Buffer Set may close it.
_Avoid_: chat buffer, project buffer, borrowed buffer

**Buffer Set Acquisition**:
Obtaining a Session's buffer for one role, learning in the same step whether
it was newly created.  The result is not a lease or a token; it only lets a
producer roll back a fresh buffer before Runtime accepts the Request.
_Avoid_: EditorWorkspace Acquisition, HostLease, ownership token, buffer handle

**Operation-scoped Child Buffer Set**:
A child Session's Buffer Set created by a producer for one bounded
operation and closed by that producer afterwards, not by Runtime's terminal
completion.
_Avoid_: per-Run cleanup, parent-owned host, direct buffer kill

**Turn**:
The lifecycle of one immutable Request after Runtime accepts it.
_Avoid_: Session, model call

**Run**:
One concrete execution by an Actor within a Turn.  Child Runs represent
delegated execution, explicitly reference their direct parent Run, and do not
create a separate Runtime.
_Avoid_: Agent, Session

**Skill**:
A named piece of task-specific instructions in a file (a folder with
`SKILL.md`, or a single file with a description) that the model finds in the
skill catalog and reads when a task needs it, or that the person invokes with
`>name`.
_Avoid_: preset (a Skill of type agent or plan is also a Preset), plugin

**Skill catalog**:
The list of Skills — name, one-line description, file location — carried in
the system text so the model knows what it can read.
_Avoid_: skill index, registry

**Region rewrite**:
One instruction applied to exactly the Rewrite target; its answer is the
replacement text alone, and it changes the buffer only when the user accepts it.
_Avoid_: inline edit, refactor

**Rewrite target**:
The text the user selected for a Region rewrite, and the only text its answer
may replace.
_Avoid_: region (when the surrounding text is meant), context

**Rewrite reference**:
Text around the Rewrite target sent so the model understands its setting; the
model may read it but never changes or returns it.
_Avoid_: context (the Context View is something else)

**Preset**:
A portable configuration source that can describe prompts, workflows,
Capabilities, and Plugin composition for a kind of work.  A Preset of type
agent is a reusable role definition that fixes its default Capability; a
Preset is not inherently an Agent and cannot widen a Permission level or own Runtime
state.  A Preset can define scoped Plugin composition; this registration
context is distinct from Session identity and Run execution.  A Session is
not a Scope layer.
_Avoid_: Agent, Agent Profile, profile

## Workspace

The behaviour behind Workspace, Temporary Workspace, Personal Workspace and
Work Record is the
[accepted contract](docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-01-workspace-work-record-session-rules.md);
which of it is built is stated per slice in the roadmap's M11.

**Workspace**:
The place work happens in: one directory that Emacs treats as a project,
together with what Superchat keeps for it — its rules (the `AGENTS.md` at its
root), its conversations and its Work Records.
A buffer belongs to the Workspace of its file; on screen a Workspace is its tab
group.
_Avoid_: project (Emacs's own object, which says where a Workspace is), working
copy (what jj calls a workspace), global memory

**Temporary Workspace**:
The Workspace of work done outside any project.  It has no directory and so no
rules, holds one conversation, and nothing in it is carried into another
conversation.
_Avoid_: default workspace, global workspace

**Personal Workspace**:
The Workspace the user designates as the home of interfaces that belong to no
directory, such as the agenda, feeds and chats.  Until one is designated, work
started from them is in a Temporary Workspace.
_Avoid_: global workspace, default workspace, home

**Work Record**:
The durable record of a piece of work that has to outlive the Run that started
it: what is to be delivered, how far it has got, why it cannot go on yet, and
where to pick it up.  Its id is what the conversations, Runs and agents working
on that piece share; it is not a log of every Run.
_Avoid_: work item, todo, task, reminder, ticket, work log

**Artifact Board**:
The durable registry of Artifacts — published pieces of work product, each with
a revision — through which a lead agent and its sub-agents pass results.
_Avoid_: Artifact Workspace, Workspace (for this), shared memory

## Composition

**Plugin**:
A unit of code that is mounted into a Scope and contributes Services, Event
listeners, tools, commands, Surfaces and policies through that Scope.
Scope-accounted contributions are reversible: unmounting the Plugin withdraws
them.  This does not make arbitrary Elisp mutation or an external Effect
reversible.  Built-in and third-party Plugins use the same composition model;
a product feature is not necessarily one Plugin.
_Avoid_: Extension (pi's term for a flat event-subscribing module), module,
package

**Scope**:
The registration context a Plugin receives.  Everything registered through a
Scope is visible within it and is withdrawn when it is disposed.  Scopes may
nest; a child sees its ancestors' contributions, never the reverse.
_Avoid_: context (overloaded with prompt context), namespace

**Service**:
A named capability with a declared interface, provided by exactly one mounted
Plugin at a time and consumed by others by name.  A Plugin declares the
Services it requires and is mounted only once they exist.  Consumers never
reach a provider's code directly.
_Avoid_: library, helper, `require`

**Event**:
A named extension point on the execution main line.  An Event declares one
combination rule for its listeners (first result wins, collect all, fold,
observe only, or an ordered-await waterfall), which is part of its contract.
An observe listener may be declared required when it is registered; its
failure is returned to the caller of that emission, which decides what it means.
_Avoid_: hook, signal

**Kernel**:
The part of Superchat that is not a Plugin: the Plugin loader, the Scope,
Service, Event and Invariant registries, the default mount list, and the
shared vocabulary every Plugin speaks (Envelope, Sink).  Kernel names
composition infrastructure, not the product responsibilities of the driver,
model loop, tool pipeline, Tape or chat window.
_Avoid_: core, runtime

**Invariant**:
A rule that holds regardless of which Plugins are mounted, checked at a seam
the Kernel owns rather than inside any one Plugin.  Examples: a source buffer
is unchanged until its Change Set is accepted; every terminal Run state is
recorded on the Tape; every tool execution passes the tool pipeline.
_Avoid_: assertion, contract

**Envelope**:
The immutable description of one Request as a producer hands it in: content,
Surface, media, Sink, and owner-prefixed context.  Every Surface produces the
same shape.
_Avoid_: request plist, legacy request

**Sink**:
The exact delivery target of a Run: the callbacks that receive its stream,
status, terminal result or failure.  A Sink carries no buffer routing; the
producer decides what to do with what it is handed.
_Avoid_: delivery function, stream function, view

## Tape and Context

**Tape**:
The append-only, durable history of Runtime Facts.  New summaries,
corrections, and judgments become new Entries; they never overwrite an older
Entry.
_Avoid_: mutable conversation state, latest state

**Entry**:
One immutable, ordered Fact on Tape with its own identity and provenance.
_Avoid_: message slot that can be replaced

**Anchor**:
An Entry that marks, indexes, or distills earlier Entries while retaining
enough source identity to return to them.  It may be a deterministic index or
an interpretation, but is always a reconstruction marker rather than a
deletion point.
_Avoid_: replacement, compressed Tape

**Tape View**:
A purpose-specific, rebuildable selection and rendering of Tape Entries.  It
may omit raw Entries or show a derived item in their place for one context,
but it never mutates Tape.
_Avoid_: stored history, rewritten Tape

**Projection**:
A rebuildable fold of Tape Facts into a current read model.  Materialized
Projection state may change or reset, but Tape Facts do not.
_Avoid_: source of truth, Tape mutation

**Handoff**:
A context or execution transition built from durable history, normally using
an Anchor as the reconstruction point.  It changes the active View, not the
past.
_Avoid_: history reset, new Runtime

## Experience Feedback

Claim, Feedback and Current View name the interpretation of retained `claim.recorded` and `feedback.recorded` Entries. These terms do not imply a built-in capture, correction or review command; see the [M3a decision](docs/decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3a-remove-closure-and-claims.md). Removing an entry point does not erase an Entry or turn a Claim into a Fact.

**Experience Thread**:
An auditable lineage of immutable Entries about one subject.  Later Claims,
Feedback, and Outcomes may extend or revise the interpretation of earlier
Facts without changing any earlier Entry.
_Avoid_: mutable memory record, latest truth, in-place learning

**Fact**:
An immutable, addressable observation already preserved by a Runtime surface,
such as a Tape event, Artifact revision, or Outcome Record.
_Avoid_: Claim, truth

**Claim**:
A contestable statement about Facts within an explicitly stated subject.  A
Claim may be wrong and may be superseded, but is never overwritten.
_Avoid_: Fact, Memory, learned capability

**Feedback**:
An append-only response that supports, challenges, or corrects one Claim and
identifies its Actor and factual basis.
_Avoid_: mutation, permission decision

**Current View**:
A Projection-derived interpretation of Claims and Feedback for one subject.  It
is rebuildable and must expose unresolved conflict instead of storing truth.
_Avoid_: canonical state, latest row wins

## Epistemic Learning

**Outcome**:
An immutable post-Execution observation from an environment, verifier, or
user about what occurred.  It does not label every earlier Action correct or
establish why the result occurred.
_Avoid_: success Pattern, step label, causal explanation

**Applicability Graph**:
An auditable relation model connecting contexts, Pattern Claims, Actions,
Outcomes, and Exercises.  Its relations express conditional support,
conflict, or missing evidence rather than universal truth.
_Avoid_: similarity index, source of truth, universal knowledge graph

**Applicability Relation**:
An append-only reviewed judgment that a Pattern Claim or Candidate applies or
fails under one Context Claim.  A failed Action or Outcome is evidence for
review, not an automatic negative relation.
_Avoid_: failure log, automatic rejection, mutable applicability flag

**Review Need**:
A rebuildable indication that recorded recovery evidence awaits an explicit
judgment.  It neither changes Promotion nor establishes an Applicability
Relation by itself.
_Avoid_: automatic verdict, negative relation, stored truth

**Experience View**:
A purpose-specific View that presents at most one active promoted Candidate applicable to the current Context. When enabled, recovery work uses it through the recovery Service; missing, conflicting, or ambiguous applicability yields no Candidate. This recovery View is distinct from a standalone prompt-context contribution.
_Avoid_: manual context attachment, automatic Learning, mutable Memory

**Exercise**:
A preregistered task designed to distinguish competing Pattern Claims or test
an applicability boundary.  Actor-visible material excludes its sealed oracle
and evaluation target.
_Avoid_: similar task, answer-bearing prompt, training example

**Promotion**:
An append-only decision that makes one versioned Candidate eligible to shape
a future View or Action policy after Evaluation.  It is not an assertion that
the Candidate is universally true.
_Avoid_: overwrite, Candidate creation, automatic truth

**Candidate**:
A reviewed Claim eligible for a separately preregistered intervention.  It is
not evidence that the pattern is useful or has been learned.
_Avoid_: Capability, promotion, validated improvement

**Learning**:
A durable change in how Runtime constructs a View or chooses an action,
justified by an auditable Experience Thread and evaluated by Outcomes
appended back to Tape.  The Candidate must come from a post-Run model
reflection over its own closed trajectory; a hand-written rule is supervised
replay, not Learning.  Human Promotion may remain the safety gate but is
recorded separately.  Learning never overwrites prior Entries or changes
model weights.
_Avoid_: context injection, one successful intervention, Memory write, Skill
overwrite, model training, fine-tuning
# Architecture

This is a map of the running implementation, not the target architecture. Read the [remaining plan](roadmap.md) for accepted changes and proposed designs, the [glossary](../CONTEXT.md) for domain language, and the [plugin guide](plugin-guide.md) to add an extension.

## Composition and ownership

Superchat is one Emacs package. Plugins contribute through Scope-accounted registrations; Service consumers call a named interface rather than selecting a provider implementation. A logical module or plugin is not necessarily a separate file.

| Responsibility | Source | What it does not own |
|---|---|---|
| Composition primitives | `superchat-plugin.el`, `superchat-service.el`, `superchat-event.el` (Event substrate, waterfalls and the fault ring), `superchat-invariant.el`, `superchat-sink.el` | Model execution, editor-resource ownership, durable Session state |
| Envelope validation and driver entry | `superchat-pipeline.el` | A second Run lifecycle; this file also defines the product `driver` plugin |
| Request preparation and chat routing | `superchat-core.el`, `superchat-parser.el`, `superchat-dispatcher.el`, `superchat-preset.el` | Runtime completion |
| Run/Turn lifecycle | `superchat-runtime.el` | Owning or closing borrowed editor buffers |
| Model transport and tool-loop instrumentation | `superchat-llm.el`, `superchat-models.el`, `superchat-agent-loop.el` | Public Run status or an alternative tool authorization decision |
| Tool selection, execution and permission | `superchat-select-tools.el`, `superchat-tools.el`, `superchat-tool-execution.el` (the execution pipeline), `superchat-sandbox.el` (permission levels), `superchat-approval.el` (approval) | Widening a permission level through child Scope shadowing |
| Prompt construction | `superchat-prompt-hooks.el`, `superchat-prompt.el` | Mutating Tape history |
| Durable facts and read models | `superchat-tape.el`, `superchat-db.el`, `superchat-projection.el`, `superchat-tape-view.el` | Treating a Projection or selected view as mutable source history |
| Workspaces, their rules, Work Records and timers | `superchat-workspace.el`, `superchat-work.el`, `superchat-timer.el`; binding and routing of conversations in `superchat.el` | Accepting or finishing a Run; confining what `eval_elisp` or a shell can reach |
| Chat View and live editor resources | `superchat.el`, `superchat-render.el`, `superchat-editor-workspace.el` | Inferring ownership from a buffer name, project or selected window |

Default plugin definitions live next to their implementations; `superchat-default-plugins.el` supplies lazy definitions. `superchat-plugins`, defined in `superchat-plugin.el`, is mounted at the end of `superchat.el`. Tools and entry points can be contributed by a shared plugin; feature names are not an inventory of independently removable plugins. Services have global providers, while contributions such as tools, commands and Event listeners have scoped visibility. The [composition contract](design/plugin-architecture.md) defines these distinctions.

The large UI, dispatcher, Runtime and LLM modules retain direct dependencies. The Kernel vocabulary and product code are also co-located in `superchat-pipeline.el`. A table calling all of these files a product-free Kernel would misdescribe the implementation.

## One request through the system

Public model producers submit an Envelope containing input, a declared Surface and an exact Sink through the `driver` Service. The driver adapts that Envelope to Runtime's internal Request; Runtime alone accepts and finishes execution.

1. **Prepare surface input.** Chat parses `@model`, `>skill`, `/command` and `#file` with `superchat-core-prepare-turn` over a preparation Turn. The dispatcher handles local commands and routing before a model Envelope exists. The preparation Turn and Runtime's execution Turn share a struct type, not a lifecycle owner.
2. **Run the driver preparation sequence.** `superchat-pipeline-submit` validates the Envelope, resolves the Session, admits the submission through Runtime, merges core state, selects tools, collects context through the `collect-context` Event and assembles the prompt. Admission may process, queue, steer, drop or handle the input without starting a new Run.
3. **Accept execution.** Runtime receives the prepared Request and borrows the caller's execution Scope. The `model-transport` provider runs model attempts and the tool loop. Tools cross the one execution pipeline, including asynchronous and delegated tool calls; the Session's permission level decides each call and the approval seam asks when it must.
4. **Persist and deliver.** Runtime uses its per-Run accounting of unsettled Effects and undecided Approvals when sealing, records Run/Turn facts and delivers through the originally accepted Sink. Required recording failures retain their audit-incomplete handling. Runtime quiesces execution; a late fact cannot reopen its terminal state. Consumers use the recorded Run terminal under the [request lifecycle contract](protocols/request-lifecycle.md).

The fixed Stage registry is retired: the eight names it declared (`parse`, `resolve-session`, `admit`, `load-state`, `select-tools`, `collect-context`, `assemble-prompt`, `save-state`) are no longer a registration or lookup surface. `parse` is the chat surface's own direct call; Session identity, state merging, admission, tool selection and prompt assembly are core driver calls; `collect-context` is an Event whose listeners return fragment lists. Extension now happens through Events and the two ordered-await waterfalls (`pre-step`, `before-provider-request`); calling the model, sealing a Run and delivering to a Sink were never Stages. The [request lifecycle contract](protocols/request-lifecycle.md) covers admission, queues, cancellation and failure handling.

## Prompt and history

`collect-context` listeners return labelled fragments. The pure `superchat-prompt-assemble` orders those fragments and returns `(:system :messages :receipt)`. Hard fragments must fit; soft fragments can be refit, truncated or omitted. A plugin adds or withdraws fragments through its own Scope registration, not by adding another driver.

The chat and inline-ask history collector uses a Tape-backed Context View with source exclusions and a remaining-budget refit. Other producers, including skills, workflows, subagents and compaction, can supply prepared text through their collector. The chat View also maintains process-local conversation state; that state is not permission to overwrite Tape. Anchor-backed compaction changes the selected View and preserves the original Entries.

The assembly receipt records selected fragment identities and budget decisions. Before each provider attempt `superchat--llm-record-request-evidence` appends a `request.header` (the system text and the tool schemas as the provider layer is about to send them) and a `request.message-list` (the messages as storage references, including material a Step inserted during the Run). The `prompt-reconstructable` Invariant runs after both rows exist and rebuilds the request from them; it fails when the receipt, the header reference or a named storage id does not resolve. It does not compare the rebuilt request with the bytes the provider received, and a request whose evidence the Tape refused is sent anyway: recording evidence never stops the request it describes.

## State and lifetimes

Session identity, execution identity, registration visibility and buffer ownership have different owners.

| State | Owner and lifetime |
|---|---|
| Tape Entries | Durable append-only facts; DB provides physical storage |
| Projection / Tape View | Rebuildable interpretations and selections of those facts |
| Session EditorWorkspace | Exact Session-owned buffer objects; its close protocol decides which buffers may close |
| Active Preset Scope | One exact Chat View activation; same-named Presets in separate Views are distinct |
| Run state | Runtime, from accepted Request through terminal handling and quiesce |
| Queued input | Runtime's in-process FIFO; a queued item retains its original Sink and borrowed Scope |
| Workspace binding of a Session | `workspace.bound` / `workspace.unbound` facts on that Session's Tape; read through `superchat-view-session-configuration` |
| Work Records and timers | Facts on the Tape (`work.*`, `timer.*`); the lists are rebuilt from them |
| Armed wake-ups and reminders | Emacs timers held in process memory (`superchat-work--armed`, `superchat-timer--armed`); rebuilt from the Tape when the `work-records` and `timers` rows are mounted |
| Which Run a woken record started | Read from the Tape: `work.woken` names the conversation the record woke in, and the first `run.started` of that conversation is its execution (`superchat-work--run-of`). It survives a restart; a wake whose conversation never ran an execution is put back as a wait |

Runtime borrows execution Scopes; it creates no automatic Run Scope. There is no Session Scope level. The Preset owner calls Runtime retirement before disposing registrations on switch or View deactivation. Generic Scope disposal is not an independent Run cancellation or buffer-close authority. See the [Preset lifecycle](design/plugin-architecture.md#preset-composition-and-lifetime) and [Session close protocol](protocols/session-editor-workspace-close.md).

## Workspaces, Work Records and timed work

A Workspace is where a conversation belongs. `superchat-workspace-of-source` decides it from the buffer a conversation is started from: a buffer inside an Emacs project belongs to that project's Workspace; a buffer in one of `superchat-personal-workspace-modes` (agenda, calendar, feeds, chat, mail) belongs to the Personal Workspace when `superchat-personal-workspace-directory` is set; anything else is a temporary conversation with no directory. The binding is recorded on the Session's Tape as `workspace.bound`, and later readers take the root from that fact, not from the buffer that happens to be current.

What a Workspace scopes today, and what it does not:

| Path | Scope in the code |
|---|---|
| Rules (`AGENTS.md`, else `CLAUDE.md`, read from disk at collection time, at most 6000 characters) | The bound Workspace's root. One collector (`superchat-prompt-collector--workspace-rules`) serves the `chat`, `inline-ask` and `inline-act` surfaces, which also get the note about remembering, and the `inline-rewrite`, `inline-annotate` and `workflow` surfaces, which get the rules alone (a rewrite's and an annotation's each followed by a sentence saying which rules apply to that answer); a delegated sub-agent gets the same rules appended to its system text. A rules file that exists and cannot be read is not treated as absent (`superchat-workspace-rules-problem`): the user gets a message, and on the three chat surfaces the request tells the model the rules are missing ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-06-rules-on-rewrite-and-workflow.md)) |
| Automatic recall and the model's `memory_search` | The conversations of the same Workspace (`superchat-view-recall-session-ids`), narrowed further by `superchat-memory-auto-recall-cross-session`; a temporary conversation searches only itself |
| The `[Work records]` block | Records of the same Workspace; a temporary conversation has none |
| The model's `work_record` tool | A record of the asking conversation's Workspace (`superchat-work--for`), for read, update, wait and done and for the record a new one follows; a conversation of no Workspace reaches only a record begun in that very conversation, which today is none. Another Workspace's record is refused without showing what it holds. The user's list and commands, the wake and what the product writes for a sub-agent reach every record ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-work-records-and-timers-belong-to-a-workspace.md)) |
| The model's `timer_list` and `timer_cancel` | Timers of the asking conversation's Workspace (`superchat-timer--within-p`), pending, fired, and fired-but-not-started; a conversation of no Workspace has only those set in it. `superchat-timer-cancel`, which the user's commands call, cancels any |
| The model's `memory_search`, `tool_history`, `file_history`, `recent_errors` | The conversation of the Run that made the call (`superchat-tools--history-session`), not the buffer current when the tool runs; `memory_search` widens that to the Workspace as above, the other three stay in the one conversation. A call that belongs to no conversation searches nothing |
| The model's `sql` tool | **Not scoped, and not offered.** It reads every Session's rows, so nothing Superchat ships offers it: it is in no built-in Preset and is not part of `superchat-llm-tool-names` `all`. It exists only where the user's own list names it ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-05-sql-leaves-the-ordinary-tool-surface.md)) |
| The user's `/recall` | Every conversation by default (`superchat-memory-recall-scope`); this is the user's own search, not the model's |
| `eval_elisp`, shells, agent-written tools | Not confined by a Workspace. They are the `full-control` class and are governed by the permission level alone |

A Work Record is the record of a piece of work that outlives one Run. The model registers it through the `work_record` tool (start, update, wait, done, read); `superchat-work.el` keeps it as `work.*` facts and decides the outcome of each execution from facts at `turn-completed`, without reading the answer. A record that is neither concluded nor waiting is continuable and waits for the user; nothing takes it up by itself.

Two things start a Run without the user typing:

- **A task timer** (`timer_create` with kind `task`). `superchat-timer-fire` appends `timer.fired` to the Workspace's most recently active conversation, tells the user, and then starts the task there: `timer.sending` is appended, the timer's text is sent, and `timer.started` names the Run that began. While an execution is running in that conversation nothing is sent and the task waits.
- **A record's wait for a time.** `superchat-work-wake` opens a new conversation bound to the record's Workspace, appends `work.woken` (naming the Workspace's main conversation), clears the wait, makes the record that conversation's current record and sends the wake message. When sending does not finish, the wait is put back. When the first Run of that conversation ends, one line is left in the main conversation.

Both go through the chat View's own send (`superchat-send-input` with `superchat-automated-input` bound), so Runtime remains the only thing that accepts the Run. Both write their "fired" fact **before** they send, so an occurrence is counted as come due once. Whether its execution started is a separate question, answered from later facts (`timer.sending`, `timer.started`, `timer.unconfirmed`; `work.woken` and the conversation's first `run.started`). When Emacs exited between the send and those facts and an execution began in that conversation afterwards, Superchat cannot tell whether the task ran: it says so and does not start it again. This is not a guarantee that an effect outside Emacs happens exactly once. What follows a send that does not happen differs between the two:

- **Work Record** (`scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el`, 14 tests). A wake counts only when a Run started in its conversation: `superchat-work--due-p` reads `run.started` on that conversation's Tape. When the send signals, the wait is put back at once and the wake is tried again after `superchat-work-wake-retry-seconds` (300), at most `superchat-work-wake-retry-limit` (3) times in one Emacs. When Emacs exited between the facts and the send, the next scan (`superchat-work-wake-due`, run when the `work-records` row mounts) sees a wake whose conversation never ran and to whose record nothing was written since, and puts the wait back; the record is then taken up in a further new conversation. A record written to after the cut-off wake is left alone. One record's wake failing does not stop the scan.
- **Timer** (`scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 12 tests). A task counts as started only when `timer.started` is on the Tape. A fired task without it (`superchat-timer-unstarted-tasks`) is started later by `superchat-timer-retry-tasks`: after `superchat-timer-retry-seconds` (300) when the send signalled, at most `superchat-timer-retry-limit` (3) times in one Emacs; after `superchat-timer-busy-retry-seconds` (30), without limit, when the conversation was busy; and first thing in `superchat-timer-catch-up` after a restart. The occurrence keeps its single `timer.fired`. One case is not started again: `timer.sending` is on record, `timer.started` is not, and a Run started in that conversation afterwards. That Run may be the task, so `timer.unconfirmed` is appended and the conversation is told that Superchat cannot tell. A fired task that has not started can be cancelled. Only a `timer.fired` carrying `awaits-start` takes part, so occurrences fired before these facts existed are left alone. The retry counts are in memory.
- The pairing of a woken record with its Run is read from the Tape: the `work.woken` fact sits in the woken conversation and names the main conversation, and a `work.reported` fact marks that the line was written. It holds across a restart. A Run that an earlier Emacs started and never finished is found by the scan (`superchat-work--report-cut-off`): the record gets the outcome `interrupted`, the main conversation is told once that the execution was cut off and was not started again, and the model later sees that on the record. When that Run did end and only the line was missing, just the line is written. Nothing is started again in either case.
- A Run that was interrupted with an external Effect unresolved ends `audit-incomplete` under the [request lifecycle contract](protocols/request-lifecycle.md); neither a timer nor a Work Record retries it.

A woken record's new conversation has no recorded permission level, so it runs under `superchat-sandbox-default-mode`. Under the default `edit-buffers` level its edits stay in buffers and each `full-control` call asks for approval, with nobody necessarily present to answer. Under `danger-full-access` a `full-control` tool can write to disk without asking. "A timed Run never reaches the disk" is therefore not a guarantee of the code; what holds is that `edit_buffer` never saves and that `save_buffer` asks on every call.

### Working beside the user

Four things hold where an agent and the user share one Emacs ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-07-working-beside-the-user.md); `scripts/beside-user-regression.el`, 13 tests):

- **An execution nobody started shows no review screen.** `superchat-changes--unattended-run-p` reads the conversation's Tape: a `timer.started` naming the Run, or a `work.woken` fact with the Run being that conversation's first. Such a Run's unsaved changes are announced in the echo area, and its ending -- completed, failed or cancelled, with or without changes -- leaves a notice counted on the mode line (`[Superchat: N]`, `superchat-changes-notices`) until the user looks at it; the notices live in this Emacs only. A Run the user started still gets the review screen, which takes the cursor.
- **A Run does not need its View.** Killing the conversation's buffer leaves the Run going; only an `ask_user` call made after that fails, with a result that tells the model the user cannot be asked. Approval questions do not depend on the View.
- **A file that changed on disk is noticed before a structured edit** (`superchat-tools--disk-change-before-edit`, by `verify-visited-file-modtime`). A buffer without unsaved changes is reloaded and the edit is returned to the model to redo against the new text; a buffer with unsaved changes is edited and the result says that saving would overwrite the disk. `read_buffer` notes the difference. The user gets one echo-area line. Edits made through `eval_elisp` or a shell do not pass this check.
- **A Work Record shown to the user names its executions** (`superchat-work-executions-text`): Run, conversation and outcome, oldest first. The record text a request carries is unchanged.

A server error body that llm.el's provider cannot read is turned into an ordinary error by an advice in `superchat-llm.el`, so the Run ends instead of waiting for a callback that never comes.

## Entry matrix

Each row is a producer that submits an Envelope through the `driver` Service. "Rules" means the Workspace rules described above. The permission level of a Session is its last recorded `sandbox.mode`, else `superchat-sandbox-default-mode`; a producer with `:tools 'none` offers the model no tool, so no level is consulted.

| Producer (surface) | Session and Workspace | Rules | Permission level | History and Work Records |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Chat window, `superchat-act` (`chat`) | The View's Session. A View opened from a buffer is its Workspace's main conversation (`superchat-conversation-session-id`), else a temporary one | The `workspace-rules` collector, from the Session's `workspace.bound` root, plus the note about remembering | The Session's | Tape Context View of the Session; recall inside the Workspace; the `[Work records]` block. A message that holds `>annotate` is this same row with three differences recorded on its Turn (`:destination annotate`): no tools, the row form added last to the system text with the target buffer's numbered text attached, and the View's Sink wrapped so the answer is also hung on that buffer (`superchat-annotate-chat-sink`) |
| In-place ask and act (`inline-ask`, `inline-act`) | `superchat-inline-session-id`: the same conversation the chat window would show for the source buffer | Same collector as chat | That Session's | No earlier turn of the conversation; a follow-up in the same overlay carries that overlay's own earlier Runs. No recall. The `[Work records]` block |
| Region rewrite (`inline-rewrite`) | Same Session as in-place ask | The Workspace's rules alone, from the same collector, placed before the request's own rewrite system text; no note about remembering | None consulted (`:tools 'none`) | None |
| Annotation (`inline-annotate`, registered by the `inline-rewrite` row) | Same Session as in-place ask | The Workspace's rules alone, from the same collector, followed by the annotation's own note; no note about remembering | None consulted (`:tools 'none`) | None. The answer is read as rows `LINE \| QUOTE \| REMARK` and hung on the source buffer as overlays; the buffer is not changed ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-remarks-hung-on-the-text.md)) |
| Task timer | The Workspace's most recently active conversation; the conversation it was set in when that was temporary | As chat: it is sent through that View | That Session's | As chat |
| Woken Work Record | A new conversation bound to the record's Workspace | As chat | The default level (a new Session has recorded none) | A new conversation, so no earlier turns; the woken record is current and appears in the `[Work records]` block |
| Delegated sub-agent (`subagent`) | Its own Session (`subagent-…`); Workspace id and root copied from the parent's tool execution context at delegation | The rules text appended to its system text by `superchat--subagent-system-with-rules`; not the note about remembering | The parent's effective level, pinned when it starts; approval policy `never`, so it never asks | None of the parent's. A prepared prompt, plus a snapshot of the parent's current Work Record when there is one; the task is recorded under that record |
| Workflow step (`workflow`) | The Session of the View the workflow runs in; that View's Workspace id and root travel in `:runtime-extras` | The Workspace's rules alone, from the same collector, on every step; no note about remembering | That Session's | A prepared step prompt; no history, no `[Work records]` block; not recorded as conversation |
| Compaction (`compaction`) | An isolated Session `<session>/compaction-<hash>` | None | None consulted (`:tools 'none`) | Only the material it is given as a prepared prompt |
| Skill matching (`skills`) | An ephemeral Session `skills-match-…` | None | None consulted (`:tools 'none`) | Only the prepared match prompt; not recorded as conversation |
| Org Babel, Magit (`org-babel`, `magit`; blocking) | The Session that owns the calling buffer when there is one, else an ephemeral id. No Workspace is resolved | None | None consulted (`:tools 'none`) | None; not recorded as conversation |

The Telega and emacsclient integrations were not traced for this table.

## Extension interfaces and surfaces

Services expose declared methods; core driver calls perform request preparation. Events have declared `observe`, `collect`, `fold`, `first` or `waterfall` combination rules. Definitions and callback arguments live in `superchat-service.el` and `superchat-event.el`, rather than in a second handwritten inventory. Observe registrations may be required; Runtime uses the current `run-sealing` emission's failures to downgrade an otherwise complete Run when its recording is incomplete. Named Authority policies can only tighten the effective decision.

Chat, inline ask/rewrite, workflow, subagent, compaction, skills, Telega and emacsclient are producers or integrations over these interfaces. Org Babel and Magit use the declared blocking adapter because their host interfaces require a synchronous result. Overlay-based rewrite applies source changes only through an accepted Change Set. Plans, Memory, Workspace and Experience retain their domain-owned read models and integrations; they do not create another Runtime. Experience remains a default plugin with its recovery Service, projection, completion observer and read-only monitor. Its standalone context collector and review commands are absent; retained Claim/Feedback event types remain readable. The removal scope is defined by the [M3a decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3a-remove-closure-and-claims.md).

Use [development verification](agents/development.md) for the canonical smoke and Kernel checks. The [roadmap](roadmap.md), not this map, owns migration order and remaining work.
# Roadmap

The one remaining plan for superchat. It says what is built, what the user has decided, what is still a proposal, and in which order the spine is simplified without giving up the safety contract already delivered. The current map of the code is [architecture.md](architecture.md); this page does not restate it.

## Status labels

Every sentence about the system in this page and in `docs/decisions/` carries one of four labels.

| Label | Meaning | Where it lives |
|---|---|---|
| `current` | In the tree and verified against the code | [architecture.md](architecture.md), component specs |
| `accepted` | The user has decided it. Whether it is unbuilt, partly built or built is stated per item below | `docs/decisions/accepted/` |
| `proposed` | A recommendation awaiting the user | `docs/decisions/proposed/` |
| `historical` | Superseded by an explicit later record; kept for reference | superseded decision records, `docs/design/turn-pipeline-contract.md`, `.wayfinder/` |

The ADRs under `docs/adr/` (0001, 0004, 0005, 0006) are accepted contracts in force; an ADR becomes `historical` only when a later record names it as superseded.

A milestone is `done` when its removal list is gone from the tree and its own acceptance section is satisfied. Code milestones name real-model (LM Studio) and scripted-transport evidence; a documentation milestone names link, example and review evidence instead.

The user decisions recorded under `docs/decisions/accepted/` on 2026-09-12 were checked against the user's own words during the collaboration between the two editing agents. None of them approves trimming the default plugin set. The [M3a removal decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3a-remove-closure-and-claims.md) of 2026-09-15 does: it removes Closure from the default set and deletes Claims.

## Baseline (`current`, 2026-09-12)

Delivered through batch 4C-2: caller-borrowed execution Scope through Stage, Event and Runtime; scoped tool, command and Authority registries; per-Chat-View Preset Scope; Runtime retires a Scope's Runs before that Scope is disposed; a Run borrows its Scope and never owns it; closing a View or leaving the mode deactivates its Preset; a Run whose external Effect is unresolved at cancellation terminates as `audit-incomplete` while the Effect itself stays open, and if a genuine late result arrives its one completion fact is appended without resuming the Run. The prompt's `history` fragment is assembled from the Tape Context View (`superchat-view-assemble`); the chat window and compaction still read a separate in-buffer history list.

Where the current contracts live: the cross-module request lifecycle in [protocols/request-lifecycle.md](protocols/request-lifecycle.md); the composition contract in [design/plugin-architecture.md](design/plugin-architecture.md); the plugin author's guide in [plugin-guide.md](plugin-guide.md); commands, dependencies and environment quirks in [agents/development.md](agents/development.md). The former [turn-pipeline-contract.md](design/turn-pipeline-contract.md) is `historical` and kept for its section links. Past batch acceptance records stay in `.wayfinder/` and are not copied into tutorials.

Batch 4C-3 (an automatic Run Scope) is not started and is not scheduled on its own; Run lifetime is designed inside M3 so that no empty Scope layer is added.

## Decisions already taken (`accepted`)

| Decision | Implementation state |
|---|---|
| DeepSeek Harness is the blueprint; superchat's goal is a complete, opinionated AI layer for Emacs ([record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-12-deepseek-harness-blueprint.md)) | A direction; no code change is implied by itself |
| Experience, Claims and Closure leave the spine and ship as official plugins; they stay in the default set ([record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-12-experience-claims-closure-as-official-plugins.md)); superseded in part by the M3a removal decision below | What stands, Experience as an official plugin in the default set with the spine loading without it, is checked by M3a |
| Following DeepSeek Harness, Closure and Claims are deleted, Experience loses its context collector and review entry and stays in the default set, and a Run cancelled during a tool still seals `audit-incomplete` at once ([record](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3a-remove-closure-and-claims.md)) | Implemented and verified under M3a; the record moved to `docs/decisions/implemented/` |
| Reference material the model may read must not apply a Preset: a superchat skill returns one, so invoking it switches model, tools and often the View's Capability, which is a mode change rather than something read to inform one thought ([record](decisions/proposed/2026-09-16-emacs-operating-context-and-reference-contract.md), slices 1 and 2 accepted 2026-09-16) | Slices 1 and 2 delivered under M5; the reading interface and guide stay `proposed`. The reviewed comparison supports origin context but does not establish the benefit of reference reading |
| Documentation follows one spec per component with a fixed structure, decision records with mandatory alternatives, present tense, one home per fact ([record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-12-documentation-regime.md)) | Scaffold in place (`docs/README.md`, `components/`, `decisions/`, `postmortems/`); component specs follow as modules land; M0 finishes the alignment |
| One conversation in two modes — a side panel while editing files, the conversation's own full tab while operating Emacs, one key between them; the attended-run s/n review of unsaved buffers in the product, colored, appearing by itself and taking the cursor (since 2026-09-25); in-buffer entry points join the conversation shown ([record](decisions/implemented/2026-09-24-editor-mode-agent-mode-and-review-screen.md)) | Implemented; every slice accepted by attended runs |
| A Workspace is an Emacs project with its rules, conversations and Work Records; no memory applies everywhere; agents do not share a conversation; work that outlives a Run has a Work Record the model registers and the product keeps and closes ([record](decisions/accepted/2026-10-01-workspace-work-record-session-rules.md), accepted 2026-10-02) | Slices 1–9 of M11 implemented, accepted by sandbox runs on one model and not yet tried in the user's own Emacs; the renames not started. The gaps the code still has are listed under [Workspaces, Work Records and timed work](architecture.md#workspaces-work-records-and-timed-work) |

Still not decided by the user: whether the model-facing tool surface drops path-file tools for Emacs-object tools. M5 approaches the surface question from the other end — what the model knows about the machine it is on, and which verbs it already has — and answers nothing about that swap. Until it is decided, no existing tool, plugin or feature (workflow, MCP, memory, plans, teams, workspace, GenUI, emacsclient) is removed from the default set, apart from what the M3a removal decision names.

## Milestones

The order M1 to M4 and each milestone's sub-goal are the two editing agents' joint recommendation under the accepted blueprint and the Authority and Tape contracts in force; the user has not approved them item by item. M5 is different: the user set its direction themselves on 2026-09-16, from how Omarchy ships operating knowledge to each agent, and accepted its first two slices. Each milestone's mechanism is `proposed` until its own record is accepted.

### M0 — Documentation alignment (`done`)

Removes: the "Destination reached" claim in the working map, stage and Kernel-file counts that disagree with the tree, and the stale sentences in the earlier proposal (a Preset without a Scope, a prompt built without the Tape). `.scratch/` stays the local issue tracker.

Acceptance, limited to the documents changed in this round: each either describes `current` behaviour or carries one of the other three labels; `docs/decisions/accepted/` holds the four decisions above; every relative link in the changed documents resolves; every runnable example in an active guide written or changed in this round has been run; for a historical document only its status header and its changed links are checked, and the old examples in its body are not claimed as re-run; both editing agents have read each other's changed files in full and resolved every conflict; no `.el` or `scripts/` file changed during M0 (hashes compared before and after). Documents not touched in this round are not claimed as re-verified.

### M1 — One model history (`done`)

Goal: one authority for model-visible history. The Tape is the source from which it is derived; the chat View may keep a cache only if that cache is regenerated from the Tape and never read as an authority.

Removes: the in-buffer history list as an authority for compaction and rendering; any path that composes prompt history from a buffer instead of the Tape.

Mechanism: a `derive-messages` function over the Tape Context View; compaction and the chat window read it; before the buffer list loses its authority, the two histories are rebuilt for the same recorded session and compared. The evidence this acceptance needs is not on the Tape today; [the request-header decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-13-reconstructable-provider-request.md) is `implemented` and fixes what to record and how to keep it from restating every request.

Delivered (`current`): `superchat-view-derive-messages` is the one derivation, honoring the compaction digest, the expanded-Anchor reading and the in-buffer cap; `request.header` and `request.message-list` are recorded before each provider attempt under the four write reasons the decision fixes; `superchat-tape-request-evidence` rebuilds a request from those rows and reports `unknown` when a reference does not resolve; `prompt-reconstructable` performs that reconstruction; the in-buffer list is regenerated from the derivation and is no longer edited by compaction or Anchor expansion.

Verification: the scripted fixture reproduces the provider-visible system text, message history and tool schemas, reports `unknown` when a reference does not resolve, and the buffer list and the derivation agree before compaction, after compaction and after Anchor expansion; compaction keeps the Anchor's identity, summary and sources. Against a real provider, one chat turn and one inline turn both complete their model exchange with every request rebuilt from the Tape and no invariant violations. The two transports assert different facts and the smoke script records which: the exact event sequence, the settled overlay and the untouched host buffer are `scripted-' keys, which the accepted criteria waive for a real model's free text, while `reconstruct-status' and `invariant-violations' are asserted in both modes.

Acceptance: for a recorded fixture (fixed input and fixed model result, scripted transport), an independent reconstruction from the Tape reproduces the provider-visible system text, messages and tool schemas of every request, including each rewrite and retry, with the source relation of every message stated; where redaction or missing evidence prevents reconstruction the check reports `unknown`, never a pass; the buffer-list history and the Tape-derived history agree on that same fixture; compaction on the fixture produces the same anchors as before. A recorded `prompt.assembled` receipt alone is not acceptance. Separately, one chat turn and one inline turn against a real provider (LM Studio) complete with their history derived from the Tape; the model's free text is not compared byte for byte with an older output.

### M2 — One tool pipeline (`done`)

Goal: every tool execution goes through one pipeline with Authority as the single decision point and approval as a seam. The mechanism is [the one-tool-pipeline decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-13-one-tool-pipeline.md).

Candidates for removal, re-confirmed against the source before M2 started (they come from the 2026-09-12 full-read audit and were not re-checked after 4C-2). All four were still present, and two are narrower than the audit described; a candidate that the source no longer shows is dropped from the list, not removed from the code:

| Candidate | Re-checked finding | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| a duplicated default tool list | `superchat--select-tools-baseline-names` and the default value of `superchat-llm-tool-names` hold the same five names in different order | removed: the baseline reads the one home (`superchat-llm-default-tool-names`), with a declared fallback literal for the load order |
| a second tool-selection pass on chat input | The decision is not recomputed -- the pipeline's `:tool-decision` reaches the LLM layer and is honored. The redundancy is a second registry query in `superchat--collect-llm-tools` | removed: a `baseline` decision's own `:materialized` structs are reused, so the chat path no longer queries the registry a second time |
| `fboundp` probes between Authority and the agent-loop wrappers | Seven probes in `superchat-authority.el`; four point at the agent loop and three at Runtime | removed for the four: each is a declared row in `superchat-authority--contributors` whose absent-case answer is written down. The three Runtime-side probes stay, out of this record's scope |
| the `on-demand` selection alias | Inert by its own documentation; kept for saved custom values and identical to `always` | retained, as the record decides |


Mechanism (`accepted`, implemented): pipeline order pre-policy → approval → monotonic guards (deny-only) → around → body → post → finalize, with the seven steps named at their boundaries in `superchat-tool-execute`; the approval prompt is a declared provider whose absence is a stated deny that the refusal reports as "no approver available" instead of "cancelled by user"; the named-policy monotonicity the record asked for already held and is a regression gate rather than new work.

Delivered (`current`): the pipeline's order is marked in the source and each step keeps the behaviour it replaced; approval resolves through one declared provider over the three existing sources in their existing precedence; the four agent-loop probes are gone, replaced by declared contributions whose missing case is stated in the table rather than implied at the call site; the default tool surface is unchanged, name for name.

Acceptance evidence: `.scratch/m1-verification/evidence-m2-acceptance.txt` records all three, and `scripts/smoke-expected/*` is byte-identical to the baseline M2 started from (`44153daa7ca8c4bc8064f5bb39f2a169f54e072feed59879655c490be57153be`). (a) An approved `read_buffer` on a real model (LM Studio, `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`) produces the Tape sequence smoke case B asserts, element for element, with one `request.header` and `superchat-tape-request-evidence` reporting `reproduced`. (b) A denied write and a denied read each reach the pipeline, run no tool body, record their own sequence (`tool.requested` then `tool.failed`, with no `tool.execution.started`) and deliver the refusal into the model's tool-result slot. (c) With a registered policy layer answering `allow` for everything, an `observer`-policy write is still refused with the policy's own text and its body still does not run, while a read in the same session still executes -- so the layer was consulted and could not loosen the inherited deny.

Acceptance (a) failed on its first run and found a real defect in M1's evidence recorder: from the second attempt on, the recorder read the system text off a prompt whose `:context` llm.el had already folded into a system interaction and cleared, so it recorded an empty system and appended a second `request.header` whose only change was that erasure. The provider always received the system text; the record did not. The recorder now takes the sending layer's own provider-facing system text instead, so the recorded sequence stays one header per series.

### M3 — One execution main line (`done`)

Goal: replace the fixed Stage registry with a single driver loop.

Removes: the Stage registry as an extension point, and the dispersed ownership of its eight names (`parse` is invoked only by the chat dispatcher over a preparation Turn, the six preparation Stages by the driver, `save-state` by Runtime); the dispersed ownership of pending input, so that follow-ups, steering and injection have one Runtime owner and steering and injection one ordered claim, over the three stores the [closing rulings](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3-closing-rulings.md) keep. The count difference between declared and invoked names is not itself a defect; the dispersed ownership is what M3 ends.

Mechanism: the [execution main line decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-14-m3-execution-main-line-resolution.md), taken by the leader under user delegation, fixes a Runtime-owned handle and target-aware inbox, pre-step and request waterfalls, a closing serial notification, and explicit `step.started` / `step.ended` Tape facts. Runtime retains sole lifecycle ownership. Existing queue, cancellation, Authority and retire-before-dispose contracts remain binding; no graceful-stop API or restartable pending queue is added.

Runtime owns the driver handle, Step boundaries, both waterfalls and the closing recheck; steer and inject have one ordered claim and audited pending-injection disposal. Earlier evidence is in `.scratch/m3-verification/evidence-m3-worker-round.txt`.

Stage retirement slice 2 is delivered (2026-09-15): `save-state`, `load-state`, `resolve-session`, `assemble-prompt` and `select-tools` are absent from the Stage vocabulary. Core assembles the prompt and builds default tool candidates; request middleware replaces tool selection within the original explicit ceiling. First and subsequent native attempts validate the replacement, preserve trusted wrappers, and record actual provider evidence. Pure tool-guidance renderers refresh dependent system blocks without repeating collectors. The default plugins remain mounted. Evidence and the narrowly authorized structural assertion replacement in smoke V are in `.scratch/m3-verification/LEADER-SLICE2.md`.

Stage retirement slice 3 (context, admission and parsing) is delivered (2026-09-15): `collect-context` is an Event whose listeners return fragment lists, and the driver's own direct call replaces the last Stage call site. `parse` is the chat surface's call to `superchat-core-prepare-turn`; submission admission and queueing live in Runtime (`superchat-runtime-admit-envelope`), with Step gating still in the pre-step waterfall. The Stage registry API (`superchat-stage-add`, `superchat-stage-call`, `superchat-stage-remove`, `superchat-stage-names`) no longer exists in the tree; the Event substrate, the two ordered-await waterfalls, the `collect-context` Event and the fault ring live in `superchat-event.el`. A required listener's execution or validation failure is reported as `context-error` by the pipeline's own collection error path and then re-signalled, so preparation still terminates. Fault records use `:site`; their recorded values are unchanged. Evidence is in `.scratch/m3-verification/LEADER-SLICE3.md`.

The M3 regression set replaced the ad-hoc probes (2026-09-15): `scripts/m3-regression.el` is one file of ERT tests grouped by contract area, run by one command, with each test in its own isolated fixture; only the leader agent edits it. It asserts contracts a fixed event sequence cannot express (who may claim what, what a refusal leaves behind, which Step a fact belongs to, what a failure is recorded as), and it caught a real product defect: a derived source's `:inputs` is a list of property lists, which the Tape's JSON round trip rendered as one object with colliding `:id` keys, so a merge or split lost every input but the last. The Tape now owns a value codec for its META column that tags that shape, so the durable record carries both inputs' identities and original sources. That closes the "source relationships" gate. The set also found that a command or skill refused by the queue while a Run is busy lost the user's draft; the dispatcher gives it back, meeting the same nine criteria as plain input. Details, the ten-way mutation check and the commands are in `.scratch/m3-verification/REGRESSION-SET.md`.

The [closing rulings](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3-closing-rulings.md) are implemented (2026-09-15). The `chat-turn-recorder` plugin registers its two `run-sealing` observers with `:required t`, and a required recording failure seals an otherwise completed Run `audit-incomplete` through `run.failed`, leaving the Step ending and `response.completed` as recorded. `stage-error`, `:stage` and `superchat-stage.el` are `context-error`, `:site` and `superchat-event.el`. Injection and follow-up drops keep one event identity, so a drop whose append committed but lost its receipt is not recorded again, and an item whose drop is unconfirmed is never admitted or dispatched as input. Later Turns' Context View includes admitted material once, from the Tape; within a Run a later Step continues its provider prompt. A tool-protocol retry, and a provider failure on a later attempt, close that Step `failed`.

The user accepted the slice 2 smoke V baseline replacement on 2026-09-15 as a bounded exception after the Stage exit and the move of parsing and assembly: `chat-parser-direct: t` and `assembler-works-without-its-plugin: t` replace lines that read the retired Stage registry. `chat-parser-direct` checks only that `superchat-core-prepare-turn` exists, so chat parsing behaviour is proven by the M3 regression set's `m3/parse/` tests (`@model`, `/command`, `>skill`, parsing before queueing, unknown commands, non-chat surfaces, `#file`) and smoke cases S, T and W. The acceptance authorizes no other baseline change and relaxes no Tape event order, Step disposition, queueing, cancellation or Authority constraint.

The user accepted M3 on 2026-09-15, with the middleware interface narrowed by [closing ruling 5](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3-closing-rulings.md): no cancellation context is passed, and after cancellation Runtime's execution boundaries keep a middleware result from opening a Step or entering the model transport, without stopping the middleware's own work. That follow-up is fixed (2026-09-16): a submission whose request middleware cancels the Run ends through the existing cancellation path with the single `cancelled` terminal, no new failure terminal and no resend, because the send boundary reports an ordinary cancellation only for this Run's own cancellation with its execution Scope still live and its recorded transport provider still current; a withdrawn transport, a missing provider or a retired Scope still errors. M3a is next.
Acceptance: the complete canonical smoke set (every case in `scripts/smoke.el`, including R1 to R9) passes against the baselines in force when M3 starts with only the accepted insertion of `step.started` and `step.ended` facts and assertions for their identity, placement and disposition. Excluding only those two new types reproduces each old event sequence; the complete new sequence is also checked. Existing events retain their order and semantics (M1's `request.header` and `request.message-list` events remain part of the baseline); sending during a Run still queues; cancel still seals `run.cancelled`, and a Run with an entered external Effect still terminates `audit-incomplete` with the Effect open; a static check that a contract "passed" does not replace these behavioural assertions. Live evidence is kept as well: one normal turn and one cancelled turn against a real provider complete with the expected terminal facts, without demanding a fixed number of model events from a non-deterministic model.

### M3a — Remove Closure and Claims, park Experience (`done`)

Goal: superchat keeps only what DeepSeek Harness has a counterpart for, as [the M3a removal decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-15-m3a-remove-closure-and-claims.md) rules, without changing how a cancelled Run ends.

Removes: `superchat-closure.el`, the `closure` plugin row and Service, and the driver's `:inject` of `closure`; Runtime's terminal guard, shadow report and closure observation ring; the Tape's Closure role tags and opaque-row marking; `superchat-claims.el`; the Experience context collector with its prompt slot; the review of recovery needs, as `/review` and as `M-x superchat-review`.

Mechanism: the record's. Runtime tracks each Run's open Effects and Approvals and seals `audit-incomplete` when any is open; reopening a saved Session seals each cut-off Run `abandoned` without a judgment; subagent handoff, workflow resume and artifact delivery decide from the Run terminal recorded on the Tape.

Delivered (`current`, 2026-09-15), in four slices. Runtime tracks each Run's entered-but-unsettled Effects and requested-but-undecided Approvals by execution identity, reported by Authority before each fact is appended, and seals `audit-incomplete` when any of them is open, whatever ending was requested, while settled work keeps its ending and a late result still appends its one fact. Subagent handoff, workflow resume, cross-Run Artifact delivery and Experience evaluation decide from the Run terminal recorded on the Tape through the `tape` Service's `recorded-run-terminal`, which answers present, absent or unknown, never resolves two recorded terminals by picking one, and is refused rather than waited for when the record cannot be read. Reopening a Session repairs a cut-off Run without judging its record, inventing no tool result and no `step.ended`. Closure, Claims, the `experience` context collector and prompt slot, the `history` collector's `:experience-view` copy and `/review` in both forms are gone; Experience keeps its recovery view, Authority's gate over it, `M-x superchat-experience-monitor`, evaluation and its projection, and old `claim.recorded` and `feedback.recorded` rows stay readable. The current documents describe this.

Verification: the M3 regression set's 31 `m3a/` tests, written before the implementation and each shown to fail at the gap it named, all pass; the whole set is 121 tests with one expected failure, the M3 middleware follow-up. The complete canonical smoke set passes, with one bounded baseline change the user approved for this removal: case V's `collectors-mounted`, `commands-remounted` and `collectors-after-remount` counts drop by one each, because the collector and the command they counted are gone; it authorises nothing else. The Kernel check passes, the package loads with no Closure or Claims feature, byte-compiling every changed module adds no warning, and against a real provider (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`) one normal turn and one cancelled turn end with the expected terminal facts. Experience's real recovery assembler, not a stub, is called once after a tool failure, returns no Candidate without Claim rows, signals nothing, and leaves the dependent tool unblocked (`.scratch/m3-verification/evidence-m3a-final.txt`).

Acceptance:

- A Run cancelled while a tool Effect is entered and unsettled seals `audit-incomplete` at once with the Effect open, and a genuine late result appends its one fact without reopening the Run. The same holds for an Approval requested and undecided. A Run whose Effects and Approvals are all settled seals with its requested status.
- Reopening a Session whose Run was cut off writes one `abandoned` pair for that Run, nothing while a Run in the Session is live, and never a synthetic tool result or `step.ended`.
- Subagent handoff, workflow resume and artifact delivery give the answers in the record's consumer table.
- No loaded file refers to Closure, Claims or the removed Experience entries. The default set without `closure` loads, and one chat turn completes.
- The complete canonical smoke set passes. Its R1, R5, R7 and R9 assertions that called `superchat-closure-judge` are rewritten against recorded facts, and any baseline file change is accepted by the user explicitly.
- The M3 regression set's M3a tests are written by the leader before implementation starts, and they pass.
- One normal turn and one cancelled turn against a real provider (LM Studio) end with the expected terminal facts.

### M4 — Surfaces as plugins, composition by profile (`done`)

Goal: the chat window, inline ask and rewrite, Org Babel, Magit and emacsclient are plugins that a profile row list assembles.

The user approved this text on 2026-09-16, and the mechanism with it: [the M4 record](decisions/implemented/2026-09-16-m4-surfaces-as-plugins.md) fixes that a profile is layers over the default rows with an explicit disable, that `superchat-plugins` stays as the default profile's layer, that a row left pending is reported with the Services it waits for, and that a surface entry point refuses when its row is not mounted, because an Emacs autoload cannot be withdrawn.

State (2026-09-16): delivered in three slices. A profile is a named list of layers over the `default` profile's rows, which are still `superchat-plugins`; a layer inherits with `(:inherit NAME)` and disables with `(NAME . :disabled)`, `(require 'superchat)` mounts `superchat-profile`, and mounting a profile makes the composition exactly its rows. `superchat-profile-report` names any row left pending with the Services it waits for. The two empty seats are gone and all five surfaces are rows: `chat`, `inline-ask`, `inline-rewrite`, `org-babel`, `magit` and `emacsclient`, each registering its own surface name, so the core seed keeps only `workflow`, `subagent`, `compaction`, `skills` and `external`. The chat row is the one that owns registration without owning loading: the chat code lives in `superchat.el`, which eager `require' pulls in, so the row owns the `chat` surface name and twelve entry points ask whether it is mounted before they open a View, build an envelope or reach the driver. An entry point whose row is not mounted refuses, naming the row and the active profile, and submits nothing; emacsclient returns that as a JSON error. Unmounting a row never touches work already running: a Run in flight keeps its Sink and reaches its terminal, because the chat Sink is built into the envelope rather than resolved from the row. The shipped `headless` profile disables every user-interface row and Telega, keeps `emacsclient`, and answers a prompt end to end; switching back to `default` restores the chat window, which can be used again. The default profile is 29 rows and the default experience is unchanged. Every one of the leader's `m4/` tests passes, with no known failure left in the regression set.

Verification: the M3 regression set's `m4/` tests, written before the implementation and each shown to fail at the gap it named, all pass, and the whole set had no expected failure left for the first time since M3 opened. Two mutants pin the chat row specifically: with the entry guard removed the front-door refusal test fails, and with the row mounted but not registering its name the ownership test fails, each biting exactly one test. The complete canonical smoke set passes with its baselines untouched (`SMOKE OK (0 mismatches)`), the Kernel check passes, the package loads with every row active and `chat` registered by its row, and byte-compiling the four changed modules produces warnings identical to the baseline's. Against a real provider (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`) the two real-model M3 turns and every M4 test pass, including the front door opening and answering. End to end, with the row unmounted all eighteen chat entry points refuse without creating a View, a Run or a provider request, while pause, resume, cancel and the read-only inspector commands still work; a Run already in flight survives both unmounting the row and switching to `headless`, keeps rendering into its window and reaches `completed` with no `run.cancelled`.

Two follow-ups found at M4's acceptance were fixed after it closed, each registered as its own issue first. The `view` Service's `sink-for` operation had no caller and is deleted; the chat Sink is built into the envelope, which is why an in-flight Run keeps rendering after its row leaves. And a chat Turn's attachment fact, which a seal-time row used to write, is now written when Runtime admits the request ([the record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-16-chat-attachments-recorded-at-admission.md)), so an image can no longer reach the model with nothing recording that it did. A third, that inline answers failed when `chat-view` was not in the profile because they reached its `markdown-to-org` through a Service while testing for the function, is fixed by making rendering a plain function ([the record](decisions/accepted/2026-09-16-rendering-is-a-function-not-a-service.md)): inline calls it directly as every other caller already did, the `view` Service has no consumers left and is deleted, and the `chat-view` row, which provided nothing else, goes with it. The default profile is 29 rows.

Removes: nothing from the default set. `superchat-plugins` stays until a profile mechanism exists and the default profile reproduces it exactly.

Acceptance: removing any row of the default profile leaves the rest loading; a `headless` profile answers one prompt with no UI plugin mounted.

### M5 — Operating context and reference reading (`done` for the accepted scopes; slices 3–4 accepted as mechanisms and operating workflow, utility not established)

Slices 3–4 closure (`accepted`, 2026-09-20): the user stopped further utility testing and accepted the implemented mechanisms and operating workflow, retaining their current defaults. The [closure report](reports/2026-09-20-m5-slices-3-4-closure.md) preserves the incomplete comparison and observed flaws. Slice 3 has three finished attempts (two declared valid and reviewed as failures despite passing state checks; one undeclared/unreviewed); slice 4 has no independent utility runs. This is not evidence of model-performance benefit, nor a waiver of product permission, approval, feedback or recording contracts. The earlier [bounded comparison plan](decisions/accepted/2026-09-20-m5-slices-3-4-utility-closure.md) is historical, not a remaining work queue.

Goal: the model is told where it actually is and what it can already do, before any new capability is built. The user reframed the tool-surface question on 2026-09-16 after reading how Omarchy ships operating knowledge to each agent's native skill entry and how yayster.el gives Emacs the same treatment: what a model needs first is not more verbs but a map of the machine it is standing on. The direction is in [the AI-layer tool-surface proposal](decisions/proposed/2026-09-16-emacs-ai-layer-tool-surface.md) (`proposed`); the mechanism is in [the operating-context contract](decisions/proposed/2026-09-16-emacs-operating-context-and-reference-contract.md), whose first two slices the user accepted the same day while the last two wait.

The contract's load-bearing finding is that superchat has no carrier for reference material. `superchat-skills-load` returns a Preset: invoking a skill applies its model, tools and pre-hook, and an agent or plan skill also switches the View's Capability and composition for the rest of the session. A skill is a mode switch, not something a model may read to inform one thought, so reference reading needs its own path that applies no Preset. Measured gaps behind the four slices: the default request offered no way to read what a symbol does; the chat entry carried no facts about the user's Emacs at all, while `superchat-act` carried them by appending an `[Emacs origin]` block to the user's own input text; and the skills directory is seeded once, so a guide shipped in a later version never reaches a user who already has any skill file.

State (`current`, 2026-09-17): slices 1 and 2 are delivered. `describe-function` joins the default tool surface next to `apropos`, so the discovery loop — find a symbol, read what it does, then act — is whole without falling back to the approval-gated `eval_elisp`. Approval is unchanged: the name was already on the user-owned unprompted read list, which is why documentation may be read without a prompt while `describe-variable`, which returns a live value, still asks. The second hand-written copy of those names in the tool-selection fallback is updated with the constant and now says it must be. Slice 2 supplies one frozen source snapshot to chat and act through the shared collector. Queued requests, renamed or dead sources and edited inspection previews retain their stated identity and text. Slices 3 and 4 — bundled reference data with a reading entry point, and the operating guide itself — stay `proposed`: the task comparison does not yet establish a benefit for either.

The reviewed comparison (`current`) gives strong task evidence for origin context: T1 succeeds 0/3 in A and 3/3 in both B and C. It does not isolate the benefit of documentation discovery, and it does not test on-demand reference reading. Product wait-clock behavior, tool budgets, model execution mistakes and an incomplete answer extractor confound broader claims; neither “knowledge is irrelevant” nor “the guide is useless” follows. The complete-answer extractor checks Runtime body identity against Tape byte size and SHA-256. The raw 27 trials remain unchanged; the corrected review is in `.scratch/abc-comparison/runs/auto-20260917-050939/REVIEW-20260917.md`.

Execution reliability (`current`): per-attempt progress clocks, explicit compatible-provider reasoning mapping, and recorded tool-budget finalization follow the [implemented decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-model-attempt-progress-and-budget-finalization.md). Their focused regression is `scripts/m5-execution-regression.el`. A final answer or a correctly closed Run does not itself meet the persistent-key-binding acceptance below; real task postconditions remain a separate check. The initial isolated task findings and observation limits are in `.scratch/m5-execution/LEADER-HANDOFF.md`.

Missing final responses (`current`): the [native callback decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-missing-provider-response.md) sends absent answer material through the existing failed path. Explicit empty strings, current-attempt visible text, tool continuation, pending steering and budget metadata keep their stated behavior; a prior tool preamble cannot stand in for a missing follow-up answer. `scripts/m5-response-regression.el` tests the real adapter and Runtime. This corrects recorded execution truth, not the model's configuration-task performance.

Tool feedback (`current`): the [existing-tool decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-emacs-tool-feedback.md) covers unique edits, no-change feedback, complete eval expressions, pre-reader effect classification and recognized evaluation errors. [Bounded diagnostic context](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-bounded-tool-error-context.md) keeps a single compact error body instead of repeating large returned objects in corrective messages. `scripts/m5-tool-feedback-regression.el` verifies these contracts through direct observations and the real wrapped tool loop. A repaired-baseline T2 trial has the requested binding both live and after an independent restart; model assertions and independently observed results are kept separate. The remaining complete configuration acceptance, including both entry paths and undo, is tracked in `.scratch/m5-tool-feedback/LEADER-HANDOFF.md`; M5 stays in progress until that acceptance is met.

Prepared request context (`current`): [act and inspected sends](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-prepared-chat-request-context.md) keep the raw system configuration separate from its preview, reuse collected context, and submit the exact final body under the current budget. Deleted attachments and source selections are not restored; since 2026-09-25 earlier turns are not part of the editable body and go with an edited body as with an unedited one. `scripts/m5-request-context-regression.el` verifies the provider-facing text and the history selection sent.

System contributions (`current`): [ordered system contributions](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-ordered-system-contributions.md) carry identity from assembly through the Request to every attempt, so a tool rewrite refreshes a dynamic block in place and never edits an explicit base or static block whose text happens to match; a middleware that replaces the whole `:system` string owns it from then on. `scripts/m5-guidance-regression.el` verifies the provider-visible system text per attempt. The default chat and act configurations do not exercise the collision; custom collectors and non-default surfaces with renderers did.

Original conversation material (`current`): [the Tape write-boundary repair](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-preserve-conversation-material.md) preserves full user intent and assistant response text, including empty strings. Previously the display-summary limit discarded their tails before the next Run or Context View could read them. Structural summaries and payload controls are unchanged; old clipped rows are not reconstructed. `scripts/m5-history-material-regression.el` verifies actual second-Run input, SQLite reopen, whole-item budgets and inspected deletion. Buffer listing now quotes exact names, and retryable feedback explicitly returns to the original task after correcting a failed call.

The full configuration trials on the preceding source completed all six Runs but did not pass either entry's complete apply/undo acceptance; the candidate operating note also failed its gate. Their frozen results are in `.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/RESULTS-20260917.md`. Those trials exercised batch finalization at 12 rounds. The final 5291b745 B trials then exercised one normal extension per Run under the unchanged deadlines: chat did not establish the binding, while act established it live and after restart but also modified the forbidden source file. Act undo restored the original observed state. All four Runs completed without timeout, but neither entry passed the full task. Actual undo HTTP carried the preceding full answer in both paths. Results are in `.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/FINAL-INTERACTIVE-RESULTS.md`; this source predates the missing-response terminal repair. The bounded check of the unchanged 1024-byte note on the same 5291b745 source and interactive conditions passes every automatic chat apply/undo state check, but the complete answers still contain verifiable API/file-state errors. Under its pre-registered gate, no C/act follows and the note is not promoted into the product. This is evidence of useful task behavior in one scenario, not a stable success rate or a reference-reader decision. The [development handoff](reports/2026-09-17-m5-execution-handoff.md) records final code verification, source-separated real results and the remaining task/contract boundaries.

Its discovery and origin tests live in `scripts/m5-regression.el`, the second leader-owned test machine: the user ruled on 2026-09-16 that new tests are not added to `scripts/m3-regression.el` any more. The set builds its own fixture on the canonical smoke helpers and is scripted-only, so it skips under `SMOKE_REAL`; real-model evidence belongs to each slice's own end-to-end run.

Verification (slice 1): the M5 set's three discovery tests pass — the default request offers the tool, a model scripted to call it gets a known function's real documentation back with nothing asked, and a user who set `prompt-for-all-reads` is still asked — with a fourth test guarding that a request naming its own tools is not widened. Mutants M57 to M60 each turn their target red. The M3 set's 139 tests, the canonical smoke set with its baselines untouched, and the Kernel check all pass; byte-compile warnings are identical to the baseline; and against a real provider (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`) the M3 live turns and every M4 test still pass, so the default experience is unchanged.

Removes: nothing. No capability leaves the default set, no user configuration is rewritten, and an explicit tool list, a stricter approval policy or a narrowed Preset keeps its meaning — an upgrade never appends to a list the user set.

Closure (2026-09-19): the acceptance below is met, within stated limits. Under M7's division of labour — the model edits the buffer and makes the change live, the user reviews the diff and saves, an independent checker restarts — the configuration task with its undo passed from the chat window (`m7-attended-chat-04-deepseek`) and from the current buffer (`m7-attended-act-06-deepseek`), all eleven checks in both phases of both entries, with `deepseek-chat`, a person answering every approval and both save reviews, and the leader's review of both final answers ([closure report](reports/2026-09-19-m5-acceptance-closure.md)). **Guaranteed:** one complete pass per entry, each on its own recorded product commit (chat on `7e368f5b`, act on `9c0c3c3e`; the chat entry was not re-run on the final commit), with the recorded model configuration and review procedure. **Not guaranteed:** a success rate — the six act runs are run history across different products, harness versions and human save decisions, not a sample from which a rate can be estimated; other models — the local 27B model has two valid failures on the chat entry and is not a configuration this acceptance passed with; the model's recovery after a refused save, which no run exercised once the contradictory tool description was gone. Slices 3 and 4 (a reference-reading carrier and an operating guide) stay `proposed`: the accepted scope did not need them to pass, which is evidence about this task and this model, not a decision against them. The observation that a reply announcing the next step with no tool call ends a Run (twice in six act runs with this model, raw responses not recoverable) stays open as an observation and is not claimed fixed.

Delivery record (historical; current acceptance scope is above). Slice 4, minimal form (2026-09-20): by the user's choice — content before carrier, on by default, no attended verification for now — requests that offer `eval_elisp` or `edit_buffer` carry [a short Emacs operating note](decisions/implemented/2026-09-20-emacs-operating-note.md) in their system text, written from the mistakes the attended runs of 2026-09-18 to 20 showed repeatedly (a keymap variable that does not exist, keys not written with `kbd`, reading `lookup-key`'s number as unbound, `load` of a file whose buffer holds the change, several expressions in one `eval_elisp`, claims about a restart nobody made) and consistent with buffer-first mutation; the 2026-09-17 candidate note could not be reused because it told the model to save. One more block in the existing `system-instructions` collector, rendered against the final tool surface; a user option turns it off or replaces the text; the only canonical smoke delta is one line each in `A.txt` and `A.full.txt`. **Not verified:** any effect on a real model — the DeepSeek acceptance passed on a product without this note, and whether the note helps or hurts it was not run. Slice 3: **mechanism delivered, utility unverified** (2026-09-20). The contract was rewritten on 2026-09-20 for permission levels and buffer-first mutation as [reference reading on demand](decisions/accepted/2026-09-20-reference-reading-on-demand.md): a short index, a read-only reference tool, pointer sentences on identified tool errors, a size cap on resident text, the standing of reference text below product permissions and user instructions, and two separately recorded acceptances (mechanism, and real-model utility). The user ruled the four open choices the same day (an Elisp data module; inline-ask left out of this round; the resident note kept unchanged during the comparison, after its two imprudent sentences were corrected; a capped real-model comparison over three task classes whose wording of results is fixed in advance). Tool lazy-loading and a general lazy-loading framework are explicitly not started — no bottleneck has been observed. Delivered the same day: the data module `superchat-reference.el` with its first page `emacs-keybindings`, the built-in tool `read_reference` on the default surface (ids only, no file access, no level asks), the index block rendered against the final tool surface, and one pointer sentence in the existing tool-failure feedback for the errors a page claims; `scripts/reference-regression.el` (13 tests, ten mutants red); canonical smoke delta one line each in `A.txt` and `A.full.txt`. A pre-existing defect surfaced and was fixed with it: the current-buffer entry treated "tools not decided yet" as the built-in default rather than the user's tool list, so the operating note (and the new index) could appear without their tools. At mechanism delivery no real model had run with these additions. The subsequent partial comparison and user-directed stop are recorded in the closure report above; benefit remains unestablished.

Acceptance: one task proves the product end, given in the contract — add a named key binding for the current major mode, usable at once, still there after a restart, and undoable — reached from both the chat window and the current buffer. Whether slices 3 and 4 happen at all is decided by a three-arm comparison on the same model and the same starting Emacs: A today's default, B discovery plus accurate context, C that plus a minimal operating note, over a temporary buffer setting, a persistent key binding and a diagnosis of a binding that does not fire, with approval waiting timed separately so an improvement can be attributed to knowledge, tools or round trips rather than to all three at once.

### M6 — Permission levels replace Authority (`implemented`, 2026-09-18)

Goal: the user stops tending a decision machine. [The accepted contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-17-permissions-the-dsh-way.md) replaces Authority's Effect classification, policy layers, approval hashes and unprompted-read lists with DeepSeek Harness's shape: one permission level per Session (`workspace-write` by default, `danger-full-access`, `read-only`), target checks inside the tools that know their target, a one-shot escalation after a denial, and an `ask`/`never` approval policy. `eval_elisp`, shell and `define_tool` are full Emacs control: asked per call under `workspace-write`, never confined by a directory rule. Four slices; the first three land behind the internal switch `superchat--permission-levels-enabled` and do not govern production Sessions, the fourth migrates the default tools, telega, MCP and the call sites, enables the levels and deletes Authority and the switch.

State (`current`): delivered; commits `6bcfc649` … `3100c947` plus the approval-interface fix. Every Session runs at a permission level — sandbox mode `workspace-write` (default) | `read-only` | `danger-full-access`, approval policy `ask` | `never` — selected with `/permissions` and recorded as `permission.level`, `sandbox.mode` and `approval.policy` Session events. `superchat-sandbox.el` decides: built-in tools run, and the ones that write to an explicit target check it where the write happens and offer a one-shot escalation; `eval_elisp`, shell tools, `define_tool` and agent-written tools are asked about per call, refused under `read-only`, free under `danger-full-access`; registered and MCP tools are asked about unless their server is in `superchat-mcp-trusted-servers`, and refused under `read-only`; the stronger of a `tools/pre-execute` listener's answer and that rule stands. `superchat-approval.el` asks: the `approval/request` waterfall ends in the human answerer, an approval covers a deep snapshot of the arguments, and `?` in the default answerer shows the tool, the reason and the whole arguments through `read-multiple-choice`'s own help. `superchat-authority.el` is only the execution pipeline. A Session whose approval policy is `never` — `danger-full-access` by default and every delegated child — asks nobody and is therefore not offered `ask_user`. `/reset` keeps the sandbox mode and approval policy but still unbinds the Workspace, so the next request's workspace root may change. A Preset's `confirm_destructive` is parsed for compatibility and has no effect. Verified by `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (79 tests), the unchanged other suites, canonical smoke, and a real-model permission acceptance on qwen3.8-27b from both entries (`.scratch/m6-permissions/acceptance/RESULTS.md`, run on `e5b37b76`; the deletion after it is carried by the regressions). Not verified: the approval question typed by a person in an interactive Emacs. Tracked separately and not part of M6: T2 completing inside the 600 s deadline on a local model, and the missing tool for reading a file no buffer visits (`.scratch/m6-permissions/issues/01-…`; `read_file` is next). Not built: `rename_file`, `delete_file`; renaming `superchat-authority.el`.

Delivery history (2026-09-18, four slices; slices 1–3 and slice 4's first steps landed behind an internal cutover switch that slice 4 turned on and then deleted, so every "behind the switch" and "switch off" statement below describes a state that no longer exists). Slice 1 was delivered first. `superchat-approval.el` is the one-shot approval seam — the `approval/request` waterfall, `never` rejecting before any answerer, four closed outcomes of which only `allowed-once` grants, the paired Run-scoped `approval.requested` / `approval.decided` audit, and Runtime's open-approval mark spanning the decision. An approval is for a deep snapshot of the arguments taken on entry, which `superchat-approval-request-snapshot` returns for the caller to execute; answerers get a separate copy. Every answerer entry settles the same way: an answerer's error is a closed `unavailable`, a quit from a listener or from the last-resort answerer is recorded as the user's quit and propagates, anyone asked renews the transport wait, and the mark is cleared only after the decision is on the Tape. `superchat-sandbox.el` owns the two knobs, the level table and the per-call policy resolution; `permission.level`, `sandbox.mode` and `approval.policy` are Session configuration events written only when a value changes and read back from the Tape on reopen. `/permissions` is registered only when the switch is on. Nothing in the tool pipeline consults the seam yet: with the switch off every existing suite, the canonical smoke baselines and the byte-compile warning set are unchanged. Slice 2 is delivered behind the same switch. `superchat-sandbox.el` decides a direct write before it happens: a buffer target resolves an indirect buffer to its base, a file path is canonicalized through its deepest existing ancestor and contained under the workspace root or the temporary directory, a remote path and Superchat's own buffers and data directory are outside every workspace, and `read-only` denies before `append_to_buffer` creates anything. `edit_buffer`, `append_to_buffer` and `replace_buffer` consult it only when they are about to change text; `save_buffer` saves the whole file of the base buffer and never asks for a file name; `write_file` refuses a file some buffer visits and names that buffer. A denial returns dsh's two marker lines; the retry with `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` validates the pairing, fails a request that does not strictly widen without asking anyone, and maps the four approval outcomes to their texts, the grant covering that one call. A symbolic link counts as existing even when its target does not, so a dangling link inside the workspace is judged by where it points, and the canonical `superchat-data-directory` is excluded even when the workspace root contains it. After a grant everything is redone from the approval seam's argument snapshot: the target is resolved again, a file that became visited or changed on disk while the question was open ends the call without writing, and the approved arguments are what is written. Successful writes report the resolved target, the layer reached and the checks made. The escalation arguments, their teaching and the two new tools enter the tool schemas only when the switch is on, and `superchat-llm.el` classifies the denial as an error and, in a round that holds one, gives every failed call its own instruction — the sanctioned retry for the denial, do-not-retry for a rejected, cancelled or unanswerable escalation, correct-and-retry for the rest; the new classification is recognized by whole sentences and only with the switch on. With the switch off the three tools return their previous strings byte for byte. `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (44 tests) verifies both slices; the slice 2 tests call the tool functions under a bound tool context, so the path through the real tool pipeline is evidence for slice 3. Slice 3 is delivered behind the same switch. In `superchat-tool-execute` the ordinary decision is the level stage's when the switch is on, with blocked calls and trusted human input keeping their own answers: `superchat-permission-level-decision` runs the `tools/pre-execute` waterfall and then the level rule as a fixed stage, the stronger of `deny`, `ask` and `allow` standing, so a listener that allows without delegating cannot spare a call its question. Built-in tools are allowed and check their own targets; `eval_elisp`, the shell tools, `define_tool` and every agent-written tool are full Emacs control — asked per call under `workspace-write`, denied under `read-only`, free under `danger-full-access`; an MCP or third-party tool is asked about unless its server is in `superchat-mcp-trusted-servers`, and denied under `read-only` either way. An `ask` goes to the approval seam, which owns the open mark and the audit pair; the executor keeps the quit path, the single refusal delivery, and runs the body from the approved argument snapshot. A Run cancelled while the question is open records the late answer and starts nothing. `define_tool`'s two in-body confirmations fall silent under levels, `workspace_write` checks the Artifact's buffer or file, a delegated child Session starts with its parent's sandbox mode and `never`, and a hard `continuation` fragment states the current policy in every request that offers tools. A review of the slice closed five gaps: an escalation granted from inside a tool body after its Run was cancelled writes nothing, that question uses the Run's borrowed Scope like the executor's own, `workspace_write` judges the fallback buffer it will actually reuse and only a file-less one is scratch, MCP trust compares the exact server recorded at registration, and a request middleware restates the policy before every provider request so a tool continuation states the level in force; `tools/pre-execute` answers with `:kind` as the contract says, and a child's inherited values are pinned. `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` (78 tests after step 3 removed the switch-off tests) covers the slices; its executor, context and review tests send scripted tool calls through the real pipeline with the switch on, while tool classes, `define_tool`'s confirmations, child seeding and the `workspace_write` target are direct function tests. Slice 4 runs in steps. Step 1a is delivered behind the switch: the approval chain ends in the human answerer, so a delegating listener reads the person's decision from its continuation; the default answerer is `superchat-approval-confirm`, whose `?` shows the arguments and returns to the question, and which counts as nobody in a batch Emacs; with levels on the executor calls no effect resolver, base decision, approval hash or argument rewriter and its Tape rows drop those keys; `tools/execute` wraps the whole invocation of a live call, body at most once, a short-circuited call recorded as blocked and never as started; `tools/post-execute` replaces or blocks a result; a registered tool needs no `:effects` and is selectable because it is installed; and a Session-owned buffer renamed to a Workspace's fallback name stays Superchat's own state. Step 1b is delivered behind the switch: each Telega request is submitted under a Scope of its own holding a `tools/pre-execute` listener that refuses every tool outside that request's list and, where the request has one, an `approval/request` answerer — a digest allows a read of a chat it was granted and never widens that set, a cowork Session delegates an ungranted read to the person and grants the chat only after their yes, the reply and triage answerers render their preview before delegating. The Scope waits with a queued request and is disposed when the Run reaches any terminal state, when submission fails, and when the queued request is dropped, which Runtime now announces as `queue-dropped` on the request's Scope from both drop paths. Under `read-only` every Telega tool is denied. Step 2 is delivered: the switch is on by default, `save_buffer` and `write_file` are on the default surface, README / README_cn carry the permissions chapter and the CHANGELOG states the changed default. Evidence that moved, and nothing else: canonical smoke `A.txt` / `A.full.txt` gained exactly the runtime-context block (its temporary workspace path recorded as `<smoke-workspace>/`), `V.txt` counts one more collector and one more command, case L denies through `tools/pre-execute`, architecture smoke R7 now asserts that an early `allow` cannot skip the level stage's question and that a listener's `deny` is loosened neither by `danger-full-access` nor by escalation arguments, and its revocation and native-callback blocks assert behaviour instead of resolver bookkeeping; the smoke fixture, seven `m5-tool-feedback` tests, three `m5/discovery` tests and two M3 seal tests bind `superchat-approval-function` (the stricter-read test now says so with a `tools/pre-execute` listener answering `ask`). Smoke V caught a defect on the way: a built-in tool stayed selectable with its plugin unmounted, so built-in descriptors are now installed by the `tools-builtin` plugin and leave with it. Rollback is a full restart on the commit before the cutover (`58d54615`), not a runtime setting. Step 2's real-model permission acceptance passed on qwen3.8-27b from both entries (chat and act): in-workspace edits and saves asked nobody, every `eval_elisp` call was asked about exactly once, and a write outside the workspace was denied first with nothing created, retried by the model with the escalation arguments and a reason, asked about once and written only after the yes; T2's completion inside the 600 s deadline was not achieved on this model and is judged separately, and the missing tool for reading an unvisited file is registered as its own issue. Step 3 is delivered: Authority's decision machinery and the cutover switch are deleted. `superchat-authority.el` went from 2308 lines to about 1200 and holds only the execution pipeline — identity and liveness, the block check (untrusted or malformed `ask_user`, a request-local block, the Experience recovery guard, none of which a level can skip), the level decision, the hand-off to the approval seam, the `tools/execute` and `tools/post-execute` waterfalls, settlement and the Tape rows, which no longer carry effects, policy, attendance or an approval hash. Gone with it: effect resolvers and the `eval_elisp` read-only classifier, the built-in and named policies, the approval hash and preview registry, `superchat-authority-confirm` with its session allowlist and the approval seam's fallback rung, the four Authority options, `superchat-authority-mode` and its mode-line segment, `/authority` with the `authority.policy.selected` projection (old rows stay as inert history), the Request keys `:authority-policy`, `:attendance` and `:approval-function` (a Session that never asks anyone is `approval.policy never`, which is also what now withholds `ask_user`), the agent loop's destructive-tool and synthesized-tool confirmations, `superchat-mcp-server-effects`, the Telega plugin's policies, effect declarations and request-local approval function, the Events `tool-permission`, `tool-args-rewrite` and `tool-result-filter`, and the `authority` Service and plugin (its one surviving method, `transport-timer-owner`, moved to the `tools` Service). Renamed: `superchat-runtime--note-open-work`, the seal error text, the Invariant `tool-execution-passes-the-pipeline`. `/reset` leaves the permission level alone. Evidence that moved, and nothing else: smoke `V.txt` (one command fewer, the Invariant's name), `U.txt` (the two Telega policy traces are gone, 13 to 11); architecture smoke asserts a surviving installation by calling its body instead of comparing its resolver and preview; 13 M3 fixture lines drop `:effects`, 4 drop `:attendance`, three M3 tests lose `authority` from their names (the M38 mutant follows), and `m5-tool-feedback` loses the test of the deleted classifier. `docs/components/superchat-tool-execution.md` is rewritten as the pipeline's reference, `superchat-tools.md`, `superchat-session.md` and the `CONTEXT.md` glossary entry follow; the historical turn pipeline specification keeps its text and gained a successor link; the current navigation and contracts (`AGENTS.md`, `architecture.md`, the request lifecycle, the composition contract, the plugin guide, the development guide) describe the permission levels and no longer the deleted interfaces. Renaming the file itself is left as a separate change; `rename_file` and `delete_file` are not built.

Removed (slice 4, step 3): `superchat-authority.el`'s decision machinery, `/authority`, `superchat-authority-unprompted-read-tools`, `-prompt-for-all-reads`, `-sensitive-buffers`, `superchat-authority-mode`, `superchat-mcp-server-effects`, `:authority-policy` and `:attendance` on the Request. The default experience changes on purpose: edits and saves inside the workspace stop asking.

### `read_file` — reading a file no buffer visits (`implemented`, 2026-09-18)

Goal: close the gap M6's acceptance exposed — no built-in tool could read a file that no buffer visits, so the model needed `eval_elisp` (asked about every call) just to look at `~/.emacs.d/init.el`. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-18-read-file.md) adds `read_file`: the disk version of a local regular text file, read through a temporary buffer with no visiting buffer and none of what opening a file triggers; `offset` / `limit` windows with the three DeepSeek Harness caps (2000 lines, 2000 characters per line, 50 KiB), a header that never lets a partial result pass for the whole file, text without line numbers, and errors for directories, devices, FIFOs, binary files, remote paths and files over 20 MiB. Reading is a product choice stated in the contract: the same under all three levels, no approval asked by the tool, no sensitive-path list — the operating system decides what can be read, and what is read goes to the model provider; `tools/pre-execute` can still tighten it. It is on the default surface and an explicit tool list is not widened.

State (`current`): the tool and `scripts/read-file-regression.el` (13 tests, all red before the tool existed, six mutants red) are delivered; every other suite, canonical smoke, Kernel check and the compile-warning set are unchanged. The real-model re-check passed on qwen3.8-27b from both entries: the two scenarios that had burned their whole 600 s because nothing could read the file (append a line to the init file without `eval_elisp`) now complete in under three minutes — `read_file` returns the disk content with no buffer left visiting the file, and the write that follows is denied first, escalated with a reason, asked about once and made only after the yes, keeping the original lines byte for byte (`.scratch/m6-permissions/acceptance/RESULTS.md`, which also records an acceptance-harness incident and its fix: an unguarded auto-yes answerer approved a model's write to the user's real init file; the file was restored and the answerer now approves only paths inside the fixture). T2's completion time stays a separate question.

### M7 — Buffer-first mutation (`implemented`, 2026-09-19; module rename delivered 2026-09-20)

Goal: the model's ordinary write capability ends at the Emacs Buffer. It may read a file, open a file as a buffer and edit buffers; writing a buffer back to disk belongs to the user and their Emacs environment, and `eval_elisp` stays the explicit, always-asked escape hatch. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-18-buffer-first-mutation.md) adds `open_file` and an outlet for unsaved changes (a per-Run list and `/changes`), retires `write_file`, takes `save_buffer` off the default surface as an always-asked narrow tool, and removes M6's target checks and one-shot escalation for writes; the workspace root stops being a write boundary. It deliberately departs from DeepSeek Harness, whose host has no Buffer object. Trigger: the attended M5 acceptance of 2026-09-18, where the model edited and saved a protected source file without anyone being asked.

State (`current`, 2026-09-18): slice 1 is delivered and only adds. `open_file` visits a file as a buffer without taking focus or touching the disk, returns an already open buffer without reverting it, gives an empty buffer for a path that does not exist, and refuses remote paths, directories, Superchat's data directory and files over `large-file-warning-threshold`; every level allows it and nobody is asked. The unsaved-changes outlet is `superchat-changes.el`: a structured buffer edit hands its resolved target to the executor, which writes `:buffer` and `:buffer-file` on that call's `tool.completed` fact (the Tape did not record tool arguments before), and candidates are those targets — a Session's, or one Run's together with the Runs it delegated to — intersected with buffers live and modified now; a buffer only `eval_elisp` changed is not listed, by design. `/changes` lists them and `superchat-changes-diff` / `-save` / `-revert` are the user's operations. The Run-end reminder is non-durable by the user's ruling: an echo-area message that is not on the Tape and need not stay in the chat history, with `/changes` recomputing the same derived list at any time. The target fields are execution evidence: `:buffer` names the base buffer that really changed (an indirect buffer records its base), and `:buffer-file` is the file observed at that moment and only locates the buffer — the list reads the live buffer's name, file and modified mark as they are now, so a later rename or `set-visited-file-name` never makes an old path pass for the current one. `scripts/m7-buffer-first-regression.el` (9 tests, all red first, four mutants red, the lookup-order case red on the first version); the other suites unchanged; canonical smoke `V.txt` counts one more command. Slice 2 is delivered and removes, in one step, everything that made permissions a question of where files may be written: `write_file` is gone; the `sandbox_permissions` / `justification` arguments, the denial markers that taught a retry, and the one-shot escalation approval are gone; the writable-roots and target-containment checks are gone, so the workspace root is no longer a write boundary. What remains is one small rule in `superchat-sandbox-buffer-edit-refusal`: an ordinary buffer is editable wherever its file lives, Superchat's own buffers never are, and `read-only` refuses every edit — finally, with nothing to retry. `save_buffer` left the default surface (eight built-in names now; no structured tool among them saves, while `eval_elisp`, asked about on every call, can still persist once the user approves it); the level stage answers `ask` for it at every level but `read-only`, so under the `never` policy it is refused even at `danger-full-access`. The default level is `edit-buffers`; `workspace-write` is canonicalized on input, in `/permissions` (with a notice) and when an old Tape row is read, and is never written. The runtime-context block the model reads now says that it changes things by editing buffers and that saving is the user's — the only canonical smoke delta (`A.txt`, `A.full.txt`). Seventeen M6 tests that asserted the deleted machinery were removed with it and six M7 tests took their place (`scripts/m7-buffer-first-regression.el`, 15 tests; four mutants red: `save_buffer` unasked at the widest level, internal buffers editable, `read-only` editing, the former name unrecognised). Slice 3's harness is ready and the attended run itself is not done: the M5 acceptance harness (`.scratch/m5-config-acceptance/`) now follows the contract's division of labour — the scripted model opens the init file as a buffer, edits it and makes it live, never saving; the reviewer is shown the file-against-buffer diff of each candidate the product's own `/changes` derivation yields and saves through `superchat-changes-save`; the restart check runs after that; a new check `model_did_not_persist` fails an entry whose model saved through an approved `eval_elisp`. With zero model calls: 22 of 22 scripted scenarios behave as expected on both entries (positives pass; ten counterexamples, including the new `model-saves` and `reviewer-declines`, are each rejected for their own reason; the buffer tools ask nobody and only `eval_elisp` is asked about), and an interactive rehearsal with a pseudo-terminal standing in for the person passes on both entries, with a declined save recorded as not complete. The real-model attended T2 has been run once on the chat entry with the user present (`m7-attended-chat-02`, 2026-09-19, on the product with the approval-wait fix) and did **not** pass: both Runs completed well inside 600 s of model-and-tool time, the model followed the contract's path (`read_file`, `open_file`, `edit_buffer`, then `eval_elisp` to make it live), never persisted anything and never touched the source file, every live check passed, and the user reviewed and saved — but the first deviation was the model's `edit_buffer` call itself, whose replacement text carried one closing parenthesis too many, so the saved init file does not read (`Invalid read syntax`), the restart checks fail in both phases, the undo kept the stray parenthesis (349 bytes against a 348-byte baseline), and both answers' statements about what happens after a restart are wrong. The model never evaluated the buffer it had edited, so nothing told it. An earlier attempt the same day (`m7-attended-chat-01`) is void: the Run was killed by the inactivity clock while its approval question was open, which led to the fix below. After the tool-feedback change below the chat entry was run again (`m7-attended-chat-03`, product `7e368f5b`): the **apply Run passed all eleven checks** — the model opened and edited the init buffer correctly at the first attempt, made the binding live with `define-key`, persisted nothing, the user reviewed a one-line diff and saved, and a fresh Emacs had the binding — and the **undo Run did not**: its first deviation was restoring the live session with `(load "…/init.el")`, which reads the disk, and under M7 the disk still held the applied version because the model's reverting edit was, correctly, only in the buffer; the load bound `describe-function` again, the model's check used `lookup-key` with a literal string instead of `kbd` (which answers `1` for any keymap) and read that as restored, and its answer's first sentence, "The live state is restored", is contrary to the observation. The file itself was restored byte for byte after the user's save and the restart check passed. The act entry was not run, by the stop-at-first-deviation rule. A **model-only comparison** followed (`m7-attended-chat-04-deepseek`, 2026-09-19): the same frozen product code, dependencies, Emacs, task and checker as run 3 (the driver refuses to start unless those fingerprints match), with `deepseek-chat` through llm.el's native DeepSeek provider instead of the local 27B model; the recorded interface differences are that reasoning-off reaches HTTP as `thinking.type=disabled` and that the product sends tool-offering requests to this provider non-streaming. The chat entry **passed**: both Runs completed in under 30 s of model-and-tool time, all eleven checks passed in both phases — including the live binding after undo, which run 3 failed; the model made its edits live with `eval-buffer` on the buffer and queried keys with `kbd` — and the leader's answer review passed with three precision blemishes, none a wrong state claim. This attributes run 3's failure to that model configuration's execution reliability rather than to a missing product mechanism. It is one run with one model. The **act entry** was then run under the same conditions (`m7-attended-act-01-deepseek`): the apply Run passed all eleven checks; the undo Run passed ten — live binding restored, restart restored, init file back to its baseline bytes, source and other modes untouched, and an answer that states accurately what it did — and failed one, `model_did_not_persist`: having found that the disk already held the applied version (the user's own save, which nothing tells the model about), the model reasoned that "restore after a restart too" required it to save, and ran `(save-buffer)` through `eval_elisp`, so the user never reviewed the undo's diff. The approval that let it through was recorded with a wait of 0.000 s: it was answered by a keystroke already in the input queue, a separate product issue now registered with a reproduction (a queued `y` approves a full-control call in 0.004 s, unseen). By the pre-set rule the act entry does not pass; this is neither run 3's failure class (the state and the answer are right) nor an interface failure. After the approval-input fix below the act entry was run again on the repaired product (`m7-attended-act-02-deepseek`, no longer a model-only comparison): no question was answered by queued input any more (every wait 0.6–2.5 s), and the entry still did not pass, for two independent reasons. In apply the model again ran `(save-buffer)` through `eval_elisp`; the question was shown and was allowed 0.9 s later, so what the model does after a refusal was not tested; every state check passed and the answer said truthfully that it had saved. In undo the model investigated (undo list, backup file), then sent a reply with text only — "let me retry …" — and no tool call; the product took it as the final answer and the Run completed with nothing undone. Slice 4 (the module rename) is not built. The contract is accepted. The default level is renamed `edit-buffers` (`workspace-write` stays an input and old-Tape alias and is never written again); a level decides how existing tools may execute and never which tools exist, so `save_buffer` is offered only by an explicit Preset or Request list and is refused under `never` even at `danger-full-access`; the unsaved-changes list is derived from the Run's structured buffer-editing Tape events intersected with buffers that are live and modified now, and does not attribute what `eval_elisp` changed; `open_file` runs Emacs's normal visit semantics, whose hooks and questions are host behaviour outside Superchat's approvals and Tape; the internal-buffer rule survives the deletion of the write sandbox as the one enforcement point. M5's T2 acceptance is re-run under M7's division of labour (the model edits, the person reviews and saves, the checker restarts), and the `superchat-authority.el` rename follows M7.

### M8 — Inline act: a task from the source buffer, finished and reported in place (`done` 2026-09-22)

Goal: close the gap three independent reviewers named on 2026-09-22 (`.scratch/discussions/2026-09-22-design-goal-review.md`): a task started from the buffer being edited ran in the chat window, so "ask here" and "do it here" were two experiences. The record is [the inline-act decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-22-inline-act.md); the user accepted the design on 2026-09-22, after it was built.

Delivered (`current`): the `inline-act` row and surface in the default profile (disabled in `headless`); `superchat-inline-act` runs a full tool Turn with the default baseline, ordinary permission levels and the minibuffer approval, reporting in an inline overlay whose header shows "Working: tool…" while a tool body runs (Runtime tells every surface since [the tool-status decision](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-surfaces-are-told-when-a-tool-runs.md); before it the header stayed at "Waiting…"); at the end the overlay appends recorded facts (tool-call count from the Tape, the unsaved-changes summary). On the overlay `d` shows the diff of an edited buffer, `r` replies on the same surface, `q` cancels the Run, and `M-RET` or a click opens the inline act menu, whose reply also stays on `inline-act`. `superchat-act` still runs in the chat window. Found on the way and fixed as its own issue: a Run hosted on a non-chat buffer (inline ask, Org Babel, Magit) had the agent loop's tool transcript inserted into that buffer; `superchat--agent-render-buffer` now renders only into a chat View.

Evidence: `scripts/inline-act-regression.el` (12, scripted transport: row and surface, refusal without the row, an edit landing in the buffer with no chat buffer created, the default baseline in the request, the footer, `d`, `r`, the menu, a denied `eval_elisp` recording the approval pair with its body never run, `q` cancelling) and `scripts/borrowed-buffer-transcript-regression.el` (3). Real model (LM Studio `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`): two inline act tasks, one of which reads another buffer and edits the current line, and one cancel, with no chat buffer created (`.scratch/m8-inline-act/real-run-01.md`, `.scratch/g2-verification/`). Not covered: refusing an approval with a real model (scripted only), other models.

### M9 — G4 closed at the composition layer (`done` 2026-09-22; load isolation not a goal)

Record: [the light M9 decision](decisions/accepted/2026-09-22-spine-loads-without-the-product.md), with its amendment. Load isolation is not a goal of this milestone: `(require 'superchat)` loads the whole product, including modules whose rows are disabled.

Delivered (`current`): unmounting a default row withdraws what that row contributes. For the seven non-surface rows (memory, changes, subagent, workflow, workspace, plan, compact) their projections, Services and listeners go and come back on remount. The built-in tools of `subagent`, `workspace` and `memory` are registered by those rows, so an unmounted row's tools are not offered to the model, cannot be selected by an explicit tool list, and a copy materialized earlier is refused before its body runs; the default tool surface is unchanged. Entry points that start new work refuse naming the row: `>>`, `superchat-workflow-execute-async` (and so `/workflow NAME`), `superchat-research-workflow` and its resume, `/subagent`, `superchat-agents-panel-show`. Listing recipes, running work's callbacks, cancel and cleanup are not blocked.

Evidence: `scripts/composition-regression.el` 22/22; M3 137/0, M6 62/62, M7 21/21, inline-act 11/11, smoke 0 mismatches, kernel check 0 fails, re-run by the leader.

