### `sql` leaves the ordinary tool surface; history tools follow the Run (`implemented` 2026-10-05, offline evidence only)

[The decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-05-sql-leaves-the-ordinary-tool-surface.md) closes the first gap slice 2 of M11 recorded. Reproduced first: from another Workspace's conversation and from a temporary one, `sql` returned a sentence said in Workspace P while `memory_search` did not. `sql` is out of the built-in `research` Preset and out of `all`; it exists only where the user's own list names it. `memory_search`, `tool_history`, `file_history` and `recent_errors` read for the conversation of the Run that called them, and search nothing when there is none, where they used to read the current buffer's conversation and fall back to every conversation. `scripts/workspace-scope-regression.el` 9 tests, 6 red first and 3 guards; the recall, composition, sub-agent rules, work-record, timer and permission suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. No real-model run. Still open from slice 2: the user's `/recall` searches every conversation, and a workflow step carried no rules (closed below, "Rules on rewrite and workflow").

### A wake that goes wrong (`implemented` 2026-10-06, offline evidence only)

Fault injection first: `scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el`, 8 tests, 5 red on the code as it stood (a wake whose send signalled, a wake cut off before the send, two due records with the first failing, the report after the in-memory link was lost) and 3 guards. The repair is in `superchat-work.el` only and adds no queue and no scheduler: a wake counts when a Run started in its conversation, read from the Tape; a wait whose wake started nothing is put back, at once when the send signals and at the next scan when Emacs had exited; the report to the main conversation is found from the `work.woken` fact instead of an in-memory table. 2 more tests after 7 mutants, of which 5 were red and 2 survived until those tests were added; 10 tests in all. The work-record, timer, history, scope and composition suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. No real-model run. The three things left open here are closed by the next entry.

### Work that came due and did not start (`implemented` 2026-10-06, offline evidence only)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-06-a-fired-task-that-did-not-start.md`. Timer tasks: `timer.sending` and `timer.started` record the send and the Run; a fired task without a Run is started later (retry in the same Emacs, and first thing after a restart); a send that may have been cut off is recorded as `timer.unconfirmed`, said in the conversation and not repeated; a busy conversation makes the task wait instead of queueing in memory; a fired, unstarted task can be cancelled. `scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 12 tests; they call functions the old code does not have, so all 12 fail there and that count says little. 11 mutants of the repair: 10 red, 1 survived (the `timer.sending` fact not written) until an assertion was added. Work Records: a failed wake is retried in the same Emacs (300 s, 3 times); an execution an earlier Emacs never finished gets the outcome `interrupted` and one line in the main conversation, and is not started again. 4 more tests in `scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el` (14 in all), 3 red on the code as it stood and 1 guard. No queue and no scheduler were added. The code was written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order and is byte-identical to the leader's prototype. No real-model run; an exit of Emacs is simulated by not sending and by hand-written facts, not by killing a process. Still open: what a cut-off execution left in unsaved buffers (step 4 of the plan).

### Rules on rewrite and workflow (`implemented` 2026-10-06; the rewrite measured on real models 2026-10-07)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-06-rules-on-rewrite-and-workflow.md`. Reproduced first by reading the request sent to the provider: a region rewrite carried only its own system text, a workflow step no system text at all. The one rules collector now also serves `inline-rewrite` and `workflow` (rules alone, no note about remembering; the rewrite's own instruction stays last); compaction and skill matching carry none. A rules file that exists and cannot be read is told to the user, and to the model on the chat surfaces; the request is not refused. `scripts/entry-rules-regression.el`, 12 tests; of the first 11, 8 were red on the code as it stood and 3 are guards. 8 mutants, all red. The rules, sub-agent-rules, rewrite, composition, history and scope suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. Written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order, byte-identical to the leader's prototype. Measured on real models on 2026-10-07 with a rule "start every answer with a line of reasoning": placing the rewrite's instruction last was not enough, DeepSeek put that line into the user's file 3 times of 3 (local Qwen 0 of 1). The rules of a rewrite are now followed by a note saying which kind of rule applies to it (`superchat-prompt--rewrite-rules-note`); after it DeepSeek 0 of 5 and Qwen 0 of 3 carried the line, and the date-format rule in the same file was followed 4 of 5 and 3 of 3. One scenario and one conflicting rule; the suite is 13 tests, the new one red on the code before and on two mutants. Still open: the Telega and emacsclient entries are untraced; a queued input after a window switch has no test of its own; a workflow step is still not recorded as conversation.

### Working beside the user: what real runs showed (2026-10-07, observations before the repairs below)

Twelve scenarios in a sandboxed Emacs with a stand-in user (`.scratch/task-sim/harness-goals/tasks-step4.el`, not in the repository), DeepSeek three times each and local Qwen once, after a first round that was used to correct the scenarios' own checks and is not counted. Counts are runs. They are small; a scenario seen once on Qwen is one run.

Held every time, both models:

- The user and the agent edit one buffer in turn: the user's edits and the agent's are all there (3 and 1).
- A Run in progress when the user switches to another Workspace's conversation, or changes preset: it completes, its edit lands, its answer is in its own conversation and nothing of it in the other (3 and 1 each).
- An approval question left unanswered for 75 seconds: Emacs keeps running timers, and after "no" the call is not run and the answer does not invent a result (3 and 1).
- A record that wakes while the user is typing a draft: the draft's text and point are unchanged (3 and 1).
- The user has unsaved edits and another program changes the file: the user's line and the other program's line both survive the agent's edit (3 and 1, in each of two variants).
- After a restart the record is found in the Workspace, carried into the new conversation, and finished there (2 and 1; a third DeepSeek run left no result file).

Did not hold:

- **A woken record's review screen takes the cursor** out of the draft the user is typing in: 2 of 3 on DeepSeek, 1 of 1 on Qwen. This is the review screen's accepted behaviour applied to work nobody is watching; whether automatic work may take focus is the user's open decision.
- **Killing the conversation's buffer while its Run works fails the Run** (3 of 3, 1 of 1): the edit is not made, the reason recorded is "Human input adapter is no longer available".
- **The agent is never told the file changed on disk** (0 of 8 runs). When the buffer had no unsaved edits before the outside change, Emacs asks its own "changed on disk; really edit the buffer?" during the agent's edit (4 of 4); Superchat neither asks it nor knows of it.
- **A Work Record's rendering does not name its executions**, only the files they changed (0 of 3): "which run did this" is answerable from the Tape, not from the record the user sees.
- **Two Runs appending to one buffer**: no line was lost or doubled and each run saw the other's lines, but on DeepSeek, in 2 of 3 attempts one of the two Runs ended one line short with an answer that announced the next line instead of saying it stopped. Cause not established. On Qwen the second concurrent request got an error body from the local server in a shape the client did not parse, and that Run never ended (waited 240 s).
- **An approval question that stands** blocks the minibuffer for everything. A record that came due meanwhile woke and made its edit (3 of 3 on DeepSeek in a rerun with the scenario corrected, 1 of 1 on Qwen); in those three reruns the woken Run then asked its own question, apparently on top of the standing one (the snapshot shows a second minibuffer), and neither moved until the stand-in answered at 300 s.

Not exercised: a human at the keyboard; killing Emacs in mid-Run (the restart scenario restarts between Runs); more than two concurrent Runs; Telega and emacsclient.

### Working beside the user: the three rulings and what was repaired (`implemented`, 2026-10-07)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-07-working-beside-the-user.md` (the user, 2026-10-07: "按照建议"). An execution nobody started (a scheduled task's, a woken record's) shows no review screen and so does not take the cursor; the user's own executions keep the 2026-09-25 behaviour. A Run whose conversation buffer is killed goes on and fails only the `ask_user` call that needs the user. Before a structured edit Superchat looks whether the file changed on disk: a buffer without unsaved changes is reloaded and the edit sent back to be redone, a buffer with unsaved changes is edited and the model told that saving would overwrite the disk; `read_buffer` says so too; the user gets one echo-area line. A Work Record opened from the list names its executions (the text sent to the model is unchanged). A server error llm.el cannot read becomes an ordinary error instead of a Run that never ends. `scripts/beside-user-regression.el`, 13 tests; of the first 11, 9 were red on the code as it stood and 2 are guards; 14 mutants, all red. Every focused suite, the M3 set (137 expected, 2 skipped, untouched), the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green; byte-compilation prints the same 79 warning lines before and after. Written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order, identical to the leader's prototype.

Real runs after the change (DeepSeek three times, Qwen once, same scenarios): the cursor stayed in the draft 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the Run with its buffer killed completed 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the model was told of the disk change and told the user 6 of 6 and 2 of 2, and Emacs's own question appeared 0 of 4; the record named the execution before the restart 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the Qwen Run that used to hang ended with the server's error (1 of 1). The first batch of the disk scenarios was misjudged by the harness (it took Superchat's own echo-area line for a question) and was rerun.

Two Runs on one buffer, one ending a line short: cause established from the provider's raw replies in both failing runs. The short Run's last reply was text only ("Second line added. Now the third."), `finish_reason` `stop`, no tool call; a reply without a tool call is the final answer. Superchat dropped no tool call and no line. Nothing was changed for it.

Still open: an automatic execution's unsaved changes are announced by one echo-area line only; edits made through `eval_elisp` or a shell do not pass the disk check; a standing approval question with a second one on top (seen in the step-4 runs) is untouched; no human at the keyboard.

### Minimum Emacs 30.1 (`implemented`, verified 2026-09-23)

[The minimum Emacs is 30.1](decisions/implemented/2026-09-22-minimum-emacs-30-1.md), the user's ruling of 2026-09-22 after the Emacs 29.4 run. On a locally built 30.1 every suite, the canonical smoke, the kernel check and a real DeepSeek chat session (read, edit, undo, refused `eval_elisp`) pass; the run found one product fault, C-g not cancelling an approval question asked from a callback, which also affected 30.1 and is fixed for every supported Emacs. Evidence: `.scratch/g3-emacs30/README.md`. Not verified: a GUI Emacs 30.1, Emacs 30.2.

### Skills, models and commands anywhere in a message (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-triggers-anywhere-in-the-message.md). `>skill`, `@model` and `/command` are read from the middle of a message the user typed, there only when the name exists; what opens the message reads as before and wins; a message a timer or a woken Work Record sends is read at its start only; a model name is checked without asking the provider. One parse point (`superchat-core--parse-input`), no second path. Evidence: `scripts/input-syntax-regression.el`, 18 tests; 12 fail on the code before the change, 6 are guards; 12 of 12 mutations caught. Not verified: a real model, the completion-then-send flow by hand in a live Emacs. Not built: a skill that applies to one request only (undecided), the other items learned from the gptel presets talk ([proposal](decisions/proposed/2026-10-10-source-turn-destination.md)).

### An agent skill for one message (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-an-agent-skill-for-one-message.md). An `agent` skill named in the middle of a message shapes that one request (persona, tools, model) and leaves the Session's preset and Tape configuration alone; at the start it still switches the Session; a `plan` skill switches wherever it is named. The one request carries the skill's own Capability, because Runtime refuses a request whose Capability differs from its Agent Profile's. Evidence: `scripts/skill-once-regression.el`, 5 tests through the real send path; 3 fail on the code before the change, 2 are guards; 6 of 6 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, three messages (plain, agent skill named mid-message, plain): DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 followed the skill on that message and dropped it on the next, with the Session not switched; the task names the buffer to read. Not done: the skill's `plugins:` are not mounted for the one message. Found then, repaired in the next entry: a message that invokes any skill, at the start or in the middle, goes out without the `[Emacs origin]` block and the window arrangement, because a skill-fixed prompt travels as `:chat-prompt-override`, which those collectors read as "the user already previewed this text"; one earlier DeepSeek run that did not name the buffer answered that it had no information.

### A skill message says where the user is (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-a-skill-message-says-where-the-user-is.md). The origin and window-arrangement collectors stay silent only for a request the user previewed (`:chat-prepared-context`); a message whose text a skill fixed (`:chat-prompt-override` alone) carries the `[Emacs origin]` block, the explicit selection and `[Emacs windows]` like any other. Evidence: `scripts/skill-context-regression.el`, 5 tests through the real send path; 4 fail on the code before the change, 1 is a guard; 4 of 4 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, a skill message that does not name the buffer ("用一句话说明 >duty 这里写的周五要交什么。"): DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 answered from the buffer the user was in; before the change one DeepSeek run on such a message answered that it had no information.

### A notice for work nobody watched (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-a-notice-for-work-nobody-watched.md). An execution Superchat started by itself (a scheduled task, a woken Work Record) leaves a notice when it ends, however it ends; the mode line shows `[Superchat: N]` until the user opens or dismisses the notices (`superchat-changes-notices`). Hooked on `turn-completed` inside the existing `changes` plugin, using the Tape-derived `superchat-changes--unattended-run-p`; no Sink was wrapped and no plugin row added. The notices live in this Emacs only. Evidence: `scripts/unattended-notice-regression.el`, 8 tests, the task and the wake on the real path; 11 of 11 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, a Work Record that wakes after two minutes while the user types a draft: DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 left exactly one notice naming the record and showed `[Superchat: 1]`, with the draft and the cursor untouched. Three more runs of that scenario gave no result; they ran at the same time as the full check suite and the same scenario passed when run alone, so they are counted as interference between parallel runs, by timing only -- how they interfered was not found.

### Remarks hung on the text (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-remarks-hung-on-the-text.md). `superchat-annotate` asks for remarks on the region or the buffer and hangs each on the text it is about, as overlays; the text is never changed. The model sees numbered lines and answers `LINE | QUOTE | REMARK`; a remark is placed where its quote is found on that line or within two lines, inside the region, and nowhere otherwise. Chosen after an experiment outside Superchat (570 rows, two models): quoting alone let the local Qwen invent 11 of 27 quotes on code, line number plus quote placed 21 of 21. Its own surface `inline-annotate`, registered by the existing `inline-rewrite` plugin row; rules carried with their own note; no tools. The transport's stand-in for an empty answer is now the constant `superchat-llm-empty-response-text`. Evidence: `scripts/annotate-regression.el`, 18 tests through a real send to a scripted model; 27 mutations, 25 caught, the other two are the two "no tools" settings that back each other. Real models through the command, sandboxed Emacs: DeepSeek 3/3 on configuration (32 of 32 each), notes, and notes under a rule asking for a line of reasoning first (never written into the answer); on code 13 to 20 remarks placed per run with 0 to 6 not placed, mostly quotes written as string literals. Local Qwen 1/1 on configuration, notes and notes under the rule; on code nothing with thinking on (output spent on reasoning at the server's 8192 context), 9 of 12 and 6 of 299 rows with thinking off. Found on the way: the harness's `thinking: disabled` is ignored by LM Studio, so every earlier local-Qwen run had thinking on. Display changed afterwards at the user's request, without Flymake: a remark is a block under its line, wrapped and indented like the line, the remarks of one line stacked in text order (6 more tests, 24 in all; 10 mutations of the display code, all caught; seen only as printed text, not in a window). Not solved: string-literal quotes and quotes holding a bar are not placed; the message for an answer cut at its length limit; no cap on a long answer; no person at the keyboard.

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-annotations-asked-for-in-a-conversation.md). In a conversation, `>annotate` makes that one message's answer also hang on the buffer the conversation was called from (or the buffer or file named after it): history carried, no tools, the conversation's own Sink wrapped and not replaced, the row form last in the system text. The mark is a destination recorded on the Turn, read at the one parse point before `>skill`, never read from a message Superchat sent itself. The Tape keeps the mark on such a message (the model is sent the text without it), so regenerate annotates again and edit restores the mark; a later message of that conversation is told the earlier answer was hung on the buffer and is finished. The note is written once a remark is on the text and is kept with its Session id, so a failed request, an answer that placed nothing, or another conversation in the same View is not told. Evidence: `scripts/annotate-chat-regression.el`, 19 tests through a real conversation with a scripted model, one of them the smoke's request-rebuild verdict on an annotating message; 29 mutations, all caught; `scripts/smoke-expected/V.txt` now counts 15 collectors. Real models through a conversation, sandboxed Emacs, three messages from 15 lines of meeting notes (a question, "mark these on the notes `>annotate`", an ordinary one): the annotating message placed every remark on its exact line in every run (DeepSeek 10 runs, local Qwen 3). Before the "it was hung" note DeepSeek answered the ordinary message after it by writing the remarks into the buffer in 1 of 3 runs; with the note, 0 of 7. Small samples; the local Qwen with thinking on did not reach its context limit here. Not solved: the "it was hung" note lives in the View, for this Emacs only; the rows stay in the conversation and its history; no person at the keyboard.

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-work-records-and-timers-belong-to-a-workspace.md). The architecture review of 2026-10-10 (`reports/architecture-review-main-2026-10-10/`) was re-run on the day's code: of its findings F1, F2, F4, F5, N1 and N2 all still reproduced. F1 and F2 are closed here: the model's `work_record` resolves a record through `superchat-work--for`, and `timer_list` / `timer_cancel` go through `superchat-timer--within-p`; another Workspace's record or timer is refused, a conversation of no Workspace reaches only what was made in it, and the user's own list and commands and the product's own writes are unchanged. A sub-agent's conversation is bound to no Workspace, so its own tool call on the record is refused, as K14 already told it. Evidence: `scripts/object-scope-regression.el`, 15 tests (two through the real tool loop), 9 red on the old code; 16 mutations, all caught; the review's two probes for these findings now fail. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, scenario OSC (alpha starts a record and sets a reminder; beta is given the record's id and asked to complete it, then asked to list and cancel every timer): DeepSeek 2 of 2 and the local Qwen 1 of 1 were refused, said so to the user without claiming success, were shown neither the goal nor the reminder, and left both untouched. N1 is closed: a scheduled task's retry limit and its own wait after a failure are now checked before a start is tried, not only when the next retry is scheduled, and the one retry timer is due at the soonest time any task asked for; before, a task whose conversation stayed busy kept that timer going and carried a failing task past its limit (6 sends at a limit of 2 in the review's probe) and brought its retries forward to every 30 seconds. Evidence: `scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 15 tests (3 new, 2 changed to move the clock past the wait before retrying); 9 mutations, all caught; the review's N1 probe now fails. A Work Record's wake has its own timer per record and did not have this fault. N2 is closed: a scheduled task whose model answered inside the send had ended before `timer.started` was written, was judged the user's own execution, and left no notice on the mode line; the timer now tells through `superchat-timer-started-functions` once the fact is written and the changes plugin leaves the notice of an execution that has already ended, still once. The review screen was not affected: it is decided from a timer, after the send returned. A Work Record's wake writes `work.woken` before it sends and did not have this fault. Evidence: `scripts/unattended-notice-regression.el`, 11 tests (3 new, with the model answering inside the send); 5 mutations, all caught; the review's N2 probe now fails. The documents the review named as describing history are corrected in `docs/architecture.md` (which Run a woken record started; what writing "fired" first does and does not promise; the mode-line notice) and `CONTEXT.md` (which conversation an in-place command joins). F4 and F5 are closed ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-the-work-record-tool-keeps-or-refuses.md)): a `start` that comes with progress, a place to pick up and a wait records them with the start, and one that cannot keep what it came with starts nothing; a wait for a time is refused, with the reason, while the `work-records` row is not mounted, and reading, updating, waiting for the user and completing still work; an `update` checks its wait before it writes. Evidence: `scripts/work-tool-regression.el`, 10 tests, 7 red on the old code; 10 mutations, all caught; the review's F4 and F5 probes now fail, so of its seven probes only the F3 guard still passes. The same question for the `timers` row was then asked and had the same answer: unmounted, `timer_create` answered "the user will be reminded" and wrote a timer nothing would fire. It is now refused with the reason and writes nothing; `timer_list` says timers are off; `timer_cancel` is unchanged ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-the-timer-tool-sets-or-refuses.md); `scripts/timer-tool-regression.el`, 5 tests, 4 red on the old code; 6 mutations, all caught). Not done from the review: an explicit dependency between a capability's tool, collector, observer and timers; plugins a one-message skill declares; the "later" view; narrow tools measured on the local model.

### Fixes from the 2026-09-23 round (`implemented`)

The act entry's failing and passing runs and the user's 2026-09-23 decisions produced five accepted records, all built on 2026-09-23. What each one has in the tree:

| Decision | State |
|---|---|
| [The next turn sees what earlier tools returned](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-the-next-turn-sees-previous-tool-results.md) | implemented (`1711be1c`, `2d16c144`, `3ac45cec`): tool events keep bounded argument and result text, later turns see one line per tool call, compaction summarizes those lines and covers their rows |
| [One finish check after a text-only reply](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-one-finish-check-after-a-text-only-reply.md) | implemented in its narrowed form (`7133d4ab`) for the chat window and act; request evidence contains the note since the last row; inline act joined on 2026-09-23, after its overlay learned to show the next reply in place of the previous one (Runtime now tells a Sink when a further attempt begins); inline ask stays out |
| [Retry a transient provider error](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-retry-transient-provider-errors.md) | implemented: transport failures first (`afba469b`), then the user's amendment, 10 retries 30 s apart and HTTP 429/5xx retried too. The status comes from a hook on llm's plz error handler; a test drives llm's real error chain with a stubbed transport, so an llm upgrade that breaks the hook fails that test. Second amendment (user: "A"): the wait is shown on screen, counting down in the chat window, once in inline ask, inline act and rewrite. Not yet seen against a real rate limit |
| [Surfaces are told when a tool runs](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-surfaces-are-told-when-a-tool-runs.md) | implemented (`9c39d99f`): the inline act overlay shows "Working: tool…" during a tool body; the chat window is unchanged |
| [Request evidence includes Step material](decisions/accepted/2026-09-23-request-evidence-includes-step-material.md) | implemented: each request names the material its Run's Steps inserted (a steer, an injection, the finish-check note) and the evidence rebuilds it from the stored text; a later turn's history item for such material rebuilds as the line the model saw, no longer as the placeholder "Injected model material" |

### Both entries pass acceptance on one commit (`accepted` 2026-09-24)

Accepted by the user ("A"): each entry ran once on `9178192c` and passed; the operating note was changed twice later the same day (`commandp`; the `lookup-key` line), so this acceptance covers the note as it was at `9178192c`, not the current text. The configuration apply/undo acceptance passed on both the chat entry (`m8-attended-both-deepseek-01`) and the act entry (`m8-attended-both-deepseek-02`) at product commit `9178192c`, DeepSeek, attended: all four phases 11/11 and all four answers consistent with the observations under the leader's review. This is the first time both entries passed on the same commit; each entry ran once, so it is not a success rate. No expression ran a command in either run (checks read `commandp`, `key-binding`, `lookup-key`), so the eval_elisp prompt stop of `9178192c` was not exercised and is covered by its regression tests only. Kept on record: the chat apply again misread `lookup-key`'s `2` as "occupied"; a garbled sentence in the chat undo; the act undo blamed the disk change on "auto-save or an outside save" (it was the user's save) and said the original text could not be restored from anywhere without checking the existing backup. Report: [both entries on one commit](reports/2026-09-24-both-entries-acceptance-one-commit.md).

### Chat entry acceptance on the latest commit (passes 2026-09-24; superseded by the both-entries acceptance above)

The configuration apply/undo acceptance passed on the chat entry at product commit `3b5af2d6`, DeepSeek, attended: both phases 11/11 and both answers consistent with the observations under the leader's review. The undo request carried the apply turn's 16 tool observations and restored the observed original `ignore`. Kept on record: to "verify" the command the apply ran `(command-execute 'describe-function)`, which most likely made the user answer a minibuffer prompt (inferred from timing; issue `.scratch/issues/2026-09-24-a-verification-ran-an-interactive-command.md`), and a typo. The act entry was not re-run on this commit (its last pass is `afba469b`), so this is not an acceptance of both entries on one version. Report: [chat entry acceptance on the latest commit](reports/2026-09-24-chat-entry-acceptance-latest-commit.md).

### Chat entry acceptance on the current commit (`accepted` 2026-09-22)

The configuration apply/undo acceptance was re-run on the chat entry at product commit `a7879950`, DeepSeek `deepseek-chat`, with the user answering every approval and doing the saves: both phases completed and passed all eleven state checks, and the user ruled the undo phase valid although its answer wrongly said the disk already held `#'ignore` (the user had saved `#'describe-function` after apply). That blemish, and both answers calling `lookup-key`'s `2` "a prefix", stay on record. DeepSeek reported the served model as `deepseek-flash` in every response. The act entry was then run on the same commit and did not pass (`m8-attended-act-deepseek-01`): apply ended on a reply that announced a check and called no tool (acceptance issue 03, third occurrence), and undo, which sees only the previous turn's user and final assistant text, did not know the original binding and restored "unbound" instead of `ignore`. The act entry's last pass is still `9c0c3c3e`. Report: [chat entry acceptance on the current commit](reports/2026-09-22-chat-entry-acceptance-current-commit.md).

### Housekeeping after M5/M7 (`done`, 2026-09-20)

The items the 2026-09-19 development audit and the 2026-09-16 follow-ups had left open, each calibrated against the tree first. Packaging: `superchat-pkg.el` now declares what the authoritative header declares (Emacs 29.1, llm 0.31.1), checked on a built tar; the README's one declared install method was exercised from a clean HOME with tracked files only; a `package.el` tar install exposed a real byte-compile error in the bundled Telega plugin (its session file could not find its sibling when compiled on its own), now fixed. Stale notes: the gptel wording left in `superchat.el` is corrected, a user option nothing read (`superchat-completion-check-delay`) is removed, both READMEs list `render-ui`. M7's fourth slice: `superchat-authority.el` is `superchat-tool-execution.el`, with every symbol it defines renamed and no aliases kept; eleven old-prefix names remain in the code on purpose — they name things that no longer exist. The declared minimum was later raised to Emacs 30.1 (2026-09-22, the user's ruling), after a run on a locally built Emacs 29.4 found two behaviour differences; evidence in `.scratch/g3-emacs29/README.md`. Emacs 30.1 itself has not been run here; the code is exercised on 32.0.50. Left to real use by the user's ruling of 2026-09-20: the observation that a reply announcing the next step with no tool call ends a Run has not recurred in the two runs since (which recorded raw response bytes), and nothing in the product changed for it, so it is recorded as not reproduced -- not as fixed -- and is to be handled if it shows up again. M5 slices 3–4 stay `proposed`.

### Clocks — the Run deadline waits for an open question (`implemented`, 2026-09-20)

Having tried the previous day's behaviour by hand — a 20-second deadline, the question withdrawn under him — the user reversed the ruling that a person's waiting time should count: a question should stay until it is answered. Of three options he chose the narrow one: [the Run deadline is stopped while an approval question is open](decisions/implemented/2026-09-20-the-run-deadline-waits-for-an-open-question.md) and continues with what was left afterwards; a Run with no question, and an unattended Run, keep the full deadline. The deadline can therefore no longer arrive with a question open, and the dismissal below remains for Runs ended by something else. `ask_user` followed the same day by the user's ruling ([decision](decisions/implemented/2026-09-20-ask-user-waits-for-the-user-too.md)): one rule in the runtime now covers both kinds of question, three more tests red before, three mutants red, the protected M3 set untouched. The user also ruled that the cumulative-limit clock machinery stays as an abstract component; with its 600-second default gone, nothing further is to be torn out. Two new tests red before, two mutants red; interactively, with a 15-second deadline a question stayed open for 40 s and the late `y` still ran the tool. The same leader-owned M3 seal test needed its `timed-out` branch to ask for that ending explicitly instead of waiting for the deadline; no assertion changed, logged with evidence.

### Clocks — no cumulative limit by default; a question does not outlive its Run (`implemented`, 2026-09-19)

Two user rulings on the long-open question about a person's waiting time. The wait **should** count against a Run's limits, so nothing is frozen for it; but the 600-second cumulative transport limit is unnecessary — the Run deadline covers the one case only it caught — and it is the limit a careful reader of approval questions runs into. [It is now off by default](decisions/implemented/2026-09-19-no-cumulative-transport-limit-by-default.md); the option and its clock machinery stay in the code, opt-in, and tearing them out is a separate item not yet contracted. And because the deadline can therefore arrive with a question open, [the question is dismissed when its Run ends](decisions/implemented/2026-09-19-a-question-is-dismissed-when-its-run-ends.md): measured before — the Run sealed `audit-incomplete`, the question stayed up, the late answer was recorded and ran nothing, nobody was told. The Run's ending is unchanged (`audit-incomplete` for an undecided approval stays pinned); one bookkeeping line in the leader-owned M3 set, which assumed a question outlives its Run, moved into an `unwind-protect`, no assertion changed, logged with evidence.

### M7 correction — `/changes` recognizes the edited buffer by its mark (`implemented`, 2026-09-19)

The development audit of 2026-09-19 reproduced a gap in M7's own rule for finding the edited buffer again ("by the recorded name, else by the recorded file; no separate buffer identity"): after a rename, an unrelated buffer that took the old name was listed and the real target missed. The user kept `/changes` and chose the fix: [the editing tool marks the buffer itself](decisions/implemented/2026-09-19-changes-recognizes-a-buffer-by-its-mark.md) with the Session and Run that changed it; candidates are the Tape's editing Runs, delegated ones included, intersected with live, unsaved buffers carrying one of their marks. No ledger, nothing on the Tape or on disk; a buffer killed and visited again is no longer listed. Five new tests (two red before), five mutants red — one only after a test was added that tells one Run's buffers from another's — and the first test ever of the delegated path, which turned out to work.

### M7 leftover — `eval_elisp` told the model to persist (`fixed`, 2026-09-19)

A third act run (`m7-attended-act-03-deepseek`) failed like the first two — the model saved through `eval_elisp`, and both requests were shown and allowed — and its review, started by a second agent and re-verified against the raw requests, found the cause in the product: the `eval_elisp` description still said "For persistence, save the actual loaded configuration and verify in a fresh Emacs that loads it", while `edit_buffer` and the runtime context said saving is the user's. M7's second slice, which was told to leave no half-compatible semantics, swept for `write_file`, the escalation arguments and the old level name and missed this sentence. The three act failures are therefore not evidence of a model crossing a clearly stated division of labour. Fixed, with a regression that no description on the default surface instructs persisting. The same run's undo is not clean evidence either: the harness ran inside its own `load`, so Emacs kept its whole source in ` *load*`, and the model read the checker's expected values from it; the harness now starts after its file has loaded and every run is scanned for contact with harness internals (the one passing run, chat-04, is clean). The first act run on a product and a harness with no known defect followed (`m7-attended-act-04-deepseek`): the model requested no save in either phase and spawned no Emacs of its own — every earlier act run had done both — the apply Run passed all eleven checks with an answer in M7's terms (edited the buffer, not saved, please save), and the one harness-contact flag, reviewed, was `(car command-line-args)` returning the Emacs binary's path. The undo Run did not pass: the model reverted the buffer correctly, said itself that the live `define-key` still had to be undone, and sent that as a reply with no tool call, which the product by design takes as the final answer; the file and the restart are restored, the live binding is not. This is the second such ending in four act runs with this model (none in its chat run, none with the local model); it is recorded as an observation, not as a product defect. To close the evidence gap the harness now records the raw bytes of every non-streaming response before decoding (never headers), and its save review became a full-window read-only buffer with `s` / `n` keys because the earlier prompt could take input while the diff sat in a small unselected window. `m7-attended-act-05-deepseek` (same product fingerprints as run 4): apply 11/11; in undo the model restored both the buffer and the live session to `ignore`, requested no save and did not stop half way, and the 29 raw responses carry exactly the tool calls that were executed; the two checks that failed — restart and restored file — follow from the user choosing not to save the undo diff in the review, which the user confirmed. That run followed — `m7-attended-act-06-deepseek`, same product fingerprints: all eleven checks in both phases, no save requested by the model, both diffs reviewed and saved by the user, 17 raw responses carrying exactly the executed tool calls, a clean harness-contact scan, and an answer review that passed with one stated judgement call (a bullet under "verification" says the change survives a restart before the answer's own closing section makes that conditional on the user's save). With the chat pass this satisfies M5's two-entry acceptance and completes this milestone's third slice; slice 4, renaming `superchat-authority.el` to `superchat-tool-execution.el`, follows as its own item.

### Fix — an approval is answered by input that came after it (`implemented`, 2026-09-19)

Found by the attended act run `m7-attended-act-01-deepseek`: the approval that let the model run `(save-buffer)` was recorded with a wait of 0.000 s — answered by a key already in the input queue, unseen. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-19-an-approval-is-answered-by-input-that-came-after-it.md): every question sets its own input boundary (pending input is discarded before it is asked, so a second `y` cannot approve the next question), `C-g` remains a quit, and a running keyboard macro is not a person (the chain answers `unavailable`, the macro's keys are not read). It keeps old input from approving; it does not prove anyone read the arguments. `scripts/approval-input-regression.el` drives an interactive child Emacs through a pseudo-terminal (5 tests, four red before in one shape, four mutants red). The act entry is to be re-run on this product; that run validates the whole repaired chain and is no longer a model-only comparison.

### Tool feedback — a broken Lisp edit is sent back (`implemented`, 2026-09-19)

The first valid attended chat run under M7 (`m7-attended-chat-02`) failed on the model's own `edit_buffer` call: one closing parenthesis too many, never noticed because the model did not evaluate the buffer it had edited. The user ruled it a model-capability problem whose right handling is to send the call back with the error so the model retries, by the tool itself, leaving nothing in the buffer. [The contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-19-a-broken-lisp-edit-is-sent-back.md): for a Lisp buffer the three structured edit tools rehearse the change on a private copy, and an edit that turns a buffer that reads into one that does not fails with an `Error:` naming the mismatch and its line while the real buffer is never touched and nothing is attributed to the call; an already unbalanced buffer is edited and told its state; other modes and correct edits are unchanged. `scripts/edit-check-regression.el` (7 tests, six red before at exactly this gap, six mutants red); through the acceptance harness a scripted model that first submits the real run's bad edit and then the right one passes both entries including the restart check. The attended chat run is to be repeated in a new directory.

### Fix — an open approval question stops the inactivity clock (`implemented`, 2026-09-18)

Found by the first attended run under M7's criteria (`m7-attended-chat-01`, void): the user read an `eval_elisp` question for 467 s and the Run failed under the open question at 181 s, because the provider attempt's inactivity timer kept running during the approval wait and was renewed only after the answer. By the user's ruling this is a product defect, not experiment configuration, so it was fixed before the acceptance continues: [the contract](decisions/implemented/2026-09-18-approval-wait-pauses-the-inactivity-clock.md) — while somebody is being asked the attempt's inactivity clock does not run, and after the decision it continues from what remained; a Run cancelled during the wait is not revived; silence with nobody waiting still times out; the cumulative limit is out of scope. `scripts/approval-wait-regression.el` (6 tests; three red before the fix in the same shape as the real run, two guards green before and after, four mutants each red on its own test); end to end in an interactive Emacs the reproduction that failed (8 s watchdog, 25 s read) now completes. The M5 acceptance harness no longer moves this timer aside.

### Later (`proposed`, gated on the undecided tool surface)

Emacs capability seams (buffer, command, eval, process, file, org, magit, lsp), each a Definition, a Provider and a Consumer, and the Emacs-object tool surface. M5 deliberately does not build these: it answers the tool-surface question from the other end, by giving the model the map and the verbs it already has and letting real tasks say what is missing. A seam is built when a task shows one is needed, not because the domain list has eight entries.

Independent and unscheduled (`proposed`): a standing Preset mount that several Views join by re-parenting. It does not depend on the tool surface. It must keep the delivered per-View isolation: two Views choosing the same Preset name are not, by that fact, joined to one mount.

Work items after [Holon's "why work items"](https://holon.run/zh-CN/blog/why-work-items) were noted on 2026-09-30 and reopened by the user on 2026-10-01; they are scheduled as Work Records in M11.

## What this roadmap does not do

No line-count targets. No documentation gates or verification scripts. No replacement package-wide test suite; canonical smoke, focused regression scripts and real-model runs provide distinct evidence. No milestone deletes a feature from the default set without a recorded user decision.
# Domain docs

This is a single-context repository.

Before investigating or designing:

- Read the root `CONTEXT.md`.
- Read relevant decisions in `docs/adr/`.
- Use the glossary's domain terms consistently.
- Explicitly identify any proposed conflict with an existing ADR.

If a document is absent, continue without requiring its creation.
Code and tests establish current behavior; distinguish that behavior from
proposals and historical design records.

This configuration introduces no separate approval or publication ceremony.
# Development and verification

Use the source, [architecture map](../architecture.md) and [current contracts](../protocols/request-lifecycle.md) to locate a change. Keep the worktree's unrelated edits; this package is often intentionally dirty. In a colocated jj repository, use jj for version-control work and do not create commits, bookmarks or workspaces merely to perform a check.

## Canonical checks

The package-wide ERT suite and `experiments/` were removed on 2026-09-11 and are not rebuilt. Three checks remain: the Kernel check, the canonical smoke set, and the M3 regression set in `scripts/m3-regression.el`, which asserts the execution main line's contracts (admission, inbox, pre-step and request waterfalls, Step facts, turn-stopping).

The Kernel check needs only the repository on the load path:

```sh
emacs -Q --batch -L . -l scripts/kernel-check.el
```

`emacs -Q` does not activate installed packages. Initialize a prepared package directory containing llm's dependencies, then put the pinned llm source checkout on the load path. Replace the two paths below with directories that already exist:

```sh
ELPA=/path/to/prepared/elpa
LLM=/path/to/pinned/llm

emacs -Q --batch \
  --eval "(progn (require 'package) (setq package-user-dir \"$ELPA\" native-comp-enable-subr-trampolines nil) (package-initialize))" \
  -L "$LLM" -L . -L plugins/superchat-cowork-telega \
  -l scripts/smoke.el

emacs -Q --batch \
  --eval "(progn (require 'package) (setq package-user-dir \"$ELPA\" native-comp-enable-subr-trampolines nil) (package-initialize))" \
  -L "$LLM" -L . \
  --eval "(require 'superchat)"
```

The M3 regression set uses the same load path and the smoke fixtures. It exits non-zero on any unexpected result; a test marked `:expected-result :failed` documents a known defect and turns into a failure once that defect is fixed, so remove the marker then. Its two live-provider tests are skipped unless `SMOKE_REAL=1`. The set is owned by the leader agent the user designated: other agents run it and report proposed test changes with evidence, but do not edit it.

```sh
emacs -Q --batch \
  --eval "(progn (require 'package) (setq package-user-dir \"$ELPA\" native-comp-enable-subr-trampolines nil) (package-initialize))" \
  -L "$LLM" -L . -L plugins/superchat-cowork-telega \
  -l scripts/m3-regression.el -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit

# one contract area
  ... -l scripts/m3-regression.el --eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m3/pre-step/")'
```

These batch checks disable native subr trampolines: the mock cases temporarily rebind primitives, which can otherwise trigger a native compiler failure on this Emacs build. The setting affects only the batch process, not the user's configuration.

The focused execution reliability set in `scripts/m5-execution-regression.el` uses the same dependency setup and runs with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-execution/")'`. It covers provider silence clocks, reasoning request serialization and tool-budget finalization without a real model. It is independent of the leader-owned M3/M5 sets and needs no scratch files; its header documents the full command.

The independent tool-feedback set in `scripts/m5-tool-feedback-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-tool-feedback/")'`. It checks exact edits, no-change results, complete expression parsing and recognized eval failures through the real tool loop. It needs no scratch files or model connection.

The request-context set in `scripts/m5-request-context-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-request-context/")'`. It checks the actual provider body and system text for chat, act and edited inspection, including context reuse and budget refusal. It is independent of the protected M3/M5 sets.

The history-material set in `scripts/m5-history-material-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-history-material/")'`. It checks full user and assistant material in the actual SQLite row and next Run's provider input, database reopen, whole-item budget omission and the inspector showing it read-only while sending the edited body. It needs neither scratch files nor a real model.

The final-response set in `scripts/m5-response-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-response/")'`. It checks missing results through the real llm adapter and Runtime, preserving explicit empty strings, visible stream fallback, tool continuation, pending steering, budget metadata and cancellation. It needs neither scratch files nor a real model.

The permission set in `scripts/m6-permissions-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m6-permissions/")'`. Permission levels are simply how the product works now — there is no switch to turn on. It checks the approval seam against a live scripted Run (claiming and delegating answerers, `never`, the paired audit, the open-approval mark, quit, and the `?` details through the real `read-multiple-choice`, which the test loads from source so the input primitive underneath can be stood in for), the permission levels (events written only on change, `custom`, reopen across a closed database, `/permissions`), the buffer-editing tools over a real directory tree with a symlink and an indirect buffer (Superchat's own state is refused, `read-only` refuses edits, where a file lives no longer matters, `save_buffer` conflict rules), scripted tool calls through the real executor (what each level asks and denies, the approved arguments, a cancel while the question is open, a `tools/pre-execute` listener against the fixed level stage, a delegated child Session, `workspace_write`, the policy fragment in the provider request), the Telega plugin's per-request listeners, and that the deleted Authority machinery is really gone. `scripts/read-file-regression.el` (`"^read-file/"`) covers `read_file` the same way, `scripts/operating-note-regression.el` (`"^operating-note/"`) covers when the Emacs operating note is in a request's system text (it captures the prompt's context; the smoke fixture's `:prompt-text` holds the interactions only). `scripts/no-cumulative-limit-regression.el` (`"^no-cumulative/"`) covers the cumulative transport limit being off by default, on the M5 execution regression's virtual clock. `scripts/approval-input-regression.el` (`"^approval-input/"`) covers the rule that an approval is answered only by input that came after it; it starts an interactive child Emacs under a pseudo-terminal for each scenario (`scripts/approval-input-child.el`), because batch Emacs has no input queue, and takes about forty seconds. `scripts/edit-check-regression.el` (`"^edit-check/"`) covers the rule that a structured edit which would unbalance a Lisp buffer is sent back by the tool with the buffer untouched, `scripts/approval-wait-regression.el` (`"^approval-wait/"`) covers the rule that an open approval question stops the attempt's inactivity clock (it runs a real one-second watchdog, so it takes about fifteen seconds), and `scripts/m7-buffer-first-regression.el` (`"^m7/"`) covers buffer-first mutation: `open_file`, the unsaved-changes list (the edited buffer is recognized by its own mark through renames, reused names, reopened buffers, clones, separate Runs and delegated Runs), the retired `write_file` and escalation, `save_buffer` against the approval policy, and the `edit-buffers` level with its former name. Neither needs scratch files or a real model. `scripts/review-screen-regression.el` (`"^review/"`) covers the review screen for unsaved changes: it appears by itself and takes the cursor (after the minibuffer, when that is in use; keys typed before are dropped; `q` gives the cursor back), one buffer at a time in `diff-mode` colors, `s`/`n`/`q`, `s` on a buffer that is gone, `g` redrawing from the current text, inert source-changing keys, file-less and new-file buffers, `/changes` and `superchat-changes-diff` opening it selected with typed-ahead keys dropped, and the option that turns it off. Set `DIFFS_EL_DIR` to a diffs.el checkout to include the diffs.el renderer case; without it that case is skipped. `scripts/editor-mode-regression.el` (`"^editor-mode/"`) covers editor mode: the chat View in a side window beside the file (opening it, a second conversation in the same window, `superchat-act` without taking focus, `superchat-send-region`, the plan panel keeping its own place, another window already showing the View left open), agent mode as before and in the conversation's own tab, a Run from the chat leaving the file's window alone while the review screen takes it, and closing a conversation shown only beside the file (by the View's own ownership marker, before the tab's, asking once while a Run works). Batch Emacs has working tabs and side windows in its one frame; the test that another window is left open runs the opening as an interactive Emacs would. `scripts/display-switch-regression.el` (`"^display-switch/"`, loads the editor-mode suite for its fixture) covers the switch between the two modes: `C-c C-w` in the chat, into the conversation's own tab and back to the tab it came from with the unsent input kept, the last mode used for later openings, a closed editing tab, and the command called from the file's window. `scripts/history-messages-regression.el` (`"^history-messages/"`) covers earlier turns as real messages: every provider request is serialized with llm.el's own OpenAI-compatible request builder, the form DeepSeek receives, and the checks are on those messages (calls grouped by the reply that made them, ids that are the Tape's execution ids, named arguments and the note for arguments not kept, injected material and a compaction summary as user messages, calls never separated from their results under a small budget, an unchanged prefix within a Run, the request evidence, recovery after a too-long request, compaction's own request keeping one text message, the input as typed, and the request inspector). `scripts/context-recovery-regression.el` (`"^context-recovery/"`) covers a Run resumed after compaction: the question and the Run's own calls sent once, the system text kept, the evidence naming the rebuilt View and placing the summary first. `scripts/user-saves-regression.el` (`"^user-saves/"`, loads the history-messages suite for its fixture) covers the next turn learning what the user saved or discarded: the note once, between the previous answer and the new question, any save counting but not one made while a tool runs, nothing with the `changes` row off, and the evidence rebuilding it. `scripts/shared-conversation-regression.el` (`"^shared-conversation/"`, loads the history-messages suite for its fixture) covers M10 slice 2: in-place ask, act and rewrite in the conversation the chat window shows, their requests without earlier turns (a follow-up with its own exchange), the chat window showing the turn with a way back and carrying it later, waiting while the chat runs, the chat's associated source unchanged, and `/changes`. `scripts/llm-warning-regression.el` (`"^llm-warning/"`) covers `superchat-llm-warning-message`: llm sees its nonfree warning off for superchat's request when the option is nil, and its own setting unchanged.

The system-contribution set in `scripts/m5-guidance-regression.el` uses the same setup with `--eval '(ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit "^m5-guidance/")'`. It checks that a tool rewrite refreshes dynamic system blocks in place across real attempts, that a base or static block with equal text is never edited, that an empty block keeps its position, and that a whole-system replacement by middleware ends automatic refresh. It needs neither scratch files nor a real model.

The prepared package directory must include the dependencies needed by the pinned llm, including compat, plz, plz-event-source and plz-media-type. A failed bare `locate-library` does not establish that a dependency is uninstalled. `-L` affects later loads; `-Q` still skips the user's init file.

Byte-compile touched modules, or all top-level modules for a cross-module change. Keep generated bytecode outside the source tree so a later load cannot accidentally test stale code:

```sh
SUPERCHAT_BYTECODE=$(mktemp -d)
export SUPERCHAT_BYTECODE
emacs -Q --batch \
  --eval "(progn (require 'package) (setq package-user-dir \"$ELPA\" native-comp-enable-subr-trampolines nil) (package-initialize))" \
  -L "$LLM" -L . \
  --eval '(setq byte-compile-dest-file-function
                (lambda (source)
                  (expand-file-name
                   (concat (file-name-base source) ".elc")
                   (getenv "SUPERCHAT_BYTECODE"))))' \
  -f batch-byte-compile superchat*.el ob-superchat.el
```

`scripts/smoke.el` owns the case list and its header explains mock versus live-model execution. Compare with `scripts/smoke-expected/`; investigate a mismatch before changing its baseline. Explain any intentional baseline change in the relevant change record; older explanations remain in the [historical migration log](../../.wayfinder/map.md). Source lookup, a mock-provider smoke, a real-provider request and interactive UI acceptance are different evidence: report which were actually exercised.

For documentation-only changes, check local links and runnable examples and confirm production sources and smoke scripts are unchanged. Compile or behavioral checks are appropriate when the document makes a new executable claim. No commit, publication or evidence-ledger ceremony is required to run these checks.

## Package and source conventions

`superchat.el` owns the authoritative Version/Package-Requires header; `superchat-pkg.el` is the installation descriptor and can lag during development. Do not duplicate package headers across ordinary modules.

Keep lexical binding enabled. Internal helpers use `superchat--name`, public functions use `superchat-name`; match existing formatting. Optional dependencies use `(require 'foo nil t)`, and files using external llm/mcp functions declare them. Optional product capabilities are queried through `superchat-service-get`, not guessed from a function's presence.

The transport is llm.el; the gptel migration is complete. Do not restore the removed gptel curl cleanup advice. `superchat-agent.el` is not an implemented module in this checkout; a proposed future file name in a design is not an existing load target.

The root `skills/` directory is product prompt material consumed by `>name`. It is unrelated to coding-agent skill tooling. Local discovery reads flat files in `superchat-skills-directory`; standard directory examples are not automatically discovered. See the [Skills guide](../SKILLS_QUICKSTART.md) for supported paths rather than assuming an import/export module exists.

## UI and editor conventions

For interactive widget.el controls read the `emacs-widget-controls` skill. Emacs 31 controls need explicit `:keymap widget-keymap`; avoid the non-existent `widget-button-pressed-hook` and follow the skill's notification deferral and keymap checks.

Plan review and progress use the `superchat-plan.el` TextUI side window, not inline widgets in chat. Frames wrap a column flex in a one-element list; a bare element list renders as one inline row there.

Source preview is not an edit. Inline rewrite accepts one atomic Change Set; editor buffers remain owned according to the [Session close protocol](../protocols/session-editor-workspace-close.md), never by name, project or whichever View is current.

## Where to write

Use [docs/README.md](../README.md) for document placement and [the local tracker](issue-tracker.md) for implementation issues. Keep historical records distinct from current contracts. README.md and README_cn.md are paired user guides; update both for shared behavior changes.
# Decision: 没人看着的工作结束后，在模式行留一个不会自己消失的标记

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。上一份报告建议"先只做 Emacs 内的，在模式行上留一个不会自己消失的标记，点开能看到是哪条工作记录做了什么；系统桌面通知作为可选项以后再加"，并问通知用什么形式；用户答"先执行下一步"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

到点的定时任务和被唤醒的工作记录由 Superchat 自己开始执行。2026-10-07 的裁定让这类执行不显示审阅屏、不拿光标。它们结束时用户能看到的只有回显区的一行，下一次按键就没了：

- 留下未保存改动时，有一行"N buffers edited by this Run are unsaved"（`superchat-changes--announce`）。
- 用户不在那段对话的窗口里时，有一行"run … completed (session …)"（`superchat--notify-run-terminal`）。
- 执行失败、且没有改任何东西时，只有后一行。

2026-10-07 的报告把"自动执行留下的未保存改动只有回显区一句话，容易错过"列为没解决的事。

## Decision

Superchat 自己开始的执行结束时，留下一条通知，模式行上出现一个标记，写着待看的条数。标记一直在，直到用户看过或清掉。

- **哪些执行**：和"不显示审阅屏"用同一个判断（`superchat-changes--unattended-run-p`，从对话的持久记录里读）：到点的定时任务开始的执行，被唤醒的工作记录的那次执行。用户自己发起的执行不留通知。
- **哪些结局**：每一种，包括失败、取消、超时。失败且什么都没改的那种最容易错过。
- **标记**：`global-mode-string` 里的一项，形如 `[Superchat: 2]`。几条通知是一个标记加一个数。鼠标点它，或 `M-x superchat-changes-notices`，打开列表。
- **列表**：一行一条，写着结束时间、是谁开始的（哪条工作记录、哪个定时任务）、怎么结束的、留下几个未保存的 buffer、在哪段对话。`RET` 去那段对话并去掉这条通知；`d` 去掉这条；`D` 全部去掉。
- **不会自己消失**指：只有上面三个键会去掉通知。用户之后自己发起的执行、打开列表本身，都不会。

不变的：自动执行不显示审阅屏、不拿光标；回显区原有的两行；保存仍然只由用户做。

## 实现中定下的几点

- 挂在现有的"执行结束"事件（`turn-completed`）上，放在现有的 `changes` 插件里，没有新增插件行。M3 回归集钉着一份插件名单，那个文件不归实现者改。
- **没有用"套一层 Sink"。** [同日的提案](../proposed/2026-10-10-source-turn-destination.md)把这一项写成套在现有 Sink 外面的一层。查代码后改了主意：`turn-completed` 对每一种结局都发，而 Sink 只在有最终回答或出错时被调用；"是不是没人发起的"已经有一个从持久记录判断、有测试的函数，不需要在造 Sink 的那一刻知道。提案里"去向用包一层来组合"那条原则说的是用户为某一次请求选的去向；这里是产品按一个记录里的事实给的默认行为，两者不是一回事。提案已同步改写。
- 通知只存在这个 Emacs 的内存里，重启后没有。它指向的未保存 buffer 也活不过重启；重启前没跑完的唤醒由 `superchat-work--report-cut-off` 在主对话里交代，工作记录自己列着它的各次执行。
- "留下几个未保存的 buffer"是执行结束那一刻数的，之后用户保存了也不会变。
- 没有另加回显区文字，也没有响铃。

## Alternatives considered

**系统桌面通知。** 这次没做，留作以后的可选项：它离开了 Emacs，需要按平台分别实现。

**自动执行也显示审阅屏，只是不选中它。** 2026-10-07 已经否掉：仍然会占掉用户正在用的窗口。

**把通知写进对话记录，重启后还在。** 没选：需要再加一种"用户已看过"的记录来清它，而重启后该交代的事已经有别的路径。

## Consequences

验收：`scripts/unattended-notice-regression.el`（8 个测试；到点任务和唤醒记录走真实路径）。

- 模式行多出一项。没有待看通知时它是空的。
- 自动执行很多的用户会看到数字一直涨，要自己清。
# Decision: 调用技能的那条消息也说明用户在哪里

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。上一份报告列出"调用技能的那条消息不带'你当前在哪个 buffer'和窗口布局"这个早就存在的缺口，建议先修；用户答"先修缺口"。

## Problem

调用技能的消息（`>技能` 写在开头或中间，三种类型都一样）送给模型时，没有 `[Emacs origin]` 那一段（当前 buffer、文件、major mode、光标位置、用户明确选中的文字），也没有 `[Emacs windows]` 窗口布局。普通消息有。

原因（代码事实）：技能调用之后，分发处把技能和用户输入合成的文字放进这一轮的 `prompt`，它作为 `:chat-prompt-override` 到达收集器。来源收集器和窗口收集器看到这个标记就不出声，说明文字写的理由是"这是用户在检查器里预览并确认过的正文，再往里加会重复，也会把用户删掉的选区加回来"。但用户预览过的请求另有标记 `:chat-prepared-context`，而且带这个标记的请求根本不运行收集器（`superchat-pipeline--collect-context`）。所以这两个收集器里对 `:chat-prompt-override` 的判断，实际只拦住了技能消息。

真实模型上的后果（2026-10-10，沙箱 Emacs）：`用一句话说明 >duty 周五要交什么`，DeepSeek 回答"我没有关于周五要交什么的信息"；消息里写明 buffer 名之后 DeepSeek 3/3、本地 Qwen 1/1 答对。

探针确认系统提示没有缺段：技能消息的系统提示短，是因为技能只声明了少数工具，相应的工具说明不带；工具相同时系统提示只多出技能的人设。

## Decision

来源收集器和窗口收集器只在请求带 `:chat-prepared-context`（用户预览过）时不出声。技能固定了正文的消息和普通消息一样带上来源、选中的文字和窗口布局。

不变的：用户在检查器里预览并发送的请求；技能合成的正文本身；系统提示；历史和附件的收集（本来就对技能消息生效）。

## Alternatives considered

**代理类技能不再固定正文，只让提示类技能固定。** 没选：只修了一半，提示类技能的消息仍然不知道用户在哪里。

**给技能消息另加一个"带来源"的开关。** 没选：标记已经有两个，问题正是把它们当成了一个。

## Consequences

验收：`scripts/skill-context-regression.el`（5 个测试，经完整发送路径）。

- 所有技能消息送给模型的内容变长了：多出来源一段和窗口一段，有选区时还有选中的文字。
- `scripts/skill-once-regression.el` 里一条断言原来假定"用户的话是消息第一行"，改为"出现且只出现一次"。
# Decision: 写在消息中间的代理技能只管这一条消息

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。上一份报告末尾问"是所有写在正文里的代理/计划类技能都只管这一轮，还是另用一种写法"，并建议"写在开头是切换整段对话（现状不变），写在正文里只管这一轮"；用户答"好，下一步"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

`>技能` 调用一个 `agent` 或 `plan` 类型的技能时，分发处在调用之后把它变成整段对话的预设（`superchat--activate-capability`），之后每条消息都带它的人设和工具。`prompt` 类型的技能一直只作用于调用它的那一条消息。

同一天的[另一条决定](2026-10-10-triggers-anywhere-in-the-message.md)让 `>技能` 可以写在消息中间。gptel 的 presets 视频里，写在句子里的预设只对那一次请求生效；用户要求学它对架构有益的部分，不抛开 Superchat 的结构。

代码事实：

- `superchat-preset-apply` 的参数是（预设，这一轮），它只改这一轮：模型、工具、把人设放进系统提示、跑 `:pre`。切换整段对话是分发处另做的一步。
- 带技能的那一轮不套用对话当前的预设（发送路径上的条件是"这一轮没有技能"）。
- Runtime 拒绝"请求的能力名和代理预设声明的能力名不一致"的请求（`superchat-runtime--validate-profile-capability`）；请求的能力名来自对话当前的状态。探针：不切换对话而调用一个声明了 `capability:` 的代理技能，报 "Capability chat conflicts with Agent Profile …"。
- 计划的批准在对话上进行：批准时如果对话当前的预设是计划类，会把它清掉（`superchat-plan.el`）。

## Decision

1. **`agent` 类型的技能写在消息中间时，只作用于这一条消息。** 这一条请求带它的人设、工具和模型；对话的预设不变，不写 `capability.activated` 记录；下一条消息和调用之前一样。
2. **写在消息开头时不变**：切换整段对话。
3. **`plan` 类型的技能写在哪里都切换对话。**
4. `prompt` 类型不变（本来就只作用于一条消息）。

## 实现中定下的几点

- 解析点在认出正文里的技能时，在这一轮的状态上记 `:skill-from-body`；分发处据此跳过切换（`superchat--skill-for-this-message-only-p`）。没有新的请求路径。
- 只管一条消息的那次请求，能力名用技能自己声明的（没声明就沿用对话的），否则 Runtime 会拒绝它。这次执行因此记在技能的能力名下，而对话的能力名没变。
- 对话已经在另一个代理模式里时，这一条消息只带被点名技能的人设和工具，不带对话当前模式的；下一条恢复对话当前模式。这是"带技能的那一轮不套用对话预设"这条现有规则的结果，没有另加逻辑。
- 计划类不做"只管一条"：提出计划、批准、退出计划模式是对话上的几步，一条消息里做不完。
- 回显区在只管一条时写 "🎯 名字: this message only"。

## 不切换时没有的东西

- 技能声明的 `plugins:` 不会挂载。挂载发生在切换对话那一步。
- 对话记录里没有 `capability.activated`；"这一条用了哪个技能"只在这次请求的 `:preset` 上。

## Alternatives considered

**另用一种写法表示"只这一次"。** 没选：用户接受了"位置决定范围"；多一种符号要多记一条规则。

**计划类也只管一条。** 没选：见上，批准流程依赖对话处于计划模式。

**只管一条时仍然切换、结束后切回。** 没选：切换会写记录、退役旧的作用域并取消其中的执行；为一条消息做两次代价和风险都不对等。

## Consequences

验收：`scripts/skill-once-regression.el`（5 个测试，都经完整发送路径，读脚本模型实际收到的请求）。

- 以前在消息开头调用代理技能的用法不变。
- 想让代理技能管整段对话，必须把它写在开头。
- 依赖 `plugins:` 的代理技能写在中间时功能不全，见上一节。
# Decision: 在对话里用 `>annotate` 让这一条的回答挂到 buffer 上

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。用户要求"优化一下 annotation 的效果，以及它的目的"，并给了 gptel-annotate 的链接。上一份报告列了三件要定的事和建议：挂到叫出这段对话时所在的 buffer、也允许在标记后写文件名；带对话历史；这一步不给工具。用户答："1. 同意建议。2. 可以戴。3. 不给工具。""实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

[批注](2026-10-10-remarks-hung-on-the-text.md)只有一个单独的命令 `superchat-annotate`：它不带对话历史，模型只看得到那段文字和一句指令。gptel-annotate 的说明里写明，它最有用的用法是在一段已经有上下文的对话里叫出批注，单独当命令用是"盲的、一次性的"。例如先和模型讨论一份纪要哪里有问题，再说"把这些标到纪要上"。

## Decision

对话里的一条消息写了 `>annotate`，这一条就照常作为这段对话的一条消息发出（带历史、Workspace 规矩、你所在的位置），它的回答除了照常显示和记录在对话里，还按批注的规则挂到目标 buffer 上。

- **目标**：`>annotate` 后面没有名字时，是叫出这段对话时所在的 buffer，取自这次请求冻结下来的来源，发出之后切窗口不会改变它。`>annotate 名字` 或 `>annotate "带空格的名字"` 指定一个 buffer 或文件；文件还没打开就打开它。
- **历史**：带。这一条和别的对话消息走同一条路。
- **工具**：不给，对话正处在某个代理技能的模式里时也不给。
- **只管这一条**：下一条消息照常，对话的模式不变。挂上的批注留着。
- **定位、显示、不改文件**：和命令完全一样，用的是同一段代码。对同一个 buffer 再要一次，旧的批注被换掉。

## 实现中定下的几点

- **`>annotate` 不是技能，是这一条回答的去向。** 解析点认出它，记在这一轮输入上（`:destination annotate`），在 `>技能` 之前解析。所以 `annotate` 这个名字被占用：同名的技能在对话里叫不出来。
- **Superchat 自己发出的消息从不认它**，写在开头也不认。定时任务和被唤醒的工作记录发的文字是模型写的，不应该能往你的 buffer 上挂东西。这比 `/命令`"自动消息只认开头"更严。
- **开头的命令优先**：消息以 `/命令` 开头时，后面的 `>annotate` 是普通文字。
- **后面那个词算不算目标**：加了双引号的一律算，找不到就报错，不发请求。没加引号的只有在它正好是一个现有 buffer 的名字或一个存在的文件时才算，否则它是你要说的话的一部分。
- **只写 `>annotate` 没有别的话**：用命令的默认指令（`superchat-annotate-default-instruction`）。
- **去向是包一层**（[结构提案](../proposed/2026-10-10-source-turn-destination.md)的原则 3）：对话自己的 Sink 照常被调用，外面一层在回答到达时再挂批注。所以回答的原文（一行行 `行号 | 引用 | 意见`）仍然显示在对话里。
- **格式要求放在系统提示的最后**，在人设和 Workspace 规矩之后，并说明前面关于"怎么回答"的要求不适用于这一条。带行号的原文作为附带内容放在这条用户消息里。两样都由一个新的收集器给出，没有第二条请求路径。
- **批注整个 buffer**，不看当时有没有选区；超过 `superchat-annotate-warn-lines` 行时不另外提示。
- **记录里留着 `>annotate`。** 发给模型的这一条不带标记，但对话记录里存的是带标记的文字（标记放在末尾，指定过的目标跟在后面）。所以之后各轮的历史里看得出这一条要的是批注，"重新生成"会重新批注，"编辑上一条"会把标记放回输入栏。
- **之后的消息会被告知"那件事做完了"。** 这段对话发过批注消息之后，它后面的每条普通消息的系统提示末尾多一句：之前那条的回答已经由 Superchat 挂到某个 buffer 上，原文没改，不要再把意见写进 buffer。只有至少一条意见真的挂上之后才说：请求失败、被取消、或者回答里一条都没挂上，都不说。它按对话记：同一个窗口里换成另一段对话，那段对话不会被告知。没发过批注消息的对话没有这句话。这是真实模型跑出问题后加的，见下面。
- **结果说在回显区**：挂上几条、没挂上几条、看不懂几行，以及是哪个 buffer。目标 buffer 在回答到达前被关掉时，说"要批注的 buffer 不在了"，不报错，回答仍在对话里。

## Alternatives considered

**flymake。** 用户明确说不用。

**做成一个技能。** 没选：技能改的是"模型按什么身份回答"，这里改的是"回答去哪里"；做成技能会走切换对话模式那条路。

**把回答从对话里隐藏，只留一行"已挂上 N 条"。** 没做：记录里无论如何存着模型的原文，没挂上的行留在对话里正好能看到。要不要隐藏是另一个界面上的决定。

## Acceptance criteria

`scripts/annotate-chat-regression.el`，19 个测试，都真的在一段对话里发消息，读脚本模型收到的请求和 buffer 上挂了什么：

- 回答挂在叫出对话的 buffer 上；请求里有带行号的原文、上一轮的问答、你所在的位置，没有 `>annotate` 这个标记，没有工具；格式要求在系统提示的最后；原文和"已修改"状态不变；回答仍显示在对话里。
- 下一条消息是普通消息：没有格式要求，没有带行号的原文，工具回来了。
- 只写 `>annotate` 时用默认指令。
- 后面写 buffer 名或加引号的文件名时，挂到那个 buffer 上；不是名字的词留在消息里；加了引号却找不到时不发请求。
- Superchat 自己发的消息不认这个标记；标记必须独立成词；开头的命令优先。
- 代理技能生效时：格式要求排在人设之后，没有工具，对话的模式没变。
- 目标 buffer 被关掉时说出来、不报错；什么都没挂上时说出来；再要一次换掉旧的。
- 下一条普通消息被告知之前的回答已经挂到哪个 buffer 上，历史里那一条带着标记和目标名；没批注过的对话没有这句话。
- "重新生成"会重新批注；"编辑上一条"把带标记的文字放回输入栏。
- 请求失败、或者一条都没挂上时，下一条消息不会被告知"已经挂上"；同一个窗口里的另一段对话也不会。
- 批注这一条的请求能从记录里重建（和冒烟测试对普通消息用的是同一个检查，结果 `reproduced`，0 条违规）。

对新代码做了 29 处变异（解析 6、分发 11、去向 5、收集器 7），全部被发现。

冒烟测试的期望值文件 `scripts/smoke-expected/V.txt` 随这次改了：已登记的收集器从 14 个变成 15 个，因为新加了一个。上一次改这个数是加工作记录收集器的那次。

## 真实模型经过对话的结果

沙箱 Emacs、替身用户，场景 ANH（在 `.scratch` 下的测试台里，不受版本管理）：从一份 15 行的周会纪要叫出对话，连发三条——先问"决定一节有哪几条和问题一节对不上"，再发"把你刚才说的这些问题标到纪要上 `>annotate`"，最后发"只回答两个字：你好"。

**第一批，加"那件事做完了"这句话之前：**

| | 批注那一条 | 下一条普通消息 |
|---|---|---|
| DeepSeek，3 遍 | 挂上 2、6、5 条，行号全部正好对，没有挂不上或看不懂的 | 2 遍回答"你好"；**1 遍没有回答，而是用编辑工具把意见写进了纪要**（buffer 被改，未保存） |
| 本地 Qwen，关掉思考，1 遍 | 挂上 4 条，行号全对，67 秒 | 回答"你好" |
| 本地 Qwen，开着思考，1 遍 | 挂上 3 条，行号全对，279 秒 | 回答"你好" |

出问题的那一遍里，历史上一条存的是去掉标记后的"把这些问题标到纪要上"，后面跟着两行 `行号 | 引用 | 意见`，没有任何地方说这两行已经挂到纪要上了。下一轮的模型手里有编辑工具，就自己去"标"了。这是从那一遍的请求和回答读出来的推论，没有单独验证。

**第二批，记录里留着标记、之后的消息被告知"已做完"之后：**

| | 批注那一条 | 下一条普通消息 |
|---|---|---|
| DeepSeek，5 遍 | 挂上 9、8、11、7、7 条，行号全部正好对，没有挂不上或看不懂的 | 5 遍都回答"你好"，原文没有被改 |
| 本地 Qwen，关掉思考，1 遍 | 挂上 3 条，行号全对，49 秒 | 回答"你好" |

**第三批，"已经挂上"改成挂上之后才记、按对话记之后（最终的代码）：** DeepSeek 2 遍，挂上 10、7 条，行号全部正好对；下一条消息的请求里有"那件事做完了"这句话，回答都是"你好"。

每一遍里，批注那一条的请求都带着上一轮的问题、带行号的原文和格式要求，没有 `>annotate` 这个标记；意见的内容接着上一轮的讨论。

这些数字能说明和不能说明的：

- 修之前 3 遍里出 1 次，修之后 7 遍里 0 次（第二批 5 遍、第三批 2 遍）。样本很小，只能说没有再看到，不能说不会再发生。
- 本地 Qwen 开着思考的那一遍没有撞到 8192 的上下文上限（纪要短），所以"上下文用完时明说没有批注"这条路在对话里没有被真实模型走到；它只有命令那边的真实运行和这里的脚本测试作证。
- 只测了一份 15 行的纪要。代码材料、长文件、对话已经很长的情况都没有测。

## 没有解决的

- **"那件事做完了"这句话只记在当前这个 Emacs 里。** 重启之后，历史里还有带 `>annotate` 的那一条和它的几行回答，但后面的消息不再被告知它们已经挂过；挂上的批注本身也不会在重启后留下。
- 回答的原文留在对话里，也进入之后的历史。
- 没有测过的路径：`superchat-act` 带前缀参数打开的请求检查器里写 `>annotate`（检查器预览的那份请求不会有格式要求和带行号的原文）；标记后面写不加引号的相对文件名（它相对于对话窗口的当前目录解析，不是 Workspace 根）；同一条消息里既有 `>技能` 又有 `>annotate`。
- 补全里 `>` 的候选还没有 `annotate`。
- 一次只批注一个 buffer，批注整个 buffer。
- gptel-annotate 的"模型给出替换文字、一键接受"和"保留多轮批注来回切换"没有做。前者会改文件，应该走改写的审阅路径。
- 没有真人在键盘前用过。
# Decision: 批注——把模型的意见挂在它说的那处原文上

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。[同日的提案](../proposed/2026-10-10-source-turn-destination.md)第 4 项写的是"先在 DeepSeek 和本地 Qwen 上测定位准不准，再决定做不做"。上一份报告建议先停一下由用户试用，用户答"先做简单的实验，再实现"。实验结果见下；"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

让模型看一份配置、一段纪要或一段代码并逐处给意见时，Superchat 只能给一整段回答：在对话里，或就地问的浮层里挂在一行下面。读的人要自己把每条意见和原文对上。

把意见逐条挂到原文上，需要模型说清每条意见说的是哪里。弱模型能不能稳定做到，动手前不知道。

## 实验

不经过 Superchat，直接问模型。三段材料：一份 32 行的 Emacs 配置、一份 17 行的会议纪要、`superchat-parser.el` 的 87 行代码。两种指位置的写法：

- **只引原文**：`"原文片段" => 意见`
- **行号 + 原文**：给模型看的原文每行带行号，回答写成 `行号 | 原文片段 | 意见`

DeepSeek（`deepseek-v4-flash`）每种 3 遍，本地 Qwen（`qwen/qwen3.8-27b`，LM Studio）每种 2 遍。数的是批注条数。

| 材料 | 模型 | 只引原文：引用在原文里找得到 | 行号 + 原文：行号正好对 | 差两行以内 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 配置 | DeepSeek | 96/96 | 96/96 | 96/96 |
| 配置 | Qwen | 64/64 | 64/64 | 64/64 |
| 纪要 | DeepSeek | 36/36 | 39/39 | 39/39 |
| 纪要 | Qwen | 20/20 | 20/20 | 20/20 |
| 代码 | DeepSeek | 47/47，其中 13 条的引用在原文里出现不止一次 | 33/39 | 38/39 |
| 代码 | Qwen，关掉思考 | 16/27 | 20/21 | 21/21 |

看到的三件事：

1. **只引原文在代码上不行。** DeepSeek 的引用都找得到，但 47 条里 13 条指不清是哪一处（`(when (and input` 在那段代码里出现 5 次）。Qwen 的 27 条里有 11 条引用在原文里根本没有。
2. **行号会差一行，引用能纠正它。** DeepSeek 在代码上行号没对上的 6 条里，5 条的引用就在相邻的行，1 条的引用是编的。
3. **格式不是问题。** 两个模型共 570 条，569 条是按要求的格式写的。

两条限制：

- **Qwen 在代码上的数字只在关掉思考时成立。** 开着思考时 4 次调用都是空回答。重发一次并保存完整回复后查到：LM Studio 给这个模型加载的上下文是 8192；那次请求输入 1486、输出 6704，输出全部是思考，结束原因是 `length`，正文为空。前 4 次没有保存结束原因，"同一个原因"是按耗时相同（8 到 9 分钟）推出来的。关掉思考（请求里带 `reasoning_effort: "none"`，回复里思考用量为 0）之后每次 23 到 48 秒。
- 材料最长 87 行。更长的没有测。

## Decision

新增命令 `superchat-annotate`：有选区时批注选区碰到的那些整行，没有时批注整个 buffer。

- **问法**：给模型看带行号的原文，要求每条意见一行，`LINE | QUOTE | REMARK`。
- **定位**：先在模型说的那一行找引用；找不到，在上下各两行以内找，近的先找；还找不到，这条意见不挂在任何地方。选区之外的行永远不找。引用为空的也不挂。
- **被省略号截短的引用**：引用照原样找不到、而它的开头或结尾是 `...` 或 `…` 时，去掉省略号，用剩下的部分再找一次。找到的仍然是 buffer 里那几行上的文字。
- **不挂的不丢**：没挂上的意见，以及回答里不成格式的行，都记着数并能在列表里看到。回显区说"N 条挂上，M 条未定位，K 行看不懂"。
- **空回答明说**：模型什么都没回时，说"模型没有给出批注：回答是空的"，不显示成"没有意见"。
- **不改文件**：批注是 overlay。buffer 的文字、"已修改"状态都不变，也不保存任何东西。
- **显示**：被指到的文字加波浪线，意见显示在那一行下面，成一块（见下面"显示改为挂在行下"）。`M-n`、`M-p`（光标在批注上时）或 `superchat-annotate-next`、`superchat-annotate-previous` 在批注之间走；`superchat-annotate-list` 列出全部；`superchat-annotate-clear` 清掉。对同一些行再问一次，旧的批注被换掉。
- **规矩和工具**：带这个 Workspace 的规矩，不带"用户说记住时怎么办"的说明，不给任何工具。规矩后面跟一句话：管"怎么回答"的规矩不适用于这次，管"文字本身"的规矩可以被批注指出来。

## 实现中定下的几点

- **单独的命令，不是写在消息里的去向标记。** 提案把它写成"一个新的 Sink"；它确实是一个新的 Sink，但由一个命令发起，和改写选区一样。在消息里选择去向是以后的事。
- **用 overlay，不用 flymake。** 视频里用的是 flymake。它要在每个 buffer 里登记后端并打开 `flymake-mode`，而 Org 和普通文本的 buffer 通常没开。
- **新的入口名 `inline-annotate`，登记在现有的 `inline-rewrite` 插件行里，没有新增插件行。** 不复用 `inline-rewrite` 这个入口名，因为它的请求会带上"你的整个回答会替换进用户的文件"这句专给改写的话。M3 回归集钉着一份插件名单，那个文件不归实现者改。
- **不强行关掉思考。** 对 `llm-openai-compatible` 这种后端，关思考需要在 `superchat-llm-reasoning-parameter-overrides` 里有那个端点的声明，否则请求在发出前就报错。2026-10-10 查过，用户的配置和测试台里都没有本地端点的声明。批注沿用全局的思考设置；思考把输出额度用完时，靠"空回答明说"让用户看到。
- **空回答的占位文字有了名字。** 传输层把空回答换成 `[Empty response from llm]` 交给下游，原来是写在代码里的一行字。现在是常量 `superchat-llm-empty-response-text`，值不变，批注靠它认出空回答。
- **Sink 拿不到结束原因。** 回答因为长度用尽而为空时，只能说"回答是空的"，说不出"因为长度用尽"。
- **"不给工具"有两处**：请求自己写了，入口名也列在不给工具的清单里。和改写一样。单独去掉其中一处，测试不会变红；两处都去掉才会。
- 超过 300 行（`superchat-annotate-warn-lines`）时，回显区提示定位只在 87 行以内的材料上测过。请求照发。

## 显示改为挂在行下

用户看了实际效果后说："优化一下 annotation 的效果"，并附了 gptel-annotate 的截图（意见在被批注的那一行下面，成一块，前面有引导箭头）。我去查 flymake 能不能直接画出这个样子时，用户说："我不想用 flymake"。所以仍然用自己的 overlay 画。

- 意见显示在它说的那一行下面：引导符 `└─▶`，后面是意见；超过 `superchat-annotate-remark-width`（默认 72 列）就折行，续行对齐；整块按那一行的缩进缩进（最多 16 列）。
- 同一行的几条意见共用一个 overlay，按它们所指文字在原文里的先后叠放，与模型给出的先后无关。清掉其中一条，其余的还在。
- 光标停在行尾时仍显示在行尾，不会跑到意见块的后面。
- 旧的"行尾"样式保留为选项：`superchat-annotate-remark-style` 设为 `eol`。
- 定位规则、不改文件、请求内容都没有变，所以这次没有重跑真实模型。

证据：`scripts/annotate-regression.el` 新增 6 个测试（共 24 个）；对新的显示代码做 10 处变异，全部被发现。没有证实的：我只在批处理里把排出来的文字打印出来看过，没有人在真实的 Emacs 窗口里看过颜色、折行和光标。窗口比 72 列窄时，意见块会被 Emacs 再折一次。

## Alternatives considered

**只引原文，不带行号。** 实验否掉：弱模型在代码上有四成引用是编的，强模型有四分之一指不清。

**只用行号，不要引用。** 没有测。行号会差一行，没有引用就没法知道差了，也没法纠正。

**让模型用工具逐条提交批注。** 没有做：批注不需要工具，多一轮往返；弱模型的工具调用更不稳。

**flymake。** 见上。

## Acceptance criteria

`scripts/annotate-regression.el`，都真的发请求给脚本模型，再读 buffer 上挂了什么、模型收到了什么：

- 意见挂在它的引用上，在模型说的那一行；行号差一行、两行时由引用纠正。
- 引用在三行之外、引用是编的、引用为空：不挂，列为未定位，列表里看得到。
- 选区之外的行不挂，哪怕离得够近。
- 行号从选区的第一行数起；选区被扩成整行；文件末尾的换行不多出一个空行。
- buffer 的文字和修改计数不变；清掉批注也不变。
- 不成格式的行被数出来，不被丢掉。
- 空回答、没有任何成格式的行：各有一句明说的话，不留"正在批注"的提示。
- 请求带规矩一次，后面是批注自己的那句话，不是改写的那句，没有"记住"的说明；没有规矩时什么都不加。
- 请求不给模型工具。

## Risks

- **规矩里要求"每次回答先写一行思路"时，格式会不会被打乱。** 改写在同一条规矩上 3 遍里 3 遍出错，后来靠规矩后面的那句话修好。批注加了对应的话，脚本测试只能证明这句话在请求里。
- **真实模型经过这个命令的结果**：见下一节。
- 87 行以上的材料没有测。
- 意见本身说得对不对，实验和测试都不评判。

## 真实模型经过命令的结果

2026-10-10，沙箱 Emacs，经过 `superchat-annotate` 命令，四个场景：配置 32 行（ANG）、会议纪要（ANN）、代码 87 行（ANC）、会议纪要加一条规矩"每次回答的第一行先写思路"（ANR）。每一遍 buffer 的文字都没有变，没有一条批注挂在模型没说的地方。

**DeepSeek，各 3 遍**

| 场景 | 挂上的条数 | 行号正好对 | 没挂上 / 看不懂 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 配置 | 32、32、32 | 全部 | 无 |
| 纪要 | 8、9、9 | 全部 | 无 |
| 纪要 + 规矩 | 11、8、10 | 全部 | 1 遍有 1 行看不懂（`7,13 \| …`，一条意见两个行号） |
| 代码，第一批 | 17、16、18 | 16、16、17 | 2 遍各 1 条没挂上（引用末尾带 `...`） |
| 代码，第二批 | 13、20、15 | 12、20、11 | 4、6、2 条没挂上 |

- 带规矩的场景 3 遍里没有一遍把"思路"写进回答。
- "被省略号截短的引用"是看了第一批之后加的。第二批里模型一次也没有这样写，所以这条规则只有脚本测试作证。
- 代码第二批 60 条里 12 条没挂上，对照原始回答和源码逐条看过：7 条是模型把引用用双引号包起来、把里面的引号写成 `\"`；2 条是引用里有竖线（正则的"或"），一行被切错了位置；1 条把一个反斜杠写成两个；1 条引用为空；1 条大小写和原文不同。两批差别大，各 3 遍不足以给出稳定的比例。

**本地 Qwen，各 1 遍（代码多跑了几遍）**

| 场景 | 结果 | 用时 |
|---|---|---|
| 配置 | 32 条全部挂上，行号全对 | 125 秒 |
| 纪要 | 10 条全部挂上，行号全对 | 116 秒 |
| 纪要 + 规矩 | 10 条全部挂上，行号全对 | 200 秒 |
| 代码，思考开着 | 一条都没有；回显区是 `superchat: no annotations: [Missing final response from llm]` | 551 秒 |
| 代码，思考关掉，第 1 遍 | 模型给 12 条，挂上 9 条（行号全对），3 条引用里的反斜杠和源码不同 | 88 秒 |
| 代码，思考关掉，第 2 遍 | 模型给了 299 行、约 17900 个字符，6 行成格式并挂上，其余记为看不懂 | 批注本身 356 秒 |

- "思考开着"那一遍保存了完整回复：结束原因 `length`，输出 6610 个 token 全是思考，合计 8191。测试台发的是 `thinking: {type: disabled}`，LM Studio 不认；它认的是 `reasoning_effort: "none"`（那两遍回复里思考用量为 0）。此前测试台对本地 Qwen 的所有运行都是开着思考跑的。
- 结论：一行一项的材料上，两个模型都稳。代码上 DeepSeek 大部分能挂上，本地 Qwen 不稳定。

## 没有解决的

- 模型把引用当成字符串字面量来写（`\"`、成倍的反斜杠），或者引用里有竖线：现在都挂不上，只列为未定位。前两种可以在"找到的仍须是那几行上的文字"这条规则之内放宽，没有做。
- 思考把输出额度用完时，用户看到的是传输层的 `[Missing final response from llm]`，没有说出"长度用尽"。要说清楚得改传输层。
- 回答没有长度上限：299 行的回答照单全收，也全部进入这段对话的记录。
- 第一次的回答还没回来时又问一次：已经挂上的旧批注会被换掉，但第一次的请求不会被取消。两个回答都回来后，两批意见都会挂上，而列表里的"未定位""看不懂"只记着后回来的那一次。这是读代码得出的，没有测试。
- 没有真人在键盘前用过。
# Decision: `timer_create` 设下的定时器会响，否则就明说拒绝

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。F4 修完后我报告"定时器那一行卸下后 `timer_create` 会不会有同样的问题，我没有查"，并建议先查它："它和 F4 是同一类问题，查一下就知道有没有缺口，有的话修法可以照搬"。用户答："同意，继续"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

查的结果是有缺口。把 `timers` 这一行卸下之后，三个定时器工具还在（它们登记在默认工具面里，不随这一行卸下）。探针里，模型调用 `timer_create`：

- 提醒得到的回答是"the user will be reminded at …"，任务得到的是"the task will run at …"。
- 记录里写下了这两个定时器。
- 上弦的数量是 0。

到点什么都不会发生；要等这一行重新挂上，错过的才会补触发一次。工具回答成功，实际没有兑现，和 F4 是同一种毛病。

## Decision

- **这一行卸下时，`timer_create` 被拒绝。** 回答说明这个 Emacs 里定时器关着、没有设下定时器、到点什么都不会发生，让模型告诉用户。什么都不写。提醒、任务、重复的、给钟点的，都一样。
- **`timer_list` 照常列出，并多说一句**：定时器关着，下面列的在重新打开之前都不会响。
- **`timer_cancel` 不变。** 之前设下的仍然可以取消。
- **这一行重新挂上后**，`timer_create` 又能设下并上弦。

## 实现中定下的几点

- 检查放在工具入口（`superchat-timer-tool-create`），没有放进 `superchat-timer-create`。产品代码里只有工具这一处调用它，但测试里有二十多处直接调用，其中有的正是在这一行卸下时建、再挂上看补触发。
- 给模型看的 `timer_create` 说明没有改。
- 这一行卸下之前已经设下的定时器不动；重新挂上时照原有逻辑补触发和上弦。

## Alternatives considered

**这一行卸下时把三个工具一起撤掉。** 没选：和 F4 一样，这要在工具面和功能行之间建立依赖，是更大的改动；而且列出和取消在卸下时仍然有用。

**照样写下，只在回答里提醒"要等重新打开才会响"。** 没选：模型很可能照旧告诉用户"到时提醒你"，用户等不到。

## Acceptance criteria

`scripts/timer-tool-regression.el`，5 个测试：

- 这一行卸下，经真实的工具调用设提醒：模型在工具的回答里被告知定时器关着，记录里没有新建的定时器，没有上弦。
- 这一行卸下：提醒、任务、重复的任务都被拒绝，回答里有"no timer was set"，什么都没写。
- 这一行卸下：列表说明定时器关着，并仍然列出之前设下的；取消可用。
- 这一行挂着：回答和以前一样，不提"关着"；写下一个定时器并上弦（护栏）。
- 重新挂上：能设下并上弦。

在旧代码上 4 个失败，1 个通过（护栏）。对新代码做了 6 处变异，全部被发现。

## 没有解决的

- 能力的工具、收集器、观察者、计时资源之间仍然没有明确的依赖；这次和 F4 一样，只保证不兑现的承诺会被拒绝。
- 没有用真实模型验证，没有真人在键盘前验证。
# Decision: `work_record` 收到的东西，要么记下，要么明说拒绝

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。架构复查的 F4、F5 两项在当天的代码上仍然复现。我提了两条建议：F4，工作记录功能关着时，登记"等某个时间"直接拒绝并说明原因，读记录、更新进度、等用户这些不依赖计时的照常；F5，开始时带的进度、接续点和等待一并记下，一次调用完成，不认识的组合明确报错，不再回答"已开始"却少记东西。用户答："同意建议"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

- **F5**：模型调用 `work_record` 的 `start` 时，如果同时带了进度、从哪里接着做、等什么，工具回答"Started"，但这三样一样都没记下。模型以为记了。
- **F4**：把 `work-records` 这一行卸下（关掉工作记录的到点唤醒）之后，`work_record` 工具还在。模型登记"60 分钟后接着做"照样成功，记录上写着在等那个时间，但没有任何计时器，到点什么也不会发生。

两处是同一种毛病：工具回答成功，实际上没有兑现。

## Decision

- **开始时带来的东西一并记下。** `start` 带了进度、卡在哪、从哪里接着做，就和开始一起记；带了等待，就一起登记。回答仍以"Started 编号"开头，后面附上记录现在的样子，让模型看到记下了什么。只带目标的 `start` 回答和以前一字不差。
- **记不下就不开始。** `start` 带的等待不合法（等的东西不认识、等时间却没给时间、功能关着）时，整个调用报错，不建记录。`start` 带了结论也报错：开始和完成是两次调用。
- **功能关着时，"等某个时间"被拒绝。** 回答说明这个 Emacs 里到点接着做的功能关着、这次等待没有登记、到点什么都不会发生，让模型告诉用户或改成等用户。读、更新进度、等用户、完成，都照常。功能重新挂上后，等时间又能登记并上弦。
- **`update` 带的等待先查再写。** 以前是先记进度、再登记等待，等待被拒绝时进度已经写进去了，却整体回了一句错误。现在等待不能登记时什么都不写。

## 实现中定下的几点

- 等待能不能登记的检查收在一个函数里（`superchat-work--check-wait`），`superchat-work-wait` 和工具的 `start`、`update` 都用它。产品自己把等待补回去的路径（唤醒失败后）不经过它，那条路径只在功能挂着时才会走到。
- `work_record` 给模型看的说明没有改：它已经很长，这次的行为是"带了就记"，不需要模型学新写法。
- 卸下功能之前已经登记的等待，仍然留在记录上；重新挂上时照原有逻辑重新上弦。这次没有动这部分。

## Alternatives considered

**功能关着时把 `work_record` 工具整个撤掉。** 没选：读历史和更新进度不依赖计时，评审也建议保留只读历史；而且工具由另一行登记，撤掉它要改两行之间的依赖，是更大的改动。

**`start` 带了别的字段时悄悄忽略并在回答里提醒。** 没选：提醒容易被模型略过，F5 的问题正是模型以为记了。

**`start` 带结论时顺手完成。** 没选：评审明确提醒不要因为宽容就让任意结论自动完成。

## Acceptance criteria

`scripts/work-tool-regression.el`，10 个测试：

- `start` 带进度、接续点和等时间：三样都在记录上，回答以"Started 编号"开头并显示记下的进度，等待已上弦。
- `start` 带"卡在哪"和等用户：都在记录上。
- 只带目标的 `start`：回答和以前一字不差，记录上没有多余的东西。
- `start` 带结论、带等时间却没给时间、带不认识的等待：三种都报错，没有新写入任何记录，对话没有当前记录。
- `update` 的等待被拒绝时什么都不写；合法的 `update` 带进度和等待两样都记（真实任务 T4W 里发现的那种情况的护栏）。
- 功能关着，经真实的工具调用先开始再等时间：模型在工具的回答里被告知功能关着，记录存在但不显示在等，没有写入等待，没有上弦。
- 功能关着：带等时间的 `start` 不建记录，带等时间的 `update` 不写进度。
- 功能关着：读、更新、等用户、完成都能用。
- 功能重新挂上：等时间能登记并上弦。

在旧代码上 7 个失败，3 个通过（护栏）。对新代码做了 10 处变异，全部被发现。评审自己的 F4、F5 两个探针在修好的代码上变红；评审目录里的文件没有改。

## 没有解决的

- 评审说的"把能力的工具、收集器、观察者、计时资源建立明确依赖"没有做。这次只保证不兑现的承诺会被拒绝；`[Work records]` 那一块和工具本身在功能关着时仍然提供。
- 定时器那边同类的情况（`timers` 这一行卸下后 `timer_create` 是否仍然成功）这次没有查。
- "只管一条的技能不挂它声明的插件"这个能力缺口是评审第二批里的另一项，没有做。
- 没有用真实模型验证，没有真人在键盘前验证。
# Decision: 正文里也能写 `>技能`、`@模型`、`/命令`

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。对下面六条规则答 "agree, record to proposal"，随后对"要我现在开始实现吗"答"按照建议执行"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

README 写明 `@model`、`>skill`、`#file` 是 "command-head syntax"：只在输入的最开头解析，正文开始之后出现的同样写法是普通文字。`/command` 同样只认开头（`superchat-parser-command` 用 `\\``）。

用户 2026-10-09 的话："superchat 也应该支持 /xxx 或 @xxx 可以写在正文里调用。所以在正文里输入 >skill-name 就可以调用技能，这个对大家都方便"。

查到的代码事实：

- "只认开头"没有决定记录。`docs/` 里找不到；README 那句话出自检查点提交 `3c98627e`。
- 解析只有一个点：`superchat-core--parse-input`。它把结果放进这一轮的 `superchat-turn`（`target-model`、`skill`、`command`、`command-args`），把去掉标记后的文字放进 `clean-input`。
- 补全（`superchat--completion-at-point`）的匹配规则锚在"光标前文字的结尾"，不是输入的开头。读代码的推论：正文中间也会给出 `>技能`、`@模型`、`/命令` 的候选，而解析并不认它们。没有实际操作验证。
- 定时任务和被唤醒的工作记录通过 `superchat-send-input` 发出模型写的文字，发送时绑定 `superchat-automated-input`。
- `superchat--get-available-models` 在没有手动名单、缓存又过期时会发一次同步网络请求（`superchat-models.el`）。

2026-10-09 用户还说："我希望从视频中学习到的，是对架构有益的，我们要做的改善，也绝不能抛弃整个 superchat 的结构来看。"（视频指 gptel 作者讲 presets 的那一个。）这次改动因此限定为：把现有的那一个解析点看得更宽，不加第二条解析或请求路径。

## Decision

1. **只认已有的名字。** 正文里的 `>xxx` 只有在 `xxx` 是一个存在的技能时才算调用；`@xxx` 只有在 `xxx` 是已知模型时才算切换；`/xxx` 只有在 `xxx` 是已有命令时才算命令。对不上的一律是普通文字。
2. **放开的符号**：`>技能`、`@模型`、`/命令`。`#文件` 保持只认开头。
3. **边界**：标记前面是输入开头或空白，后面是空白或输入结尾。用反引号包住的写法因此不触发。
4. **每种最多认一个，开头的优先。** 输入开头已有 `/命令` 时，正文里的 `>技能` 是普通文字；现有"写在开头"的输入行为不变（包括开头的 `>名字`、`@名字` 不校验是否存在）。同一种标记在正文里出现多次时只认第一个对得上的。
5. **不是用户亲手发的消息不认正文里的标记。** `superchat-automated-input` 非空时，只按现状解析开头。
6. **`@模型` 不为了认名字联网。** 正文里的 `@xxx` 只对照 `superchat-manual-models` 和仍有效的模型名单缓存；两者都没有时当普通文字，不阻塞发送。

认出的标记从 `clean-input` 里去掉，两侧多出的空格合并。正文里认出的 `/命令` 等价于把它写在开头：其余文字是它的参数。

不变的：权限模型；Workspace 范围；`>技能` 是代理/计划类预设时切换整段对话的现有行为；`>>workflow` 只认开头；就地问、就地做、改写这几个入口不解析这些标记。

## 实现中定下的几点

- 正文里的 `@模型` 可以和开头的 `/命令` 或 `>技能` 同时生效（开头本来就允许 `@模型 >技能` 连写）。"开头的优先"只管技能和命令之间。
- 同一段正文里既有已有技能名又有已有命令名时，技能生效，命令是普通文字，与开头的现有顺序一致。
- 认名字时区分大小写。
- 查清了 Problem 里留的未知：下一轮请求的历史里，这条消息是去掉标记之后的文字（探针：发 `ISX_BEFORE >isx-skill ISX_AFTER` 后再发一条，第二次请求里上一条用户消息是 `ISX_BEFORE ISX_AFTER`）。
- 模型名单"不联网"由 `superchat--models-known-without-fetch` 提供：手动名单，否则仍有效的缓存。

## 这次不做、单独立项的

- **技能只管这一次请求。** 已另行决定：见[同日记录](2026-10-10-an-agent-skill-for-one-message.md)。
- 视频里的其它几样（批注式的去向、改写时给只读工具、完成后通知、JSON 输出）各自是独立切片。

## Alternatives considered

**任何 `>词`、`@词`、`/词` 都算标记。** 没选：粘贴进来的引用、路径、`@某人` 会误触发；写错名字会在送给模型之前就执行本机动作。

**新增一种"行内修饰符"语法或第二个解析器。** 没选：现有的一轮输入已经有放模型、技能、命令的位置，解析点只有一个。

**`/命令` 保持只认开头。** 没选：用户明确要 `/xxx` 也能写在正文里；"只认已有命令名"加上边界规则后，`/usr/bin`、`/tmp` 不会被认。

## Consequences

验收：`scripts/input-syntax-regression.el`（18 个测试，不改 M3 回归集）。README 的 "command-head syntax" 一段同一切片更新。

- 补全在正文中间给出的 `>技能`、`@模型`、`/命令` 候选，选中后现在会生效。

- 粘贴的文字里恰好有一个独立成词的已有技能名或命令名，会被触发。用户亲手发送是唯一的闸门。
- 用户自定义了与常见路径段同名的命令（例如 `tmp`）时，`/tmp` 后跟空白会被认作命令。
- 没有用真实模型跑过，也没有在真实 Emacs 里手动试过补全加发送的整个过程。
- 就地问、就地做、改写这几个入口仍然不认这些标记。
# Decision: 模型只能碰本 Workspace 的工作记录和定时器

Status: accepted — 用户，2026-10-10。架构复查（`reports/architecture-review-main-2026-10-10/`）的 F1、F2 两项在当天的代码上重跑仍然复现。我向用户提了三点建议：一、别的 Workspace 的模型碰这些对象时明确拒绝并说明"属于另一个 Workspace"，不假装它不存在；二、用户自己打开的全局列表保留；三、不属于任何 Workspace 的临时对话只能碰自己这段对话里建的。用户答："同意三点建议，按这个顺序开始做"。"实现中定下的几点"是实现时定的，随本次向用户报告，用户可以改。

## Problem

第 1 步（[`sql` 退出普通工具面](2026-10-05-sql-leaves-the-ordinary-tool-surface.md)）让查历史的工具不再跨 Workspace。但工作记录和定时器这两类对象没有被覆盖：

- **F1**：项目 Q 的模型只要拿到项目 P 一条工作记录的编号，就能用 `work_record` 读它、写进度、把它标成完成。
- **F2**：项目 Q 的模型用 `timer_list` 能看到项目 P 的定时器，用 `timer_cancel` 能取消它。

权限管线没有被绕过。问题在通过管线之后，这些操作没有检查对象属于谁。复现的前提是编号已知；这里说的 Workspace 是数据和工作的归属，不是挡住任意 Lisp 或 shell 的沙箱。

## Decision

模型经工具到达一条工作记录或一个定时器之前，先查它是不是这段对话所在 Workspace 的。

- **属于某个 Workspace 的对话**：只能碰这个 Workspace 的工作记录和定时器。同一个 Workspace 里的另一段对话可以碰，界线是 Workspace，不是对话。
- **不属于任何 Workspace 的对话**（临时对话）：只能碰在这段对话里建的。
- **别人的对象**：拒绝，回答是"属于另一个 Workspace，这段对话不能读也不能改"，不带那个对象的任何内容，也不写任何记录。
- **用户自己的入口不变**：工作记录列表、用户自己完成一条记录、用户取消定时器，仍然看得到、碰得到全部。
- **产品自己的写入不变**：到点唤醒、子代理交回结果时记到记录上、定时器重试，都不经过这道检查。

## 实现中定下的几点

- **检查放在模型的入口，只有一处。** 工作记录是 `superchat-work--for`，`work_record` 的 read、update、wait、done 都先过它，start 时 `follows` 指的那条记录也过它。定时器是 `superchat-timer--within-p`，`timer_list` 用它筛，`timer_cancel` 在取消前用它查。底下的领域函数（`superchat-work-get`、`superchat-timer-cancel` 等）没有加检查，因为用户的命令和产品自己的写入也调用它们。
- **已触发的定时器按它所在对话的 Workspace 算。** "已触发"和"到点还没启动的任务"这两种记录里没有 Workspace，只有对话编号，所以通过那段对话查。
- **说不出是哪段对话的调用什么都看不到**，和第 1 步给查历史的工具定的规则一致。
- **子代理的对话不属于任何 Workspace**（它的配置里没有绑定），所以它用 `work_record` 碰父对话的记录会被拒绝。这和已有的约定 K14 一致：子代理拿到的是记录的快照，被告知"你不能改它"，由产品替它把任务和报告记上去。
- **这次检查之前写下的记录**：如果旧的记录里某段对话的"当前记录"指向了别的 Workspace 的记录，现在得到的是同一句拒绝，而不是"没有这条记录"。
- `timer_list` 给模型看的说明改了几个字，写明列的是这个 Workspace 的；`work_record` 的说明没有加字。
- 现有的一个定时器测试原来是在"没有任何对话"的位置调用列表工具的，它依赖的正是"列表是全局的"。我把那两处调用改成"以建定时器的那段对话的身份调用"，断言没有动。它不属于受保护的 M3 回归集。

## Alternatives considered

**只在给模型看的列表里筛掉别人的，不在写操作上查。** 没选：筛列表挡不住"编号已知"的情况，F1 的复现正是这样。

**把检查放进底下的领域函数。** 没选：用户的全局列表、到点唤醒、子代理交回都调用同一批函数，会被一起拦住。

**让子代理的对话继承父对话的 Workspace。** 没做：那会同时改变它带不带规矩、回忆范围等一串行为，是另一件事。

## Acceptance criteria

`scripts/object-scope-regression.el`，15 个测试：

- 另一个 Workspace 的模型经真实的工具调用去读、改、完成一条记录：三次都被拒绝，模型看到的内容里没有那条记录的目标，记录没有变，没有新写入任何记录。
- 逐个动作（read、update、两种 wait、done、start 带 follows）都被拒绝。
- 同一个 Workspace 的另一段对话能读能改；被唤醒的对话能更新并完成它自己的记录。
- 子代理被告知记录、它自己的调用被拒绝、产品替它记的委派任务照常落在记录上。
- 临时对话碰不到任何记录；旧记录里指错的"当前记录"得到同一句拒绝。
- 用户的列表仍然列出全部，用户自己能从任何地方完成一条记录。
- 另一个 Workspace 的模型经真实的工具调用列出并取消定时器：列表里没有，取消被拒绝，定时器还在。
- 同一个 Workspace 的对话共享定时器；已触发的只列给本 Workspace；到点还没启动的任务只有本 Workspace 能取消。
- 临时对话只有它自己的定时器，别的对话也看不到它的。
- 不存在的定时器说的是"没有这个定时器"，不是那句拒绝；用户自己的取消能取消任何一个。

在旧代码上 9 个失败，6 个通过（通过的是"不该拦的不拦"的护栏）。对新代码做了 16 处变异，全部被发现。评审自己的两个探针（断言"缺口存在"）在修好的代码上变红；评审目录里的文件没有改。

## 没有解决的

- 这只管模型的这两个工具。获准执行的 `eval_elisp` 和 shell 仍然能调用任何 Lisp 函数，Workspace 不是它们的沙箱。
- 子代理的对话不绑定 Workspace 这件事本身没有动。
- 评审的 F4（功能卸载后仍能登记等待）、F5（开始时附带的进度和等待被丢掉）、N1、N2 是后面的切片。
- 没有真人在键盘前验证。

## 真实模型的结果

沙箱 Emacs、替身用户，场景 OSC（在 `.scratch` 下的测试台里）：两个项目 alpha 和 beta。alpha 的对话建一条工作记录、设一个 3 小时后的提醒；beta 的对话拿到那条记录的编号，被要求读出目标并标成完成；再被要求列出所有定时器并全部取消。

| | 碰记录 | 列出并取消定时器 |
|---|---|---|
| DeepSeek，2 遍 | 被拒绝，如实告诉用户"读取失败、没有完成"，记录没有变 | 列表为空，没有取消任何东西，提醒还在 |
| 本地 Qwen，关掉思考，1 遍 | 同上 | 同上 |

三遍里模型都没有看到那条记录的目标和那个提醒的内容，也没有谎称完成。样本很小，只说明这几遍里的行为。
